47	2608.06227	From Passive Mirrors to Active Agents: Holonic Digital Twins for Physical AI over Networks	Despite advances in artificial intelligence (AI) across multiple sectors, today's AI tools, including deep learning and generative AI, still fail when embedded into physical systems, such as robots and vehicles operating under real-world physical laws. This stems from their inability to maintain reliable world models for long-horizon planning under uncertainty and generalize to unseen scenarios. In this context, wireless networks, through pervasive sensing and communication, can orchestrate physical intelligence. However, current architectures optimize throughput, latency, and reliability and cannot support real-time physical AI coordination, requiring agents to maintain shared spatiotemporal context. To address these challenges, a network of holonic digital twins (HDT-Nets) framework is proposed to deliver real-time physical AI inference through holonic agents that actively reason about their environment rather than passively mirror physical assets. Each HDT is realized as a hierarchical structure spanning the physical agent and network edge, reasoning autonomously at the local level while cooperating with neighboring HDTs to form collectively intelligent units. In HDT-Net, causal Markov blankets spanning sensing, communication, and control determine which agents must coordinate and enable counterfactual reasoning over multi-domain interventions. Active inference within these boundaries unifies perception, action, and learning by minimizing expected free energy while deciding which beliefs to transmit based on their cognitive value to the receiver. Category theory ensures that transmitted beliefs preserve semantic structure across heterogeneous agents with incompatible representations. Finally, integrated information theory quantifies when collective intelligence exceeds independent operation and how network intelligence evolves through coordinated learning and information exchange.	2026-08-06 16:17:43+00	2026-08-06 16:17:43+00	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06227v1	{cs.NI,cs.AI,cs.IT,eess.SY}	https://openalex.org/W7201839308	f85e081db4af77dcb701983953576d109d28f3ba	0	5	3.5	EMBEDDED	{"arxiv_id_versioned": "2608.06227v1", "authors_raw": ["Christo Kurisummoottil Thomas", "Omar Hashash", "Walid Saad"], "primary_category": "cs.NI", "comment": null, "doi": null, "openalex": {"openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/W7201839308", "cited_by_count": 0, "referenced_works_count": 0, "publication_year": 2026, "type": "preprint", "authorships": [{"name": "Christo Kurisummoottil Thomas", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146026335", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Omar Hashash", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5072240167", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Walid Saad", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5145949839", "affiliation": null}], "concepts": [{"name": "Computer science", "score": 0.5975000262260437}, {"name": "Artificial intelligence", "score": 0.5516999959945679}, {"name": "Inference", "score": 0.517300009727478}, {"name": "Reliability (semiconductor)", "score": 0.4131999909877777}, {"name": "Markov decision process", "score": 0.39800000190734863}]}, "s2": {"semantic_scholar_id": "f85e081db4af77dcb701983953576d109d28f3ba", "tldr": "A network of holonic digital twins (HDT-Nets) framework is proposed to deliver real-time physical AI inference through holonic agents that actively reason about their environment rather than passively mirror physical assets.", "influential_citation_count": 0, "citation_count": 0, "open_access_pdf": ""}, "hn": {"mentions": 5, "total_points": 117, "top_story_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41347388", "top_story_points": 112}}	2026-08-07 13:50:22.419217+00	2026-08-18 00:51:30.326648+00
50	2608.06218	Muon on the Stiefel Manifold Admits an Exact Closed-Form Update	We study Muon, a recently proposed matrix-aware optimization method, in the context of the Stiefel manifold. This manifold consists of matrices with orthonormal columns and is ubiquitous in machine learning and scientific computing. Existing extensions of Muon to this manifold rely on heuristic, approximate, or iterative updates with varying computational efficiency. We show that the corresponding Stiefel Muon update admits an exact closed-form solution and use this result to develop Skewon, a practical algorithm for orthogonality-constrained optimization with an efficient implementation. We further establish first-order convergence guarantees for Skewon in the smooth non-convex setting.	2026-08-06 16:09:56+00	2026-08-06 16:09:56+00	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06218v1	{math.OC,cs.LG,math.NA}	https://openalex.org/W7201864640	\N	0	7	3	EMBEDDED	{"arxiv_id_versioned": "2608.06218v1", "authors_raw": ["Mikhail Solonko", "Molozhavenko Alexander", "Maxim Rakhuba"], "primary_category": "math.OC", "comment": null, "doi": null, "openalex": {"openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/W7201864640", "cited_by_count": 0, "referenced_works_count": 0, "publication_year": 2026, "type": "preprint", "authorships": [{"name": "Mikhail Solonko", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5145939190", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Molozhavenko Alexander", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146023744", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Maxim Rakhuba", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5139346739", "affiliation": null}], "concepts": [{"name": "Stiefel manifold", "score": 0.9157999753952026}, {"name": "Orthonormal basis", "score": 0.6858999729156494}, {"name": "Context (archaeology)", "score": 0.6413999795913696}, {"name": "Convergence (economics)", "score": 0.5367000102996826}, {"name": "Manifold (fluid mechanics)", "score": 0.5206999778747559}]}, "hn": {"mentions": 7, "total_points": 261, "top_story_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=24196052", "top_story_points": 149}}	2026-08-07 13:50:22.419217+00	2026-08-29 16:24:06.283174+00
51	2608.06216	Continual Learning in Transition	Classical continual learning (CL) has primarily focused on enabling models to update and retain knowledge through parameter-centric mechanisms, e.g., training strategies, architectural designs, and weight adaptation. However, emerging paradigms are reshaping the scope of CL beyond this traditional model adaptation view. For instance, on-policy learning broadens the space of update mechanisms; test-time training extends CL from the training phase to inference; and external harness components such as memory, skill libraries, and interaction protocols extend the evolutionary boundaries of model capabilities far beyond the static parameter space. Collectively, these developments indicate a transition from parameter-centric learning toward system-level adaptation. To characterize this transition, we examine the evolution of continual learning through three dimensions: When, How, and Where learning occurs. The How dimension encompasses off-policy, on-policy, and beyond-gradient optimization mechanics. The When dimension captures evolution across pre-training, post-training, and inference-time stages. The Where dimension delineates updates occurring within internal parameters versus external structural constraints. Anchored by this tri-axial framework, we systematically survey representative methods, trace the ongoing transition of continual learning, and discuss the key challenges, broader implications, and future directions arising from this paradigm shift.	2026-08-06 16:07:26+00	2026-08-06 16:07:26+00	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06216v1	{cs.LG,cs.AI}	https://openalex.org/W7201871506	\N	0	5	1.764	EMBEDDED	{"arxiv_id_versioned": "2608.06216v1", "authors_raw": ["Zhiyan Hou", "Dan Zhang", "Tao Feng", "Liyuan Wang", "Wei Li", "Xiangzhao Hao", "Hongyan An", "Junfeng Fang", "Haokai Ma", "Zhaohui Xu", "Haiyun Guo", "Jinqiao Wang", "Tat-Seng Chua"], "primary_category": "cs.LG", "comment": "23 pages, 6 figures, 1 table. Survey on continual learning in the LLM and agentic-AI era", "doi": null, "openalex": {"openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/W7201871506", "cited_by_count": 0, "referenced_works_count": 0, "publication_year": 2026, "type": "preprint", "authorships": [{"name": "Zhiyan Hou", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5123477302", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Dan Zhang", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146067245", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Tao Feng", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146035194", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Liyuan Wang", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146014136", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Wei Li", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5145967939", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Xiangzhao Hao", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5124900824", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Hongyan An", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5012249897", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Junfeng Fang", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146038642", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Haokai Ma", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5006833163", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Zhaohui Xu", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5123855924", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Xinyu Tang", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5147125444", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Haiyun Guo", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5147091964", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Jinqiao Wang", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5147063302", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Tat-Seng Chua", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5147081634", "affiliation": null}], "concepts": [{"name": "Dimension (graph theory)", "score": 0.7200999855995178}, {"name": "Scope (computer science)", "score": 0.5622000098228455}, {"name": "Adaptation (eye)", "score": 0.5267999768257141}, {"name": "Computer science", "score": 0.5238000154495239}, {"name": "Key (lock)", "score": 0.4999000132083893}]}, "hn": {"mentions": 5, "total_points": 14, "top_story_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id