22	2608.06301	HarnessOpt-Bench: Evaluating LLMs at Harness Optimization	As LLMs are increasingly deployed within agentic systems, their capabilities depend not only on the model weights but also on the harness: the prompts, tools, control flow, memory, and orchestration code surrounding them. This makes automated harness optimization -- the iterative and evaluation-guided improvement of a harness by an AI system -- both an important route to improving AI systems and a demanding capability for AI systems themselves. Yet the community lacks a common protocol for measuring how well frontier LLMs perform at this task. We introduce HarnessOpt-Bench, a benchmark for end-to-end harness optimization under expensive and stochastic evaluation. An optimizer, an LLM paired with a coding harness, receives a target agent's seed harness, graded evaluation feedback, and a fixed target-evaluation budget. It edits the harness and nominates a final candidate, which is scored by its normalized gain over the seed on a held-out test partition that remains inaccessible throughout search. A trusted execution environment enforces the evaluation boundary, meters target-agent resource use, and preserves candidate versions for audit. We evaluate 5 frontier LLMs as optimizers both under a shared coding harness and under their native harnesses across 4 downstream tasks, over 111 scored runs. Experiment results show that optimizer models separate more than the coding harnesses they act through, native harnesses are not consistently superior, and gains vary substantially across tasks and seed regimes. These results establish harness optimization as a measurable and discriminative capability with large space for improvement.	2026-08-06 17:21:05+00	2026-08-06 17:21:05+00	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06301v1	{cs.AI,cs.CL,cs.LG}	https://openalex.org/W7201862772	\N	0	3	3	EMBEDDED	{"arxiv_id_versioned": "2608.06301v1", "authors_raw": ["Varun Ursekar", "Apaar Shanker", "Yash Maurya", "Shehab Yasser", "Vijay S. Kalmath", "Veronica Chatrath", "Yuan Xue"], "primary_category": "cs.AI", "comment": null, "doi": null, "openalex": {"openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/W7201862772", "cited_by_count": 0, "referenced_works_count": 0, "publication_year": 2026, "type": "preprint", "authorships": [{"name": "Varun Ursekar", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5027798802", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Apaar Shanker", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5114425389", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Yash Maurya", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5145955049", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Shehab Yasser", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5145994727", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Vijay S. Kalmath", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5136684417", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Veronica Chatrath", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146018076", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Yuan Xue", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146006208", "affiliation": null}], "concepts": [{"name": "Computer science", "score": 0.5961999893188477}, {"name": "Coding (social sciences)", "score": 0.5188000202178955}, {"name": "Benchmark (surveying)", "score": 0.4399000108242035}, {"name": "Discriminative model", "score": 0.43849998712539673}, {"name": "Partition (number theory)", "score": 0.3314000070095062}]}, "hn": {"mentions": 3, "total_points": 118, "top_story_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=28273115", "top_story_points": 113}}	2026-08-07 13:50:22.419217+00	2026-08-29 16:24:01.394155+00
15	2608.06331	Tytan: Interactive Neurosymbolic Construction of Analytic Semantic Schemas from Relational Data	From natural-language query interfaces to automated report generation, data analysis tools need a description of the data: the real-world entities it contains, which columns function as measures or identifiers, and how tables connect into units of analysis. Today, this semantic layer is usually written by hand. This is a knowledge-acquisition bottleneck that limits the scalability of analytic systems, keeps non-technical users dependent on experts, and is itself error-prone. We present TYTAN, a system for automatically constructing an analytic semantic schema from a relational database and, when available, a short user-provided description. TYTAN combines symbolic analysis of the database with LLM-based semantic inference for entity proposal, role assignment, and naming. When the evidence leaves a decision ambiguous, TYTAN asks the user a targeted natural-language question. We evaluate TYTAN on eight databases spanning real-world and benchmark domains along the three axes that define a schema's functional utility: (i) coverage, are all important entities and features captured?; (ii) retrieval correctness, do the schema's instructions actually reach the data; and (iii) characterization accuracy, are semantic types correct? Across the seven reference domains, TYTAN reaches every entity, attribute, and aggregable feature of the expert-corrected reference schemas (100% coverage). Additionally, 100% of its retrieval instructions execute correctly (1,678 of 1,678 self-generated claims), and semantic roles agree with the reference on 92-100% of matched attributes. Checking the underlying data showed the small disagreement is in the reference, not in TYTAN. On a held-out blind test (a live, ten-table database with no declared keys), TYTAN recovers the full entity structure with verified keys and satisfies 100% of the satisfiable expectations of five independent blind annotators.	2026-08-06 17:40:26+00	2026-08-06 17:40:26+00	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06331v1	{cs.DB,cs.AI}	https://openalex.org/W7201887839	\N	0	5	2.194	EMBEDDED	{"arxiv_id_versioned": "2608.06331v1", "authors_raw": ["Donna Hooshmand", "Shubham Shahi", "Cameron Barrie", "Abhratanu Dutta", "Marko Sterbentz", "Harper Pack", "Kristian J. Hammond"], "primary_category": "cs.DB", "comment": "20 pages, 4 figures, 6 tables", "doi": null, "openalex": {"openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/W7201887839", "cited_by_count": 0, "referenced_works_count": 0, "publication_year": 2026, "type": "preprint", "authorships": [{"name": "Donna Hooshmand", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5093329982", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Shubham Shahi", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5111079604", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Cameron Barrie", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146013776", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Abhratanu Dutta", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146031152", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Marko Sterbentz", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5087714040", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Harper Pack", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5072066579", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Kristian J. Hammond", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5114000097", "affiliation": null}], "concepts": [{"name": "Computer science", "score": 0.8278999924659729}, {"name": "Information retrieval", "score": 0.5867000222206116}, {"name": "Relational database", "score": 0.5374000072479248}, {"name": "Inference", "score": 0.4724000096321106}, {"name": "Semantic equivalence", "score": 0.4510999917984009}]}, "hn": {"mentions": 5, "total_points": 28, "top_story_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46648981", "top_story_points": 17}}	2026-08-07 13:50:22.419217+00	2026-08-29 16:24:19.26443+00
10	2608.06351	Challenges in Evaluating Explanation Methods for Static and Evolving Data	This paper addresses the limitations of Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) with respect to insufficient evaluation. They are illustrated through the DetoxAI image recognition system for bias detection and concept unlearning. Then, an example of a human-grounded evaluation of methods for explaining image classification is presented. The paper further explores methods for adapting explanations to evolving data streams with concept drift. Experiences with adapting counterfactuals for this problem are discussed. Finally it is related to the challenges of tracking the co-evolution of data, models, and explanations.\\footnote{This paper has been accepted for a publication in J.Nalepa (ed) Explainable AI in Space. Proceedings of EASi 2026 Workshop at IJCAI-ECAI 2026 Bremen, Springer CCIS vol 3107 (2016).}	2026-08-06 17:53:10+00	2026-08-06 17:53:10+00	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06351v1	{cs.AI}	https://openalex.org/W7201865830	\N	0	4	2.07	EMBEDDED	{"arxiv_id_versioned": "2608.06351v1", "authors_raw": ["Jerzy Stefanowski"], "primary_category": "cs.AI", "comment": "13 pages, 1 figure = this paper is a preprint of the workshop [Explainable AI in Space] paper for IJCAI ECAI 2026 conference", "doi": null, "openalex": {"openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/W7201865830", "cited_by_count": 0, "referenced_works_count": 0, "publication_year": 2026, "type": "preprint", "authorships": [{"name": "Jerzy Stefanowski", "openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/A5146046176", "affiliation": null}], "concepts": [{"name": "Counterfactual conditional", "score": 0.7348999977111816}, {"name": "Computer science", "score": 0.6477000117301941}, {"name": "Artificial intelligence", "score": 0.5444999933242798}, {"name": "Tracking (education)", "score": 0.3668000102043152}, {"name": "Data science", "score": 0.33390000462532043}]}, "hn": {"mentions": 4, "total_points": 23, "top_story_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48453051", "top_story_points": 17}}	2026-08-07 13:50:22.419217+00	2026-08-29 16:24:20.774649+00
19	2608.06310	RRC: Unlocking Generative Reward Models in LLM Reinforcement Learning via Ranking-Based Reward Construction	Recent advances in reward modeling show a paradigm shift from discriminative reward models to generative reward models. However, despite their strong capabilities in response ranking, generative reward models have not realized their potential in reinforcement learning (RL). Our analysis reveals that this limitation arises from a mismatch between the comparative nature of generative reward modeling and the scalar scoring paradigm adopted by existing RL algorithms. To bridge this gap, we propose a Ranking-based Reward Construction (RRC) approach, which enables generative reward models to provide more effective RL learning signals by deriving rewards from relative preference rankings. RRC introduces two complementary strategies: self-competitive ranking, which exploits comparisons among sampled responses, and anchor-guided ranking, which enables scalable ranking-based reward construction with a small set of reference responses. Experiments across open-ended chat and reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that RRC substantially improves RL training with generative reward models, achieving consistent gains over existing reward construction approaches. Our code can be found at https://github.com/wangclnlp/RRC.	2026-08-06 17:24:36+00	2026-08-06 17:24:36+00	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06310v1	{cs.LG,cs.CL}	https://openalex.org/W7201857470	\N	0	5	3	EMBEDDED	{"arxiv_id_versioned": "2608.06310v1", "authors_raw": ["Chenglong Wang", "Ziming Zhu", "Yifu Huo", "Bei Li", "Qiaozhi He", "Yan Ding", "Xiaoyang Hao", "Yuxin Gao", "Tianhua Zhou", "Xiaojia Chang", "Tongran Liu", "Jingbo Zhu"], "primary_category": "cs.LG", "comment": null, "doi": null, "openalex": {"openalex_id": "https://openalex.org/W7201857470", "cited_by_count": 0, "referenced_works_count": 0, "publication_year": 2026, "type": "preprint", "authorships": [{"name": "Chenglong Wang", "openalex_id": "https://o