1	49	analogy	READY	{"analogies": [{"concept": "Encoding force and normalized time alongside spatial position in trajectory learning", "domain": "jazz improvisation", "setup": "A jazz musician listens to another improviser play a solo and transcribes it. A student could write down just the note names and rhythms. But what made the solo compelling was also the subtlety: when the player dug in hard on certain notes, when they backed off, when they rushed or laid back behind the beat. A notation capturing only pitch and duration is technically correct but emotionally hollow. A notation adding attack intensity and timing nuance gives the next player enough information to inhabit the same feeling.", "mapping": ["the original improviser's solo \\u2192 the human demonstrations of handwriting", "transcribing only notes and rhythm \\u2192 learning trajectories from spatial position (x, y) alone", "the dynamics and timing subtlety \\u2192 force profile and normalized time dimension", "the second musician's ability to recapture the feeling \\u2192 the robot's generated trajectory perceived as human-like", "notation including both pitch and dynamics \\u2192 the four-dimensional state vector (x, y, force, time)"], "where_it_breaks": "A jazz player reads notation in real time and reinterprets it based on context. The robot commits to a fixed consensus path offline. Notation is infinitely expressive; the four-dimensional representation is lossy. A musician still brings personal interpretation; GMR produces a single averaged trajectory."}, {"concept": "Segmentation of multi-stroke characters into independent trained segments", "domain": "film editing", "setup": "A director shoots a scene from multiple angles and takes, then edits them together. Instead of blending all takes frame-by-frame into an average, the editor recognizes natural boundaries\\u2014the start and end of each beat. She selects the best take for that beat, cuts them together intentionally at seams, and preserves clarity by respecting structure. Averaging all material frame-by-frame would produce a ghostly blur.", "mapping": ["original scene filmed multiple times \\u2192 human demonstrations of multi-stroke characters", "distinct shots (wide, close-up) \\u2192 distinct stroke segments of 'k' (vertical and diagonal)", "pen lifts (gaps in contact) \\u2192 boundaries between takes and intentional cuts", "fitting one model per segment independently \\u2192 training a GMM per stroke", "joining segments together \\u2192 concatenating GMR outputs with transitions"], "where_it_breaks": "An editor makes creative choices about which take is 'best'; the algorithm fits all demonstrations simultaneously. An editor adds layers like music; trajectory concatenation is purely temporal. A film cut is intentional; GMR produces smooth, averaged paths."}, {"concept": "Gaussian Mixture Model + Gaussian Mixture Regression as building consensus from multiple voices", "domain": "eyewitness testimony", "setup": "A lawyer interviews five witnesses to an accident, each with different vantage points and biases. The cyclist saw the car's speed, the pedestrian saw braking, the driver saw the light. None is lying, but accounts overlap and diverge. Rather than taking any single account as truth, she builds a model: which facts show high agreement? When a new question arises\\u2014'what typically happens?'\\u2014she generates a plausible reconstruction respecting areas of agreement and spreading uncertainty over disputed points.", "mapping": ["five witnesses to the accident \\u2192 multiple human demonstrations of a character", "each witness's vantage and biases \\u2192 natural variation across demonstrations (pressure, timing, style)", "clustering coherent testimony \\u2192 the K=20 Gaussian components in the GMM", "learning which features have consensus \\u2192 covariance structure of components capturing shared structure", "fitting the model \\u2192 expectation-maximization (EM) algorithm", "generating a plausible scenario \\u2192 Gaussian Mixture Regression producing a smooth trajectory"], "where_it_breaks": "Witnesses forget, update memory, influence each other; demonstrations are static and independent. A prosecutor can drill into details; the GMM treats all information equally once fitted. A jury decides on narrative; the trajectory smooths all variation into one consensus path."}, {"concept": "Using a continuous 0\\u2013100 perceptual scale to establish a human-likeness benchmark for robot motion", "domain": "restaurant critic", "setup": "A restaurant critic tastes a new dish and asks: is this cooking at high level? A checklist of objective features (salt, heat, plating) misses what matters. The critic's real task is to gauge something subtler: does this taste lived-in or engineered? She assigns a score on a 1\\u201310 scale with anchored endpoints\\u2014'1: all edges sanded down' and '10: the chef cooks for their own table'\\u2014so readers can picture a '7' and calibrate themselves. A single holistic number on a calibrated scale is more useful than binary yes/no.", "mapping": ["the new dish \\u2192 robot-generated handwriting trajectories", "objective checklist (salt, heat, plating) \\u2192 simple metrics like path length or force magnitude", "the critic's holistic impression of aliveness \\u2192 perceived human-likeness as unified perceptual quality", "1\\u201310 scale with anchored endpoints \\u2192 0\\u2013100 continuous scale between 'robotic' and 'human-like'", "the critic's score of 7 \\u2192 the overall mean of 71.5 as calibration for future work", "future critics reading the score \\u2192 future researchers measuring progress against the benchmark"], "where_it_breaks": "Critics have years of expertise; study participants had no special training in handwriting or robotics. A critic's score reflects context (chef's background, competition); ratings were made in isolation. A critic may revisit and change; this was one-shot. A critic writes detailed reasoning; a single number collapses all explanation into one datum."}]}	\N	1	v1	claude-haiku-4-5-20251001	18720	5655	0.046995	77704	\N	1	2026-08-26 23:15:27.393807+00	2026-08-26 23:15:27.703276+00
2	49	diagram	READY	{"mermaid": "flowchart TD\\n    A([22 Participants]) --> B[Touchscreen Teleoperation\\\\n52 Characters x 3 Repetitions]\\n    B --> C[3142 Demonstrations\\\\nPosition, Force, Time]\\n    C --> D[Stroke Segmentation\\\\nPen-lift Detection]\\n    D --> E[4D State Vector\\\\nx, y, force, normalised time]\\n    E --> F[GMM Fitting\\\\nK=20 Components per Segment]\\n    F --> G[GMR Inference\\\\nSmooth Consensus Trajectory]\\n    G --> H[Segment Stitching\\\\nLinear Lift Transitions]\\n    H --> I[Generated Trajectory\\\\n100 Hz Uniform Grid]\\n    I --> J[Simulated Robot Arm\\\\nForce-modulated Visualisation]\\n    J --> K[User Study\\\\n21 Participants, 0-100 Scale]\\n    K --> L([Human-Likeness Score\\\\nMean 71.50 out of 100])\\n\\n    subgraph Data Collection\\n        A\\n        B\\n        C\\n    end\\n\\n    subgraph Extended GMM+GMR Pipeline\\n        D\\n        E\\n        F\\n        G\\n        H\\n        I\\n    end\\n\\n    subgraph Perceptual Evaluation\\n        J\\n        K\\n        L\\n    end", "diagram_type": "flowchart", "caption": "The framework collects multi-dimensional handwriting demonstrations via touchscreen teleoperation, encodes them into per-segment GMMs over position, force, and normalised time, reconstructs full character trajectories via GMR and segment stitching, then evaluates perceived human-likeness through a controlled user study.", "walkthrough": ["22 participants each wrote all 52 uppercase and lowercase Latin characters three times using a capacitive stylus on a 13-inch touchscreen, guided by stroke-order prompts to keep demonstrations consistent.", "The touchscreen teleoperation interface logged planar position, contact force at 80 Hz via two load cells, and timestamps via ROS \\u2014 capturing the full dynamics of each stroke.", "After manual filtering, 3,142 usable demonstrations were retained, forming an open-source dataset of rich, structured handwriting trajectories.", "Pen-lift gaps between strokes were detected using a threshold on Euclidean distance in a weighted 4D feature space, splitting each character into discrete, independently processed stroke segments.", "Each demonstration is represented as a 4D state vector \\u2014 x, y, contact force, and time normalised to [0,1] by average segment duration. This richer input is the paper's key extension over standard GMM+GMR systems that model position only.", "A Gaussian Mixture Model with K=20 components (chosen empirically on held-out demonstrations) is fitted per stroke segment using the EM algorithm, capturing the distribution of human dynamics across all participants.", "Gaussian Mixture Regression conditions on normalised time to retrieve a single smooth consensus trajectory per segment, generalising across the variability in demonstrations.", "Segments are stitched back in temporal order with linear interpolation across pen-lift intervals, and resampled to a uniform 100 Hz grid \\u2014 producing one canonical trajectory per character-case combination.", "A simulated robot arm replayed each generated trajectory with marker thickness and transparency modulated by the learned force profile, so evaluators could perceive force variation visually.", "21 new participants rated each trajectory on a continuous 0\\u2013100 scale anchored between robotic and human-like, yielding a mean human-likeness score of 71.50 (SD=22.56), with 81.2% of ratings exceeding the neutral midpoint of 50."]}	\N	1	v1	claude-sonnet-4-6	5249	1914	0.04991639999999999	28482	\N	1	2026-08-26 23:17:13.695455+00	2026-08-26 23:17:13.731173+00
3	49	code	READY	{"language": "python", "code": "import numpy as np\\nfrom sklearn.mixture import GaussianMixture\\n\\n# \\u2500\\u2500 Utilities \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n\\ndef normalize_time(traj):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Scale raw timestamps to [0, 1].\\n    The paper normalizes by average segment duration across demonstrations;\\n    here we normalize each segment independently for simplicity.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    t = traj[:, 0]\\n    t_norm = (t - t.min()) / (t.max() - t.min() + 1e-9)\\n    return t_norm\\n\\ndef detect_segments(traj, delta_gap=10.0):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Split a multi-stroke trajectory into segments at pen-lift boundaries.\\n\\n    The paper detects lifts by thresholding the Euclidean distance between\\n    consecutive samples in a 4-D feature space [x, y, f, t_norm].\\n    A jump means the pen left the surface and repositioned.\\n\\n    traj columns: [t, x, y, force]\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    xy = traj[:, 1:3]\\n    dists = np.linalg.norm(np.diff(xy, axis=0), axis=1)\\n    split_idx = np.where(dists > delta_gap)[0] + 1   # +1: index after the gap\\n    segments = np.split(traj, split_idx)\\n    return [s for s in segments if len(s) > 2]        # drop degenerate chunks\\n\\ndef resample_segment(seg, n_points=100):\\n    \\"\\"\\"Resample a segment to a uniform time grid (paper uses 100 Hz output).\\"\\"\\"\\n    t_orig = seg[:, 0]\\n    t_new  = np.linspace(t_orig[0], t_orig[-1], n_points)\\n    resampled = np.column_stack([\\n        np.interp(t_new, t_orig, seg[:, col])\\n        for col in range(seg.shape[1])\\n    ])\\n    return resampled\\n\\n# \\u2500\\u2500 Core algorithm \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n\\ndef fit_gmm_segment(demonstrations_seg, K=20):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Fit a GMM to pooled demonstrations for one stroke segment.\\n\\n    State vector per timestep (Eq. 1 in paper):\\n        p(t) = [x, y, force, t_norm]\\n    Training sample (5-D, Eq. 2):\\n        [t, x, y, force, t_norm]\\n\\n    K=20 components chosen empirically by the authors on held-out data.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    pooled = []\\n    for demo in demonstrations_seg:\\n        seg = resample_segment(demo)\\n        t_norm = normalize_time(seg)\\n        # 5-D training vector: [t, x, y, force, t_norm]\\n        sample = np.column_stack([seg[:, 0], seg[:, 1], seg[:, 2],\\n                                  seg[:, 3], t_norm])\\n        pooled.append(sample)\\n    data = np.vstack(pooled)                   # pool all N demonstrations\\n\\n    gmm = GaussianMixture(n_components=K, covariance_type='full',\\n                          reg_covar=1e-4, random_state=0)\\n    gmm.fit(data)\\n    return gmm\\n\\ndef gmr_predict(gmm, t_query):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Gaussian Mixture Regression: retrieve E[p | t] for each query time.\\n\\n    For each Gaussian component k, the conditional mean is:\\n        mu_{p|t,k} = mu_p,k + Sigma_pt,k / Sigma_tt,k * (t - mu_t,k)\\n\\n    The overall prediction is a weighted sum across components,\\n    with weights proportional to the marginal likelihood of t under each k.\\n    This is the standard GMR closed-form solution (Calinon et al., 2010).\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    K = gmm.n_components\\n    preds = np.zeros((len(t_query), 4))   # output: [x, y, force, t_norm]\\n\\n    for i, t in enumerate(t_query):\\n        weights = np.zeros(K)\\n        cond_means = np.zeros((K, 4))\\n\\n        for k in range(K):\\n            mu  = gmm.means_[k]           # shape (5,): [t, x, y, f, t_norm]\\n            cov = gmm.covariances_[k]     # shape (5, 5)\\n\\n            mu_t  = mu[0]\\n            mu_p  = mu[1:]                # [x, y, force, t_norm]\\n            sig_tt = cov[0, 0]            # scalar variance of t\\n            sig_pt = cov[1:, 0]           # cross-covariance (4,)\\n\\n            # Conditional mean: mu_p + Sigma_pt * Sigma_tt^{-1} * (t - mu_t)\\n            cond_means[k] = mu_p + (sig_pt / (sig_tt + 1e-9)) * (t - mu_t)\\n\\n            # Weight = pi_k * N(t; mu_t, sig_tt)  \\u2014 marginal likelihood of t\\n            weights[k] = gmm.weights_[k] * \\\\\\n                np.exp(-0.5 * (t - mu_t)**2 / (sig_tt + 1e-9))\\n\\n        weights /= (weights.sum() + 1e-9)       # normalise\\n        preds[i] = (weights[:, None] * cond_means).sum(axis=0)\\n\\n    return preds   # columns: [x, y, force, t_norm]\\n\\n# \\u2500\\u2500 Top-level pipeline \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n\\ndef learn_character(demonstrations, K=20, n_out=100, delta_gap=10.0):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Full GMM+GMR pipeline for one character-case combination.\\n\\n    demonstrations : list of arrays, each shape (T_i, 4) \\u2192 [t, x, y, force]\\n    Returns        : list of arrays (one per segment) with generated trajectory\\n                     columns [t, x, y, force, t_norm]\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    # Segment every demonstration at pen-lift boundaries\\n    demo_segments = [detect_segments(d, delta_gap) for d in demonstrations]\\n\\n    # Keep only demonstrations that agree on number of segments (most common)\\n    seg_counts = [len(s) for s in demo_segments]\\n    n_segs = max(set(seg_counts), key=seg_counts.count)\\n    demo_segments = [s for s in demo_segments if len(s) == n_segs]\\n\\n    generated_segments = []\\n    for seg_idx in range(n_segs):\\n        seg_demos = [d[seg_idx] for d in demo_segments]\\n        gmm = fit_gmm_segment(seg_demos, K=K)\\n\\n        # Query time spans average segment duration\\n        avg_dur = np.mean([d[-1, 0] - d[0, 0] for d in seg_demos])\\n        t_query = np.linspace(0, avg_dur, n_out)\\n\\n        traj = gmr_predict(gmm, t_query)          # [x, y, force, t_norm]\\n        generated_segments.append(\\n            np.column_stack([t_query, traj])       # prepend absolute time\\n        )\\n\\n    return generated_segments   # stitch in caller with linear pen-lift transitions", "what_it_does": "Implements the paper's extended GMM+GMR algorithm for handwriting trajectory learning. Each stroke segment of a character is modelled with a 20-component Gaussian Mixture Model over a 5-D space (time, x, y, contact-force, normalised-time); Gaussian Mixture Regression then retrieves a smooth consensus trajectory conditioned on time. Pen-lift discontinuities are handled by segmenting each demonstration before fitting and stitching the resulting segments back in temporal order.", "example_usage": "\\n# Mock two demonstrations of a single-stroke character (e.g. 'o')\\n# Each demo: shape (T, 4) \\u2192 [t, x, y, force]\\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\\ndef fake_demo(noise=2.0, n=80):\\n    t = np.linspace(0, 2.0, n)\\n    angle = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, n)\\n    x = 30 * np.cos(angle) + rng.normal(0, noise, n)\\n    y = 30 * np.sin(angle) + rng.normal(0, noise, n)\\n    force = 1.2 + 0.3 * np.sin(angle) + rng.normal(0, 0.1, n)\\n    return np.column_stack([t, x, y, force])\\n\\ndemos = [fake_demo() for _ in range(6)]\\nsegments = learn_character(demos, K=5, n_out=50)\\n\\n# Each segment is shape (50, 5): [t, x, y, force, t_norm]\\nprint(segments[0].shape)   # \\u2192 (50, 5)\\nprint(segments[0][:3, :])\\n# Expected: smooth circular trajectory with ~1.2 N force profile\\n# array([[ 0.   , 30.x  ,  0.y  ,  1.2f ,  0.  ],\\n#        [ 0.04 ,  ...  ,  ...  ,  ...  ,  0.02],\\n#        [ 0.08 ,  ...  ,  ...  ,  ...  ,  0.04]])\\n", "caveats": ["The paper uses sklearn's GaussianMixture (EM) but initialises with K=20 chosen on held-out data; this implementation exposes K as a parameter and does not include the model-selection procedure.", "Segment detection in the paper uses a weighted 4-D feature space [x, y, f, t_norm] with tuned per-character thresholds; here only Euclidean distance in (x,y) is used with a single global threshold delta_gap.", "The paper normalises t_norm by the *average segment duration across all N demonstrations*; this implementation normalises each segment independently for clarity.", "Pen-lift linear interpolation between segments is described but not implemented here \\u2014 callers receive a list of segments to stitch.", "The paper resamples input demonstrations to a uniform 40 Hz grid before training; this implementation resamples each segment to n_points=100 points without enforcing a physical frequency."]}	\N	1	v1	claude-sonnet-4-6	11407	2689	0.07960350000000001	45717	1	1	2026-08-26 23:50:14.085061+00	2026-08-26 23:50:14.106519+00
4	49	math	READY	{"intuition": "The paper's core idea is that handwriting is not just a spatial path \\u2014 it also has a force signature and a rhythm. By encoding all three (position, force, normalized time) into a probabilistic model, you can learn a single \\"consensus\\" trajectory from many messy human demonstrations. The Gaussian Mixture Model clusters the joint distribution of time and state; Gaussian Mixture Regression then reads back a smooth trajectory by conditioning on time. The key structural extension is splitting multi-stroke characters into independent segments before fitting, so a pen-lift in the middle of a letter doesn't corrupt what the model learns about each individual stroke.", "equations": [{"latex": "p(t) = [x(t),\\\\; y(t),\\\\; f(t),\\\\; t_{\\\\text{norm}}(t)]^\\\\top", "label": "Equation 1, the state vector", "plain_reading": "At every timestep, the thing being learned is a four-number bundle: where the pen tip is in x and y, how hard it is pressing down, and how far through the stroke it is as a fraction between zero and one.", "what_each_symbol_means": ["p(t) \\u2014 the full state of the pen at time t; the fundamental unit of a demonstration", "x(t) \\u2014 horizontal position of the pen tip, in millimetres", "y(t) \\u2014 vertical position of the pen tip, in millimetres", "f(t) \\u2014 contact force applied by the pen, in Newtons", "t_{\\\\text{norm}}(t) \\u2014 time normalized to [0,1] by the average segment duration; makes demonstrations comparable regardless of how fast someone wrote"], "why_it_matters": "This is the paper's primary extension over vanilla GMM+GMR, which only encodes (x, y). By folding force and normalized time into the state vector, the learned model captures not just where the pen goes but how firmly and at what pace \\u2014 the two dimensions that make motion feel human rather than mechanical."}, {"latex": "P(t,\\\\, p) = \\\\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\\\pi_k \\\\, \\\\mathcal{N}\\\\!\\\\left(p \\\\mid \\\\mu_k,\\\\, \\\\Sigma_k\\\\right)", "label": "Equation 2, the GMM joint distribution", "plain_reading": "The probability of seeing state p at time t is a weighted average of K bell-curve blobs in the joint time-and-state space. Each blob has its own centre and shape, and the weights all add up to one.", "what_each_symbol_means": ["P(t, p) \\u2014 the joint probability density over time t and state p, learned from all demonstrations", "K \\u2014 number of Gaussian components; set to 20 in this paper, chosen empirically", "\\\\pi_k \\u2014 mixing weight of the k-th component; how much of the overall distribution that blob accounts for", "\\\\mathcal{N}(p \\\\mid \\\\mu_k, \\\\Sigma_k) \\u2014 a multivariate Gaussian blob centred at \\\\mu_k with spread \\\\Sigma_k", "\\\\mu_k \\u2014 mean vector of the k-th component, split into a time part and a state part", "\\\\Sigma_k \\u2014 covariance matrix of the k-th component, encoding how state dimensions co-vary with each other and with time"], "why_it_matters": "This is the encoding step. All N demonstrations are pooled and the EM algorithm fits these K Gaussians to the cloud of (time, state) samples, compressing many noisy human trajectories into a compact probabilistic representation that GMR can then query."}, {"latex": "\\\\Sigma_k = \\\\begin{bmatrix} \\\\Sigma_{tt,k} & \\\\Sigma_{tp,k} \\\\\\\\ \\\\Sigma_{pt,k} & \\\\Sigma_{pp,k} \\\\end{bmatrix}", "label": "Equation 3, the partitioned covariance matrix", "plain_reading": "Each Gaussian blob's covariance matrix is carved into four quadrants: how time varies with itself, how the state dimensions vary among themselves, and the cross-terms that capture how state changes as time changes.", "what_each_symbol_means": ["\\\\Sigma_k \\u2014 full 5\\u00d75 covariance matrix for the k-th Gaussian component", "\\\\Sigma_{tt,k} \\u2014 scalar variance of the time dimension for component k", "\\\\Sigma_{pp,k} \\u2014 4\\u00d74 covariance among the four state dimensions (x, y, f, t_norm) for component k", "\\\\Sigma_{tp,k} \\u2014 1\\u00d74 cross-covariance between time and state; encodes how the state tends to look at a given time", "\\\\Sigma_{pt,k} \\u2014 transpose of \\\\Sigma_{tp,k}"], "why_it_matters": "GMR uses exactly these cross-covariance blocks to compute the conditional expectation of state given time. Without the off-diagonal terms, the model could not recover a smooth trajectory from a time query \\u2014 they are the mathematical bridge between encoding and retrieval."}, {"latex": "\\\\|x_{i+1} - x_i\\\\|_2 > \\\\delta_{\\\\text{gap}}", "label": "Equation 6, the pen-lift detection criterion", "plain_reading": "If the jump from one sample to the next in the weighted four-dimensional feature space is larger than a threshold, declare a pen lift and start a new stroke segment there.", "what_each_symbol_means": ["x_{i+1}, x_i \\u2014 consecutive samples in the four-dimensional feature space (position, force, and time, with unit-balancing weights applied)", "\\\\|\\\\cdot\\\\|_2 \\u2014 Euclidean distance in that weighted space", "\\\\delta_{\\\\text{gap}} \\u2014 the threshold distance above which a gap is declared a pen lift"], "why_it_matters": "Multi-stroke characters like 'F' or 'k' have pen lifts that create discontinuities no single GMM can bridge cleanly. This detection step splits each demonstration into segments, each of which gets its own GMM+GMR fit, then stitches them back together \\u2014 the key structural adaptation that lets the framework handle the full alphabet."}], "the_trick": "The cross-covariance blocks (\\u03a3_tp and \\u03a3_pt) inside each Gaussian component are what make GMR work. When you ask \\"what should the state be at time t?\\", GMR computes a weighted conditional mean across all K components using exactly those blocks \\u2014 effectively reading off the slope of the relationship between time and state that was baked in during EM fitting. That single matrix partition turns a density estimator into a trajectory retriever, with no separate regression model needed."}	\N	1	v1	claude-sonnet-4-6	11408	1728	0.0675165	36436	1	1	2026-08-26 23:54:56.352606+00	2026-08-26 23:54:56.365768+00
