8	70	llamaparse	# BENDTWIN: ROBUST DENSE-TO-SPARSE PHYSICAL RECONSTRUCTION WITH BENDING-AWARE DIFFERENTIABLE SPRING–MASS MODELS\n\nYixiong Jing∗, Qi Wang∗\nUniversity of Cambridge, Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences\nyj401@cam.ac.uk, qi.wang@ia.ac.cn\n\nLin Chen, Junwei Jiang, Guangming Wang†\nNorthwestern Polytechnical University, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, University of Cambridge\nnpuchenlin@foxmail.com, junwei.jiang97@outlook.com, gw462@cam.ac.uk\n\nHaibing Wu, Olaf Wysocki, Wanli Ma, Brian Sheil\nUniversity of Cambridge\nhw657@cam.ac.uk, okw24@cam.ac.uk, wm369@cam.ac.uk, bbs24@cam.ac.uk\n\narXiv:2608.06164v1 [cs.CV] 6 Aug 2026\nAugust 7, 2026\n\n## ABSTRACT\n\nReconstructing objects with mechanical properties from video observations enables physically consistent dynamic prediction, benefiting robotics planning and interaction. Existing spring–mass based physical driven reconstruction approaches offer efficient and differentiable physical reconstruction, but they typically rely on axial springs alone. Such formulations oversimplify the underlying structural mechanics and can become mechanically under-constrained when the physical graph is coarsened, limiting their ability to preserve stable local deformation. We present BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework for video-based reconstruction and future prediction of deformable objects. BendTwin introduces bending stiffness and damping over local surface triplets, penalizing deviations from rest angles and regularizing higher-order deformation. These bending constraints improve mechanical stability while preserving the simplicity of spring–mass system. Experiments show that BendTwin consistently outperforms the axial-only PhysTwin baseline. Ablation studies further demonstrate that the bending constraints maintain system stability across different downsampling ratios and consistently improve upon the original PhysTwin formulation. Overall, BendTwin provides an effective approach for constructing mechanically faithful digital twins from sparse-view RGB-D videos.\n\n**Keywords:** Differentiable Physics · Physics-Informed Reconstruction · Spring–Mass Models\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nCreating digital twins of real-world objects is essential for predicting how objects move and deform under interaction. Accurate simulation of future dynamic states from a physically consistent digital twin enables a wide range of downstream applications, including robot manipulation planning [1, 2, 3], data generation for policy learning [4, 5, 6], and physically grounded rollouts within vision-based world models [7]. To obtain such digital twins, it is critical to...\n\n\n\n# Sparse-view RGB-D 3D Reconstruction\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we introduce BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework designed for the reconstruction of deformable objects from sparse-view RGB-D videos. By augmenting conventional axial spring formulations with explicit bending stiffness and damping defined over local surface triplets, BendTwin enhances mechanical expressiveness, enabling improved reconstruction, re-simulation, and future prediction. Our experiments demonstrate that BendTwin consistently outperforms the axial-only PhysTwin baseline, particularly in scenarios with sparse physical graphs.\n\n## Introduction\nThe ability to infer physically plausible models directly from observations such as RGB-D videos has the potential to reduce manual modeling efforts and bridge the gap between simulated dynamics and real-world behavior. However, achieving precise reconstruction of the physical model of a real object remains a significant challenge. Traditional graphics and engineering pipelines often rely on carefully designed constitutive models and discretizations [8, 9, 10], typically assuming that the geometry and mechanical properties of objects are known a priori or identified through controlled experiments. While these methods can yield accurate results, they are often expensive and difficult to generalize.\n\nRecent advancements in 3D reconstruction [11, 12] and generative models [13, 14] have enabled detailed geometric modeling of real-world objects. When combined with classical continuum mechanics simulators, these advancements can produce visually convincing deformations [15, 16]. However, material properties in prior work are often manually assigned or generatively produced, which may not accurately reflect the true dynamics of the corresponding objects, thereby limiting physical fidelity. Differentiable physics [17, 18, 19] addresses this limitation by allowing for the direct optimization of unknown physical parameters in governing equations through the matching of simulated dynamics to observations.\n\nPhysTwin [20] leverages this paradigm by reconstructing object geometry from sparse-view RGB-D videos using 3D Gaussian Splatting [11] and TRELLIS [13], optimizing a differentiable spring–mass system with learnable mechanical and contact parameters while enforcing visual consistency via Gaussian rendering. However, PhysTwin primarily relies on axial springs and damping. While effective for capturing stretching-dominated motion, axial-only formulations constrain only pairwise distances and do not explicitly regulate local angular deformation. As the physical graph (e.g., the node counts for PhysTwin [20]) becomes sparse with fewer axial connections, the limited mechanical expressiveness of axial-only formulations can leave the system under-constrained, leading to challenges in preserving stable local deformation.\n\nTo address this limitation, we introduce BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework illustrated in Fig. 1. BendTwin augments the conventional axial spring formulation with explicit bending stiffness and damping defined over local surface triplets. These bending constraints complement axial length constraints by penalizing deviations from rest angles, enabling the model to better preserve local shape and resist unstable deformation modes. Consequently, BendTwin remains mechanically stable even as the spring–mass graph is coarsened to far fewer nodes and connections, while maintaining the simplicity of explicit spring–mass dynamics.\n\nOur experiments demonstrate consistent improvements over the axial-only PhysTwin baseline in reconstruction, re-simulation, and future prediction. Ablation studies further show that explicit bending improves mechanical fidelity and maintains stable behavior as the physical graph becomes increasingly sparse under different downsampling ratios.\n\n## Contributions\nIn summary, our contributions are:\n- We propose BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework that augments axial interactions with explicit bending stiffness and damping over local surface triplets, improving mechanical expressiveness for video-based physical reconstruction, simulation, and prediction.\n- We show that explicit bending constraints stabilize the spring–mass system under different downsampling ratios, allowing BendTwin to retain accuracy with far fewer nodes where axial-only formulations become under-constrained.\n\n## Method\n[Details of the method would be included here, including equations and specific algorithms used in BendTwin.]\n\n## Results\n[Results of the experiments, including quantitative and qualitative comparisons with PhysTwin and other baselines, would be presented here.]\n\n## Conclusion\n[The conclusion would summarize the findings and potential future work related to BendTwin and its applications in 3D reconstruction and simulation.]\n\n\n\n# BendTwin: A Bending-Aware Differentiable Physics Framework\n\n## Abstract\nWe present BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable physics framework designed to estimate mechanical parameters by matching simulated dynamics to partial 3D observations from videos. By augmenting traditional spring-mass models with explicit bending stiffness and damping, BendTwin enhances the mechanical stability of sparse systems while maintaining the simplicity of explicit differentiable simulation.\n\n## Introduction\nThe reconstruction of physically consistent digital twins from multi-view RGB-D videos poses significant challenges, particularly in accurately estimating mechanical parameters. This paper introduces BendTwin, a framework that addresses these challenges by incorporating bending-aware mechanics into a differentiable physics simulation.\n\n## Method\n\n### 2.1 Problem Formulation\nGiven multi-view RGB-D videos of an object undergoing interaction, our goal is to reconstruct a digital twin by estimating its mechanical parameters using BendTwin. The process begins with sparse-view RGB-D observations, which are discretized into a spring-mass system for inverse mechanical parameter estimation.\n\nWe represent the object as a graph \\( G = \\{V, E, T\\} \\), where \\( V \\) is a set of surface mass nodes connected by axial springs \\( E \\) and bending triplets \\( T \\). Each node \\( i \\in V \\) has a position \\( x^t \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\) and a velocity \\( v^t \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\) at time \\( t \\). The system state at time \\( t \\) is defined as \\( X_t = \\{x^t, v^t\\}_{i \\in V} \\).\n\nLet \\( X^{obs} \\) denote the partial 3D observation of the object at time \\( t \\) (e.g., a point cloud derived from sparse-view RGB-D frames). Our objective is to estimate the physical parameters of the deformable object, which we collect into:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\{k_{ij}, \\gamma_{ij}\\}_{(i,j) \\in E} \\cup \\{k_{\\theta,(i,j,k)}, c_{b,(i,j,k)}\\}_{(i,j,k) \\in T} \\cup \\alpha_{contact},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( k_{ij} \\) is the axial stiffness and \\( \\gamma_{ij} \\) is the dashpot damping coefficient. The term \\( k_{\\theta,(i,j,k)} \\) penalizes deviations from the rest angle of triplet \\( (i, j, k) \\), and \\( c_{b,(i,j,k)} \\) specifies viscous bending damping proportional to the angular rate \\( \\dot{\\theta}_{(i,j,k)} \\). The parameters \\( \\alpha_{contact} \\) characterize the contact model, including restitution and friction coefficients.\n\nOur objective minimizes the discrepancy between simulated and observed motion:\n\n\\[\n\\min_{\\alpha, G_t} L(\\hat{X}^{obs}) = \\sum_{t=0}^{T} \\left( L_{geo}(\\hat{X}^t, X_t) + L_{trk}(\\hat{X}^t, X_t) \\right) \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad X_{t+1} = F_{\\alpha, G_t}(X_t).\n\\]\n\n## Results\nThe BendTwin framework was evaluated on various datasets, demonstrating its effectiveness in reconstructing the dynamics of deformable objects. The integration of bending mechanics significantly improved the accuracy of the simulations compared to traditional spring-mass models.\n\n## Conclusion\nBendTwin provides a robust solution for estimating mechanical parameters in deformable objects using sparse RGB-D observations. By incorporating bending-aware mechanics, it enhances the stability and accuracy of simulations, paving the way for more realistic digital twins in various applications.\n\n## Figure Captions\nFigure 2: BendTwin framework overview. (a) represents the 3D reconstruction from sparse-view RGB-D observations into a point-based surface discretization. (b) indicates that BendTwin extends the spring–mass model with explicit bending triplets in addition to axial springs and (c) optimizes physical parameters using geometry and tracking losses. (d) visualizes the details of the proposed bending mechanism. (e) demonstrates differentiable explicit simulation of the bending-aware spring–mass system.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n\n### 2.2 Bending-Enhanced Spring–Mass Dynamics\n\nNaive spring–mass models (used in PhysTwin [20] and Spring-Gau [21]) rely solely on axial springs to resist stretching and compression. While effective for modeling compliant deformation, axial-only interactions do not explicitly model bending mechanics. Consequently, bending-dominated surface deformations must be approximated indirectly through axial coupling, limiting the mechanical expressiveness of the system and reducing its ability to faithfully reproduce complex deformation behaviors.\n\nTo address these limitations, BendTwin augments the spring–mass model with explicit bending stiffness defined directly over local surface configurations. Specifically, we introduce bending springs over node triplets \\((i, j, k)\\) to penalize angular deviations, allowing bending resistance to be modeled explicitly. These bending constraints complement axial springs and reduce under-constrained local deformation modes when the system contains fewer sampled nodes and connections. Meanwhile, BendTwin preserves the simplicity of spring–mass systems while improving the mechanical stability of sparse discretizations.\n\nWe define the total internal potential energy \\(E_{\\text{int}}(X)\\) as\n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{int}}(X) = \\sum_{(i,j) \\in E} E_{\\text{axial}}(i, j) + \\sum_{(i,j,k) \\in T} E_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(E_{\\text{axial}}(i, j)\\) denotes the standard axial spring energy, whose induced nodal forces are identical to those used in PhysTwin [20] and therefore omitted for brevity. This paper only introduces the derivation of the additional forces introduced by the proposed bending energy \\(E_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k)\\), which can be obtained by differentiating \\(E_{\\text{bend}}\\) with respect to node positions:\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{bend}} = -\\nabla_{x} E_{\\text{bend}}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n### 2.2.1 Bending Stiffness\n\nTo model bending, we introduce bending springs over local triplets \\((i, j, k) \\in T\\), where \\(j\\) is the center node and \\(i, k\\) are its neighbors. The rest angle \\(\\theta^0\\) corresponds to the angle between edges \\((i, j)\\) and \\((k, j)\\) in \\(G_0\\). We define the bending energy as\n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k) = \\frac{1}{2} k_{\\theta,(i,j,k)} (\\theta - \\theta^0)^2, \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) denotes the current angle between the two edges. Differentiating Eq. (5) yields restoring forces that act to reduce the angular deviation:\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{bend}} = -k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) d(i,j,k), \\quad f_{\\text{bend}} = -k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) d(i,j,k). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d(i,j,k)\\) and \\(d(i,j,k)\\) are unit-scaled directional vectors determined by the local geometry of the \\((i, j, k)\\). These directions ensure torque-consistent application of force and are derived in the Appendix. By Newton’s third law (force balance within the \\((i, j, k)\\)), the force on \\(j\\) is\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{bend}} = - (f_{\\text{bend}} + f_{\\text{bend}}) = k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) (d(i,j,k) + d(i,j,k)). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n### 2.2.2 Bending Damping\n\nIn addition to elastic bending stiffness, we introduce bending damping to dissipate energy associated with angular motion to avoid the oscillations of the system caused by the extra bending stiffness. For each bending triplet \\((i, j, k)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) \\text{ measures the relative angular motion of edges } (i, j) \\text{ and } (k, j).\n\\]\n\nWe model bending damping as a viscous torque proportional to this angular rate, with damping coefficient \\(c_{b,(i,j,k)}\\). The resulting nodal damping forces are\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{rot}}^i = c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) d_i, \\quad f_k = c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) d_k. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe equivalent damping force introduced on the center node \\(j\\) is\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{rot}}^j = - (f_{\\text{rot}}^i + f_{\\text{rot}}^k) = c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) (d_i + d_k). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe force directions \\(d(i,j,k)\\) and \\(d(i,j,k)\\) are identical to those used for elastic bending forces. The explicit computation of \\(\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k)\\) from nodal velocities is provided in the Appendix.\n\n## Results\n\n[Results content goes here.]\n\n## Conclusion\n\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n\n[References content goes here.]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## 2.2.3 Efficient Triplet Construction for High-Valence Nodes\n\nIf node \\( j \\) has \\( N \\) neighbors, enumerating all neighbor pairs yields \\( O(N^2) \\) triplets, which is expensive and often introduces redundant stiffness in the model. Instead, we construct \\( O(N) \\) triplets by selecting a reference neighbor \\( r(j) \\in N(j) \\) and forming triplets \\( (r(j), j, k) \\) for all \\( k \\in N(j) \\setminus \\{r(j)\\} \\) (or a random subset). This preserves bending expressivity while keeping the model scalable. Given the axial springs, bending triplets, and external interactions, the total force acting on node \\( j \\) is computed as\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{tot}} = \\sum_{i \\in NE(j)} (f^{\\text{axial}} + f^{\\text{dash}}) + \\sum_{(i,j,k) \\in T} (f^{\\text{bend}} + f^{\\text{rot}}) + f^{\\text{ext}}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n## 3 Experiments\n\nIn this section, we evaluate BendTwin to assess the impact of bending stiffness on mechanical fidelity and simulation stability. Specifically, we examine (1) whether bending-aware constraints improve object reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation and future prediction over the axial-only PhysTwin baseline; and (2) whether bending constraints preserve accuracy and stability as the physical system is progressively downsampled.\n\n### 3.1 Experimental Settings\n\n**Dataset.** We use the non-cloth deformable object sequences from the PhysTwin benchmark [20]. The revised evaluation excludes fabric-like sheet sequences because cloth is highly compliant and exhibits little intrinsic resistance to bending or shearing, whereas our bending-aware model assumes objects with sufficient structural support for stable bending constraints. Following the original protocol, each sequence is split into training and testing frames with a 7:3 ratio. The training split is used for the reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation, while the test split evaluates the future prediction.\n\n**Baselines.** The primary comparison is against PhysTwin [20], which adopts an axial spring–mass formulation. This comparison directly evaluates whether incorporating bending constraints improves accuracy and enables the assessment of mechanical stability in sparse physical systems.\n\n**Evaluation metrics.** To evaluate reconstruction and prediction accuracy, we report 3D metrics including Chamfer Distance (CD) and tracking error, and 2D rendering metrics including PSNR, SSIM [22], LPIPS [23], and silhouette IoU computed from 3D Gaussian Splatting renderings.\n\n**Implementation details.** Our implementation is based on PyTorch and Warp for GPU-accelerated spring–mass simulation. All experiments are performed on one NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPU. We follow PhysTwin [20] for 3D reconstruction. Parameters \\( \\alpha \\) are optimized via Adam with \\( lr = 10^{-3} \\) to minimize \\( L_{\\text{geo}} + L_{\\text{trk}} \\) with equal weights. For the active non-cloth evaluation, all simulations use the differentiable explicit Euler rollout \\( F^{\\text{exp}} \\) with sub-step \\( \\Delta t = 5 \\times 10^{-5} \\) s, \\( M_t \\approx 667 \\) sub-steps per frame, and maximum axial stiffness \\( k_{ij} = 10^5 \\). Collision parameters \\( \\alpha_{\\text{contact}} \\) use restitution 0.5/0.7 and friction 0.3.\n\n### 3.2 Results\n\n**Reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation.** We first evaluate reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation on the non-cloth object set using the explicit Euler simulator for all methods. The quantitative results are reported in Table 1. Compared with the axial-only PhysTwin baseline, BendTwin reduces CD from 0.0058 to 0.0047 (e.g., 19.0% improvement), and reduces tracking error from 0.0091 to 0.0078 (e.g., 14.3% improvement). These gains indicate that the added bending stiffness and damping provide useful local shape constraints beyond pairwise axial springs, allowing the physical graph to better preserve object structure during re-simulation.\n\nThe improvement on geometric accuracy is reflected in the rendered observations. BendTwin improves PSNR from 28.429 to 28.605, a gain of 0.18 dB, raises IoU from 79.5 to 80.6, and reduces LPIPS from 0.022 to 0.021. Overall, the results show that explicit bending constraints make the reconstructed dynamics more geometrically accurate while maintaining comparable visual quality for deformable objects.\n\n**Future prediction.** We further evaluate future prediction by optimizing physical parameters on the training portion of each sequence and then simulating forward to unseen frames. As shown in Table 1, BendTwin also improves long-horizon prediction over PhysTwin. CD decreases from 0.0095 to 0.0079 (e.g., 16.8% reduction), and tracking error decreases from 0.0166 to 0.0153 (e.g., 7.8% reduction). These results suggest that bending-aware constraints...\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n[Method content goes here.]\n\n## Results\n\n### Quantitative Results\nTable 1 presents the quantitative results on Reconstruction &#x26; Re-simulation and Future Prediction. The metrics include Chamfer Distance (CD), Tracking Error (Track), Intersection over Union (IoU), Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), Structural Similarity Index (SSIM), and Learned Perceptual Image Patch Similarity (LPIPS).\n\n| Model            | CD ↓   | Track ↓ | IoU % ↑ | PSNR ↑  | SSIM ↑  | LPIPS ↓ | CD ↓   | Track ↓ | IoU % ↑ | PSNR ↑  | SSIM ↑  | LPIPS ↓ |\n|------------------|--------|---------|---------|---------|---------|---------|--------|---------|---------|---------|---------|---------|\n| PhysTwin         | 0.0058 | 0.0091  | 79.5    | 28.429  | 0.963   | 0.022   | 0.0095 | 0.0166  | 68.7    | 26.412  | 0.957   | 0.036   |\n| BendTwin (Ours)  | 0.0047 | 0.0078  | 80.6    | 28.605  | 0.963   | 0.021   | 0.0079 | 0.0153  | 71.5    | 26.755  | 0.957   | 0.035   |\n\nThe results indicate that BendTwin outperforms PhysTwin in both reconstruction and future prediction tasks. Specifically, BendTwin improves IoU from 68.7 to 71.5 and PSNR from 26.412 to 26.755, corresponding to gains of 2.8 percentage points and 0.34 dB, respectively. These improvements suggest that explicitly modeling bending stiffness leads to more physically consistent motion propagation over time.\n\n### Qualitative Results\nFigure 3 illustrates the qualitative results of BendTwin and the comparison method on the reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation and future predictions of object deformation. The qualitative results show that our method is closer to the true values than other methods, with red boxes highlighting regions where BendTwin better captures object deformation than PhysTwin.\n\n### Ablation Studies\n#### Downsampled Physical System\nWe evaluate whether providing extra bending constraints maintains stable and accurate dynamics as the physical graph is downsampled. For each sampling ratio \\( r \\), both PhysTwin and BendTwin downsample the full physical graph at the same ratio \\( r \\), ensuring a matched graph. The only difference between the two systems is the presence of the proposed bending springs. A smaller \\( r \\) retains fewer mass points (e.g., progressively compressing the physical system) for testing whether bending constraints preserve stability where axial springs alone become under-constrained.\n\nTable 2 shows that BendTwin consistently outperforms the axial-only PhysTwin across all metrics at both downsampling ratios, and that its advantage grows substantially as the physical graph becomes sparser. At \\( r = 0.1 \\), BendTwin reduces reconstruction CD by 9.6% and tracking error by 10.8%, while improving PSNR by 0.45 dB and IoU by 1.6%.\n\n## Conclusion\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n\n### Robustness to Physical-Graph Downsampling\n\nTable 2 presents the robustness of both PhysTwin and BendTwin to physical-graph downsampling. Both models retain interior and exterior nodes and are downsampled at the same ratio \\( r \\); the only difference is the proposed bending springs.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Sampling ratio } r &#x26; \\text{Model} &#x26; \\text{Reconstruction \\&#x26; Re-simulation} &#x26; \\text{Future Prediction} \\\\\n\\hline\n&#x26; &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow \\\\\n\\hline\n0.05 &#x26; \\text{PhysTwin} &#x26; 0.0139 &#x26; 0.0175 &#x26; 72.2 &#x26; 26.926 &#x26; 0.958 &#x26; 0.0309 &#x26; 0.0216 &#x26; 0.0241 &#x26; 60.8 &#x26; 25.276 &#x26; 0.953 &#x26; 0.0476 \\\\\n&#x26; \\text{BendTwin (Ours)} &#x26; 0.0102 &#x26; 0.0130 &#x26; 77.2 &#x26; 27.996 &#x26; 0.961 &#x26; 0.0262 &#x26; 0.0176 &#x26; 0.0202 &#x26; 67.8 &#x26; 26.417 &#x26; 0.955 &#x26; 0.0411 \\\\\n\\hline\n0.1 &#x26; \\text{PhysTwin} &#x26; 0.0088 &#x26; 0.0120 &#x26; 78.2 &#x26; 28.105 &#x26; 0.962 &#x26; 0.0245 &#x26; 0.0150 &#x26; 0.0198 &#x26; 66.6 &#x26; 26.116 &#x26; 0.955 &#x26; 0.0409 \\\\\n&#x26; \\text{BendTwin (Ours)} &#x26; 0.0079 &#x26; 0.0107 &#x26; 79.7 &#x26; 28.552 &#x26; 0.963 &#x26; 0.0222 &#x26; 0.0139 &#x26; 0.0181 &#x26; 69.1 &#x26; 26.620 &#x26; 0.957 &#x26; 0.0362 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n### Removal of Interior Nodes\n\nPhysTwin discretizes each volumetric object into exterior surface nodes and interior nodes. Although the interior nodes are unobserved and excluded from evaluation, the axial springs incident to them provide internal mechanical support and help prevent the surface from collapsing inward during interaction. We argue that BendTwin no longer requires this support because its bending term directly penalizes changes in local surface angles, thereby resisting the non-physical surface distortions that the interior axial springs are intended to suppress. A volumetric object may therefore be represented as a surface-only physical system.\n\nWe demonstrate this with an ablation at full sampling density (\\( r = 1.0 \\)), removing all interior nodes from BendTwin and simulating only the surface nodes. We compare this surface-only configuration with the axial-only PhysTwin baseline, which retains its complete interior discretization. All metrics are evaluated on surface points for both methods, ensuring that the evaluation support is identical.\n\nAs reported in Table 3, removing the entire interior discretization does not degrade the surface-level accuracy of BendTwin. For reconstruction and re-simulation, the surface-only BendTwin reduces CD by approximately 6.9% and tracking error by 3.3%, while increasing IoU by 1.1 percentage points. The gains are more pronounced for future prediction, i.e., CD and LPIPS decrease by approximately 5.3% and 6.7%, respectively, while PSNR increases by 0.570 dB and IoU by 3.3 percentage points. Bending constraints are therefore sufficient to replace the mechanical role of interior nodes. A pure surface system reproduces volumetric deformation as faithfully as an axial-only system that supports its interior explicitly, while simulating fewer mass points.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Configuration} &#x26; \\text{Interior nodes} &#x26; \\text{Reconstruction \\&#x26; Re-simulation} &#x26; \\text{Future Prediction} \\\\\n\\hline\n&#x26; &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{PhysTwin} &#x26; \\text{w/} &#x26; 0.0058 &#x26; 0.0091 &#x26; 79.5 &#x26; 28.429 &#x26; 0.963 &#x26; 0.0225 &#x26; 0.0095 &#x26; 0.0166 &#x26; 68.7 &#x26; 26.412 &#x26; 0.957 &#x26; 0.0357 \\\\\n\\text{BendTwin (Ours)} &#x26; \\text{w/o} &#x26; 0.0054 &#x26; 0.0088 &#x26; 80.6 &#x26; 28.487 &#x26; 0.963 &#x26; 0.0220 &#x26; 0.0090 &#x26; 0.0167 &#x26; 72.0 &#x26; 26.982 &#x26; 0.959 &#x26; 0.0333 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n## Results\n\n[Results content goes here.]\n\n## Related Works\n\n### 3D Reconstruction with Dynamics\n\nDynamic reconstruction goes beyond multi-view geometric consistency and additionally requires temporal coherence modeling to capture long-term changes over time. Classical approaches typically rely on inter-frame correspondence estimation, such as feature-based matching [24, 25] to perform tracking and reconstruction across frames. However, these pipelines often struggle to produce dense reconstructions due to sparse keypoint reliance and tracking drift over long sequences.\n\nRecent advances in neural scene representations, particularly Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [12], have introduced implicit volumetric representations capable of synthesizing high-fidelity novel views. Extensions to dynamic settings [26, 27] incorporate temporal deformation fields to model non-rigid scene evolution. More recently, 3D Gaussian [content continues here].\n\n[Continue with the rest of the paper.]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\nSplatting has emerged as an explicit and efficient 3D representation enabling real-time, high-quality rendering. Dynamic extensions such as 4D Gaussian representations tightly integrate visual tracking, spatio-temporal representation learning, and differentiable rendering, achieving temporally consistent dynamic scene reconstruction with improved efficiency and fidelity. Despite these advances, current reconstruction methods remain largely geometry- and appearance-driven. The reconstructed objects typically lack integrated physical priors, which limits their ability to predict future states or support physically grounded interaction. Without embedding physics-based constraints or dynamics modeling, reconstructed 4D scenes function primarily as descriptive representations rather than interactive prediction or action simulation.\n\n## 4.2 Physical Simulation and Differentiable Physics\n\nClassical physical simulation in computer graphics relies on established numerical formulations such as Lagrangian mechanics, finite element methods, material point methods (MPM), and position-based dynamics. These approaches have enabled realistic simulation of rigid and deformable objects across a wide range of applications. However, they typically assume that material parameters are manually specified or calibrated offline, limiting their applicability when physical properties are unknown. Differentiable physics aims to bridge this gap by making complex dynamic systems differentiable, allowing unknown physical parameters to be optimized from observations using gradient-based methods. A number of differentiable simulators have been developed for rigid-body dynamics and soft-body dynamics. While these methods enable parameter optimization by fitting simulated dynamics to observations, they do not reconstruct a complete digital twin that simultaneously recovers geometry, material behavior, and appearance from sparse visual inputs.\n\n## 4.3 Physics-Driven 3D Reconstruction\n\nRecent works incorporate continuum mechanics into neural scene representations such as NeRF and 3D Gaussian Splatting to enable dynamic deformation and interaction. However, these approaches typically assume predefined material properties and focus on visually plausible motion rather than recovering true object dynamics. Differentiable physics addresses this limitation by enabling optimization of physical parameters directly from RGB observations, but such methods often operate on implicit geometry and do not explicitly reconstruct physically consistent 3D structure. A more closely related line of work constructs physical digital twins using spring–mass models grounded in reconstructed geometry. Spring-Gaussian couples 3D Gaussian splatting with elastic simulation, and PhysTwin reconstructs deformable objects from sparse-view RGB-D videos and differentiably optimizes a spring–mass system to reproduce observed interactions. While flexible and scalable, these axial-spring formulations lack explicit modeling of more complex mechanical behaviors, such as bending-dominated deformation, which motivates our proposed method.\n\n## 5 Conclusion\n\nWe presented BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework for physics-informed reconstruction and future prediction of deformable-object dynamics from sparse-view RGB-D videos. By augmenting axial spring interactions with explicit bending stiffness and damping over local surface triplets, BendTwin penalizes deviations from rest angles and improves mechanical expressiveness while preserving the simplicity and differentiability of spring–mass models. BendTwin demonstrates competitive performance across both dense and sparse spring–mass systems. Overall, BendTwin provides an efficient and mechanically expressive foundation for deformable-object digital twins in robotic manipulation, planning, and physical interaction.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Xuanlin Li, Kyle Hsu, Jiayuan Gu, Oier Mees, Karl Pertsch, Homer Rich Walke, Chuyuan Fu, Ishikaa Lunawat, Isabel Sieh, Sean Kirmani, Sergey Levine, Jiajun Wu, Chelsea Finn, Hao Su, Quan Vuong, and Ted Xiao. Evaluating real-world robot manipulation policies in simulation. In Pulkit Agrawal, Oliver Kroemer, and Wolfram Burgard, editors, Proceedings of The 8th Conference on Robot Learning, volume 270 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, pages 3705–3728. PMLR, 06–09 Nov 2025.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we present a novel approach to robot benchmarking that leverages real-to-sim translation techniques. Our method, termed Robotarena, allows for scalable evaluation of robotic policies in simulated environments that closely mimic real-world conditions. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach through a series of experiments that highlight the advantages of using real-world data to inform simulation parameters.\n\n## Introduction\nThe gap between simulation and real-world performance remains a significant challenge in robotics. Traditional methods often rely on static simulation environments that do not account for the variability and unpredictability of real-world scenarios. Recent advancements in real-to-sim translation have opened new avenues for bridging this gap. In this work, we explore the potential of these techniques to create a scalable benchmarking framework for robotic systems.\n\n## Method\nOur approach consists of several key components:\n\n1. **Data Collection**: We gather extensive real-world data from robotic interactions in various environments.\n2. **Simulation Environment**: We develop a simulation framework that incorporates the collected data to create realistic scenarios.\n3. **Policy Evaluation**: We implement a policy evaluation mechanism that assesses the performance of robotic policies in both real and simulated environments.\n\nThe mathematical formulation of our policy evaluation can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Performance} = f(\\text{Real Data}, \\text{Simulated Data}, \\text{Policy})\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( f \\) is a function that quantifies the performance of a given policy based on the comparison of real and simulated outcomes.\n\n## Results\nWe conducted a series of experiments to validate our approach. The results indicate that policies evaluated in our Robotarena framework demonstrate improved performance when deployed in real-world scenarios compared to those evaluated solely in traditional simulation environments.\n\nFigure 1 illustrates the performance metrics of various policies across different environments, highlighting the effectiveness of our real-to-sim translation method.\n\n## Discussion\nThe findings suggest that integrating real-world data into simulation environments can significantly enhance the reliability of robotic policy evaluations. Future work will focus on refining our simulation techniques and exploring additional applications of our framework in diverse robotic tasks.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur research contributes to the ongoing efforts to close the sim-to-real gap in robotics. By leveraging real-to-sim translation, we provide a scalable and effective benchmarking solution that can adapt to various robotic applications.\n\n## References\n1. Yash Jangir et al. (2025). Robotarena: Scalable robot benchmarking via real-to-sim translation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.23571.\n2. Kaifeng Zhang et al. (2025). Real-to-Sim Robot Policy Evaluation with Gaussian Splatting Simulation of Soft-Body Interactions. arXiv e-prints, arXiv:2511.04665.\n3. Yevgen Chebotar et al. (2019). Closing the sim-to-real loop: Adapting simulation randomization with real world experience. In ICRA, pages 8973–8979. IEEE.\n4. Fabio Muratore et al. (2022). Neural posterior domain randomization. In Conference on robot learning, pages 1532–1542. PMLR.\n5. Miguel Angel Zamora Mora et al. (2021). Pods: Policy optimization via differentiable simulation. In Proceedings of the 38th International Conference on Machine Learning, pages 7805–7817. PMLR.\n6. Yanjiang Guo et al. (2025). Ctrl-world: A controllable generative world model for robot manipulation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.10125.\n7. Eftychios Sifakis and Jernej Barbic (2012). Fem simulation of 3d deformable solids: a practitioner’s guide to theory, discretization and model reduction. In ACM SIGGRAPH 2012 courses, pages 1–50.\n8. Matthias Müller et al. (2007). Position based dynamics. Journal of Visual Communication and Image Representation, 18(2):109–118.\n9. Alexey Stomakhin et al. (2013). A material point method for snow simulation. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 32(4):1–10.\n10. Bernhard Kerbl et al. (2023). 3d gaussian splatting for real-time radiance field rendering. ACM Trans. Graph., 42(4):139–1.\n11. Ben Mildenhall et al. (2021). Nerf: Representing scenes as neural radiance fields for view synthesis. Communications of the ACM, 65(1):99–106.\n12. Jianfeng Xiang et al. (2025). Structured 3d latents for scalable and versatile 3d generation. In CVPR, pages 21469–21480.\n13. Jianfeng Xiang et al. (2025). Native and compact structured latents for 3d generation. Tech report.\n14. Tianyuan Zhang et al. (2024). Physdreamer: Physics-based interaction with 3d objects via video generation. In European Conference on Computer Vision, pages 388–406.\n15. Tianyi Xie et al. (2024). Physgaussian: Physics-integrated 3d gaussians for generative dynamics. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pages 4389–4398.\n16. Yi-Ling Qiao et al. (2021). Efficient differentiable simulation of articulated bodies. In International Conference on Machine Learning, pages 8661–8671. PMLR.\n17. Yi-Ling Qiao et al. (2020). Scalable differentiable physics for learning and control. arXiv preprint arXiv:2007.02168.\n18. Tao Du et al. (2021). Diffpd: Differentiable projective dynamics. ACM Transactions on Graphics (ToG), 41(2):1–21.\n19. Hanxiao Jiang et al. (2025). Phystwin: Physics-informed reconstruction and simulation of deformable objects from videos. ICCV.\n20. Licheng Zhong et al. (2024). Reconstruction and simulation of elastic objects with spring-mass 3d gaussians. In European Conference on Computer Vision, pages 407–423.\n21. Zhou Wang et al. (2004). Image quality assessment: from error visibility to structural similarity. IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, 13(4):600–612.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel approach to dynamic scene rendering using neural radiance fields. We introduce a method that leverages the capabilities of deep learning to enhance the realism and efficiency of rendering dynamic scenes. Our approach is evaluated against existing methods, demonstrating significant improvements in both visual quality and computational performance.\n\n## Introduction\nDynamic scene rendering has become increasingly important in computer graphics and computer vision. Traditional methods often struggle with the complexities of real-time rendering, particularly in dynamic environments. Recent advancements in neural networks, particularly in the context of neural radiance fields (NeRF), have opened new avenues for improving rendering techniques. This paper aims to explore these advancements and propose a new framework that integrates deep learning with traditional rendering techniques.\n\n## Method\nOur method builds upon the foundation of neural radiance fields, extending their capabilities to handle dynamic scenes. We define the scene representation as a function \\( F: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4 \\), where \\( F \\) outputs color and density values for any given 3D point and viewing direction. The training process involves optimizing the parameters of \\( F \\) using a dataset of images captured from various viewpoints.\n\n### Training Procedure\nThe training procedure consists of the following steps:\n1. **Data Collection**: Capture a series of images from different angles and positions.\n2. **Network Architecture**: Design a neural network that can effectively learn the mapping defined by \\( F \\).\n3. **Loss Function**: Utilize a loss function that minimizes the difference between the rendered images and the ground truth images.\n\n## Results\nWe conducted extensive experiments to evaluate the performance of our proposed method. The results indicate that our approach significantly outperforms traditional methods in terms of both rendering quality and speed. For instance, our method achieved a peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) of 35.2 dB, compared to 30.1 dB for the baseline method.\n\n### Figure 1\n*Figure 1: Comparison of rendering quality between our method and traditional methods. The left image shows the output from the baseline method, while the right image displays the results from our proposed approach.*\n\n## Conclusion\nIn conclusion, our proposed method for dynamic scene rendering using neural radiance fields demonstrates substantial improvements over existing techniques. The integration of deep learning not only enhances the visual quality of rendered scenes but also improves computational efficiency. Future work will focus on further optimizing the network architecture and exploring additional applications of this approach in real-time rendering scenarios.\n\n## References\n1. Richard Zhang, Phillip Isola, Alexei A Efros, Eli Shechtman, and Oliver Wang. The unreasonable effectiveness of deep features as a perceptual metric. In Proceedings of the IEEE conference on computer vision and pattern recognition, pages 586–595, 2018.\n2. David G Lowe. Distinctive image features from scale-invariant keypoints. International journal of computer vision, 60(2):91–110, 2004.\n3. Raul Mur-Artal and Juan D Tardós. Orb-slam2: An open-source slam system for monocular, stereo, and rgb-d cameras. IEEE transactions on robotics, 33(5):1255–1262, 2017.\n4. Albert Pumarola, Enric Corona, Gerard Pons-Moll, and Francesc Moreno-Noguer. D-nerf: Neural radiance fields for dynamic scenes. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF conference on computer vision and pattern recognition, pages 10318–10327, 2021.\n5. Keunhong Park, Utkarsh Sinha, Jonathan T Barron, Sofien Bouaziz, Dan B Goldman, Steven M Seitz, and Ricardo Martin-Brualla. Nerfies: Deformable neural radiance fields. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF international conference on computer vision, pages 5865–5874, 2021.\n6. Guanjun Wu, Taoran Yi, Jiemin Fang, Lingxi Xie, Xiaopeng Zhang, Wei Wei, Wenyu Liu, Qi Tian, and Xinggang Wang. 4d gaussian splatting for real-time dynamic scene rendering. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), pages 20310–20320, June 2024.\n7. Youtian Lin, Zuozhuo Dai, Siyu Zhu, and Yao Yao. Gaussian-flow: 4d reconstruction with dynamic 3d gaussian particle. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pages 21136–21145, 2024.\n8. Miloš Zefran and Francesco Bullo. Lagrangian dynamics. Robotics and Automation Handbook, pages 5–1, 2005.\n9. Marco Cusumano-Towner, Arjun Singh, Stephen Miller, James F. O’Brien, and Pieter Abbeel. Bringing clothing into desired configurations with limited perception. In 2011 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation, pages 3893–3900, 2011.\n10. Chenfanfu Jiang, Craig Schroeder, Joseph Teran, Alexey Stomakhin, and Andrew Selle. The material point method for simulating continuum materials. In ACM SIGGRAPH 2016 Courses, SIGGRAPH ’16, New York, NY, USA, 2016. Association for Computing Machinery.\n11. Yuanming Hu, Yu Fang, Ziheng Ge, Ziyin Qu, Yixin Zhu, Andre Pradhana, and Chenfanfu Jiang. A moving least squares material point method with displacement discontinuity and two-way rigid body coupling. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 37(4):1–14, 2018.\n12. Miles Macklin, Matthias Müller, and Nuttapong Chentanez. Xpbd: position-based simulation of compliant constrained dynamics. In Proceedings of the 9th International Conference on Motion in Games, pages 49–54, 2016.\n13. Filipe de A. Belbute-Peres, Kevin A. Smith, Kelsey R. Allen, Joshua B. Tenenbaum, and J. Zico Kolter. End-to-end differentiable physics for learning and control. In Proceedings of the 32nd International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, NIPS’18, page 7178–7189, Red Hook, NY, USA, 2018. Curran Associates Inc.\n14. Jonas Degrave, Michiel Hermans, Joni Dambre, et al. A differentiable physics engine for deep learning in robotics. Frontiers in neurorobotics, 13:406386, 2019.\n15. Miles Macklin, Kenny Erleben, Matthias Müller, Nuttapong Chentanez, Stefan Jeschke, and Tae-Yong Kim. Primal/dual descent methods for dynamics. In Computer Graphics Forum, volume 39, pages 89–100. Wiley Online Library, 2020.\n16. Marc Toussaint, Kelsey R. Allen, Kevin A. Smith, and Joshua B. Tenenbaum. Differentiable physics and stable modes for tool-use and manipulation planning - extended abstract. In Proceedings of the Twenty-Eighth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence, IJCAI-19, pages 6231–6235. International Joint Conferences on Artificial Intelligence Organization, 7 2019.\n17. Moritz Geilinger, David Hahn, Jonas Zehnder, Moritz Bächer, Bernhard Thomaszewski, and Stelian Coros. Add: Analytically differentiable dynamics for multi-body systems with frictional contact. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 39(6):1–15, 2020.\n18. David Hahn, Pol Banzet, James M Bern, and Stelian Coros. Real2sim: Visco-elastic parameter estimation from dynamic motion. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 38(6):1–13, 2019.\n19. Yuanming Hu, Jiancheng Liu, Andrew Spielberg, Joshua B Tenenbaum, William T Freeman, Jiajun Wu, Daniela Rus, and Wojciech Matusik. Chainqueen: A real-time differentiable physical simulator for soft robotics. In 2019 International conference on robotics and automation (ICRA), pages 6265–6271. IEEE, 2019.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel approach to modeling and simulating physical systems using neural networks. We introduce a framework that integrates physics-based principles with deep learning techniques to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of simulations. Our method leverages differentiable programming to enable gradient-based optimization, allowing for real-time control and adaptation of physical simulations.\n\n## Introduction\nThe intersection of machine learning and physics has garnered significant attention in recent years, particularly in the context of simulating complex physical phenomena. Traditional simulation methods often struggle with high-dimensional data and intricate interactions, leading to computational inefficiencies. In this work, we propose a new framework that combines the strengths of neural networks with established physics principles to address these challenges.\n\n## Method\nOur approach is built upon a differentiable programming paradigm, which allows us to formulate physical laws as differentiable functions. This enables the use of gradient descent methods for optimization. The core of our framework consists of a neural network that learns to approximate the dynamics of a physical system. We define the loss function as follows:\n\n\\[\nL = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\left( y_i - f(x_i; \\theta) \\right)^2 + \\lambda R(\\theta)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_i\\) are the observed outputs, \\(f(x_i; \\theta)\\) is the neural network prediction, and \\(R(\\theta)\\) is a regularization term.\n\n## Results\nWe evaluated our framework on several benchmark problems, including cloth simulation and rigid body dynamics. The results demonstrate that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance in terms of both accuracy and computational efficiency. For instance, in the cloth simulation task, our approach reduced the simulation time by 50% compared to traditional methods while maintaining high fidelity.\n\n## Discussion\nThe integration of physics-based principles with neural networks opens new avenues for research in simulation and control. Our findings suggest that this hybrid approach can significantly enhance the performance of existing simulation techniques. Future work will focus on expanding the applicability of our framework to more complex systems and exploring its potential in real-time applications.\n\n## Conclusion\nIn conclusion, we have presented a novel framework that combines neural networks with physics-based modeling to improve the simulation of physical systems. Our results indicate that this approach not only enhances accuracy but also significantly reduces computational costs. We believe that this work lays the groundwork for future advancements in the field of physics-informed machine learning.\n\n## References\n[42] Yuanming Hu, Tzu-Mao Li, Luke Anderson, Jonathan Ragan-Kelley, and Frédo Durand. Taichi: a language for high-performance computation on spatially sparse data structures. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 38(6):201, 2019.\n\n[43] Yi-Ling Qiao, Junbang Liang, Vladlen Koltun, and Ming C. Lin. Scalable differentiable physics for learning and control. In Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Machine Learning, ICML’20. JMLR.org, 2020.\n\n[44] Junbang Liang, Ming Lin, and Vladlen Koltun. Differentiable cloth simulation for inverse problems. Advances in neural information processing systems, 32, 2019.\n\n[45] Qizhen Ying, Guangming Wang, Yangchen Pan, Victor Adrian Prisacariu, Brian Sheil, and Yixiong Jing. NEXUS: Neural Energy Fields for Physically Consistent Contact-Rich 3D Object Dynamics. arXiv e-prints, page arXiv:2606.15015, June 2026.\n\n[46] Yutao Feng, Yintong Shang, Xuan Li, Tianjia Shao, Chenfanfu Jiang, and Yin Yang. Pie-nerf: Physics-based interactive elastodynamics with nerf. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pages 4450–4461, 2024.\n\n[47] Tianyi Xie, Yiwei Zhao, Ying Jiang, and Chenfanfu Jiang. Physanimator: Physics-guided generative cartoon animation. In Proceedings of the Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Conference, pages 10793–10804, 2025.\n\n[48] Xuan Li, Yi-Ling Qiao, Peter Yichen Chen, Krishna Murthy Jatavallabhula, Ming Lin, Chenfanfu Jiang, and Chuang Gan. PAC-neRF: Physics augmented continuum neural radiance fields for geometry-agnostic system identification. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n\n[49] Zhiyuan Gao, Jiageng Mao, Hong-Xing Yu, Haozhe Lou, Emily Yue ting Jia, Jernej Barbic, Jiajun Wu, and Yue Wang. Seeing the wind from a falling leaf. In The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, 2025.\n\n[50] Yixiong Jing, Xingyuan Chen, Guangming Wang, Olaf Wysocki, Haibing Wu, and Brian Sheil. PhySPRING: Structure-Preserving Reduction of Physics-Informed Twins via GNN. arXiv e-prints, page arXiv:2605.07687, May 2026.\n\n\n\n# A Derivation of Bending Forces and Bending Damping\n\nIn this section, we provide the derivations for the bending restoring forces and the bending damping forces induced by a bending triplet \\((i, j, k)\\). Following the main paper, we adopt the compact notation\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}} = -k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) d(i,j,k)\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}} = -\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) \\hat{d}(i,j,k),\n\\]\n\nwhere the explicit expressions of \\(\\hat{d}_i\\) and \\(\\hat{d}_k\\) are derived below.\n\n## A.1 Notation\n\nFor a bending triplet \\((i, j, k)\\) with center node \\(j\\), define\n\n\\[\nu = x_i - x_j, \\quad v = x_k - x_j, \\quad \\ell_u = \\|u\\|, \\quad \\ell_v = \\|v\\|, \\quad \\hat{u} = \\frac{u}{\\ell_u}, \\quad \\hat{v} = \\frac{v}{\\ell_v}\n\\]\n\nThe included angle is computed from\n\n\\[\nc = \\hat{u}^T \\hat{v}, \\quad \\theta = \\arccos(c), \\quad s = \\sqrt{1 - c^2} = \\sin(\\theta).\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\theta^0\\) denotes the rest angle in the canonical configuration. The bending energy is given by\n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k) = \\frac{1}{2} k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0)^2,\n\\]\n\nwith force convention \\(f = -\\nabla_x E_{\\text{bend}}\\).\n\n## A.2 Derivation of Bending Forces\n\nDifferentiating Eq. (13) gives\n\n\\[\n\\nabla_x E_{\\text{bend}} = k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\nabla_x \\theta = -k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\nabla_x c,\n\\]\n\nwhere we used \\(\\nabla_x \\theta = -\\frac{1}{s} \\nabla_x c\\). A standard calculation yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial u} = \\frac{1}{\\ell_u} \\hat{v} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\hat{u}}{\\partial u}, \\quad \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v} = \\frac{1}{\\ell_v} \\hat{u} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\hat{v}}{\\partial v},\n\\]\n\nand since \\(u = x_i - x_j\\) and \\(v = x_k - x_j\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x_i} = \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial u}, \\quad \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x_k} = \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}, \\quad \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x_j} = -\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial u} - \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}.\n\\]\n\nCombining the above with \\(f = -\\nabla_x E_{\\text{bend}}\\) yields the nodal forces\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}}_i = k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\frac{1}{\\ell_u s} \\hat{v},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}}_k = k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\frac{1}{\\ell_v s} \\hat{u},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}}_j = - (f^{\\text{bend}}_i + f^{\\text{bend}}_k).\n\\]\n\nAccordingly, the directional vectors used in the main paper can be identified as\n\n\\[\nd(i,j,k) = \\frac{1}{\\ell_v s} \\hat{v}, \\quad d_i = \\frac{1}{\\ell_u s} \\hat{u}, \\quad d_k = \\frac{1}{\\ell_v s} \\hat{v}.\n\\]\n\n## A.3 Bending Damping\n\nWe model bending damping as a viscous torque proportional to the angular rate:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = -c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k).\n\\]\n\nTo compute \\(\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k)\\), define the unit normal and tangential directions\n\n\\[\n\\hat{n} = \\frac{u \\times \\hat{v}}{\\|u \\times \\hat{v}\\|}, \\quad \\hat{t} = \\hat{n} \\times \\hat{u}.\n\\]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n\nLet node velocities be \\( v_i, v_j, v_k \\). A consistent first-order approximation is given by\n\n\\[\n\\theta(i,j,k) = \\frac{u \\cdot t^\\top (v_i - v_j) \\cdot t^\\top (v_k - v_j)}{\\ell_u \\cdot \\ell_v} \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nThe derivation of the equivalent nodal damping forces follows the same steps as Equation 15, therefore taking the same geometric directions as the elastic bending forces:\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}}_i = c_b \\theta(i,j,k) d_i \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}}_k = c_b \\theta(i,j,k) d_k \\tag{25}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}}_j = - (f^{\\text{rot}}_i + f^{\\text{rot}}_k) \\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( d(i,j,k) \\) and \\( d(i,j,k) \\) are given in Eq. (20).\n\n## Results\n\nNumerical considerations indicate that when \\( \\theta \\approx 0 \\) or \\( \\theta \\approx \\pi \\), \\( s = \\sin \\theta \\) becomes small. We clip \\( c \\) to \\([-1 + \\epsilon, 1 - \\epsilon]\\) and use\n\n\\[\ns = \\sqrt{\\max(1 - c^2, \\epsilon)} \\| \\tilde{u} \\times \\tilde{v} \\|\n\\]\n\nIf \\( s \\) is below a threshold, we skip the damping term for that triplet.\n\n## Conclusion\n\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n\n[References content goes here.]	[]	[]	44774	2026-08-18 02:00:55.932079+00	2026-08-18 02:00:55.932079+00
9	1	llamaparse		[]	[]	288	2026-08-18 02:00:56.231939+00	2026-08-18 02:00:56.231939+00
10	2	llamaparse	# The Bitter Lesson of Tool Calling\n\nIshan Patel, Sahil Sen, Elias Lumer, and Vamse Kumar Subbiah\nCommercial Technology and Innovation Office\nPricewaterhouseCoopers, U.S.A\n\n## Abstract\n\nTool use transforms LLMs into agents that act beyond their training data, and for code-capable models, programmatic tool calling extends this further by replacing rigid JSON calls with scripts that chain and parallelize naturally. However, a systematic evaluation of tools as code on an established benchmark across current and prior model generations under real-world task conditions has not been conducted. In this work, we empirically compare programmatic tool calling (PTC) to native JSON tool calling across 14 language models on BFCL v4. In the programmatic tool calling paradigm, tools are exposed as typed Python stubs that the model invokes through code, with execution and results handled in a single agent turn. Programmatic tool calling matches or exceeds native JSON tool calling in 11 of 14 models on BFCL v4, with the GPT-5.6 family achieving a 10.6% improvement over the JSON tool calling baseline. Further, it matches or outperforms the baseline in 13 of 14 models under parallel fan-out and holds stable under context rot conditions where the baseline degrades 2.3% on average. Our results demonstrate that programmatic tool calling is a viable and robust alternative to JSON tool calling, with performance tracking model capability across release generations. We run three ablation studies targeting sequential chaining, parallel fan-out, and context rot, the task structures where the limitations of JSON tool calling have been most commonly claimed.\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nLarge language models (LLMs) now act as tool-calling agents, invoking external services through APIs that require the model to emit a structured JSON object at each function call (Anthropic, 2024; Guo et al., 2026). For models that can already write executable code, this format is a design choice, not a necessity. Prior work has established a theoretical case for replacing JSON tool calls with executable code. CodeAct (Wang et al., 2024) showed that code actions achieve up to 20% higher task success with 30% fewer interaction turns on multi-tool tasks, with gains concentrated in parallel and compositional scenarios. A more recent study found that code-only output restrictions change pass rates by an absolute change of fewer than 3% on coding-agent tasks (Yang et al., 2026). Function calling is a harder test case. It requires precise argument serialization, multi-step chaining, and fan-out across heterogeneous APIs, constraints absent from coding-agent evaluations and exactly where paradigm choice has the largest claimed effect. Whether programmatic tool calling matches JSON tool calling under these conditions, across model families and adversarial context loads, remains untested.\n\nWe find that 11 of 14 models match or exceed baseline accuracy under programmatic tool calling on BFCL v4, with results dividing along model generation lines. All five Anthropic models and the three newest GPT generations match or exceed baseline accuracy, while three older GPT models do not. On context flooding, programmatic tool calling sees an absolute improvement of 5.5% on average, compared to an absolute 32% degradation for the filesystem-discovery comparison approach. Our contributions are:\n\n1. A systematic evaluation of programmatic tool calling against JSON tool calling across multiple model generations.\n2. Empirical evidence demonstrating the advantages of programmatic tool calling in real-world task conditions.\n3. Insights from ablation studies that highlight the strengths and weaknesses of both paradigms.\n\n## 2 Method\n\n[Details of the method would be included here, including experimental setup, data collection, and analysis techniques.]\n\n## 3 Results\n\n[Results would be presented here, including statistical analyses, comparisons, and any relevant figures or tables.]\n\n## 4 Discussion\n\n[Discussion of the implications of the results, limitations of the study, and suggestions for future research would be included here.]\n\n## 5 Conclusion\n\n[Concluding remarks summarizing the findings and their significance would be included here.]\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the efficacy of programmatic tool calling compared to traditional JSON tool calling in large language models (LLMs). We demonstrate that programmatic tool calling not only matches but exceeds the accuracy of JSON tool calling, particularly in complex sequential tasks. Our findings reveal significant advantages in execution efficiency and robustness under adversarial conditions, suggesting a paradigm shift in how LLMs can effectively utilize tools.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized the way we approach problem-solving in artificial intelligence. Recent advancements have highlighted the potential of programmatic tool calling as a superior alternative to JSON tool calling. This paper aims to explore the viability of programmatic tool calling, particularly in the context of its accuracy and efficiency in executing tasks across various model families.\n\n# Method\nWe study whether programmatic tool calling can replace JSON tool calling for LLM tool use without sacrificing accuracy. We evaluate two paradigms on BFCL v4: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. In each paradigm, the model receives the same task description and must invoke the correct functions with the correct arguments. Only the mechanism for expressing and executing tool calls differs. In the context rot ablation, we additionally include a filesystem-based condition as an internal reference point.\n\n# Results\nOur results indicate that programmatic tool calling maintains 100% enumeration accuracy at N = 100, while JSON tool calling drops tool calls entirely above a model-specific fan-out threshold (N = 70–72 for Claude Sonnet 5). Furthermore, programmatic tool calling shows a significant accuracy advantage on sequential tasks, with an 18.8% absolute gap over JSON tool calling at lengths ≥ 12. Under context flooding, programmatic tool calling remains stable, while JSON tool calling degrades by 2.3% on average.\n\n# Related Work\nTwo lines of work motivate replacing JSON tool calls with executable code. The first is code-action agents. CodeAct (Wang et al., 2024) demonstrated that code actions outperform JSON alternatives on multi-tool tasks, particularly in parallel and compositional scenarios. Recursive Agent Harnesses (Lumer et al., 2026) extend this finding, showing that executing code in a subprocess bypasses the per-turn tool-call cap that constrains native JSON function calling at high fan-out.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings of this study suggest that programmatic tool calling offers a robust and efficient alternative to JSON tool calling in LLMs. The advantages observed in accuracy and stability under various conditions indicate that adopting programmatic tool calling could significantly enhance the performance of agentic systems. Future work should focus on further refining these methods and exploring their implications in real-world applications.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper evaluates two paradigms for tool calling in language models: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. We analyze their performance based on a benchmark function catalog, focusing on the execution mechanisms and the implications for model accuracy and efficiency. The results indicate that while both paradigms achieve similar accuracy, the programmatic approach offers advantages in terms of flexibility and execution control.\n\n# Introduction\nThe integration of external tools into language models has become increasingly important for enhancing their capabilities. This research investigates two primary paradigms for tool calling: JSON tool calling, which utilizes structured JSON objects, and programmatic tool calling, which involves writing and executing Python scripts. We aim to understand the differences in execution mechanisms and their impact on model performance.\n\n# Method\n## Task Definition\nEach benchmark entry specifies a natural-language user query \\( q \\), a set of available functions \\( F = \\{f_1, \\ldots, f_k\\} \\) each with a typed signature, and a ground-truth set of function calls \\( C^* = \\{(f_i, a_i)\\} \\) where \\( a_i \\) is the argument mapping for call \\( i \\). A model is deemed correct on an entry if its output produces a set of calls that matches \\( C^* \\) under the benchmark’s normalized string comparison, which strips punctuation and lowercases all values before matching. This definition is consistent across all paradigms, with differences arising in prompting and output parsing.\n\n## Paradigms\n### JSON Tool Calling\nIn this paradigm, the model receives a system prompt containing function schemas formatted as JSON tool definitions and an API call that invokes the tool-calling endpoint. The model outputs a structured JSON object at each function call, which is the standard deployment pattern for tool-augmented LLMs.\n\n### Programmatic Tool Calling\nThe model receives a system prompt that embeds the source of a typed Python module whose functions correspond one-to-one with the benchmark’s function schemas. Each stub function captures its arguments and returns them as a structured dict. The model writes a Python script that imports this module and calls the appropriate functions. The agent loop executes the script in a native shell subprocess, capturing stdout, from which the scorer extracts function names and argument values.\n\n# Results\nThe evaluation of both paradigms reveals that they consume the same number of LLM calls per entry, making accuracy comparisons straightforward. For instance, consider a task that asks the model to retrieve the circumference of a circle with radius 7 and the area of a square with side 5. In JSON tool calling, the model emits two sequential JSON tool-call objects. In programmatic tool calling, the model writes a Python script that executes the necessary calculations.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the strengths and weaknesses of JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. While both paradigms achieve similar accuracy, the programmatic approach provides greater flexibility and control over execution, which may be beneficial in more complex scenarios. Future work will explore the scalability of these paradigms in larger and more diverse benchmarks.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 1: Overview of the two primary paradigms evaluated. In JSON tool calling, the model emits JSON tool-call objects via the API. In programmatic tool calling, it writes a Python script using typed stubs; the agent loop executes it in a subprocess. A filesystem-discovery condition is included as a secondary reference point.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of different function calling paradigms in programming language models, specifically focusing on JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. We analyze the accuracy of these paradigms in various task structures, including chaining and parallelism, using a benchmark dataset (BFCL v4) that encompasses a wide range of function call scenarios.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in programming language models have led to the exploration of various paradigms for function calling. This study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of JSON tool calling versus programmatic tool calling in terms of accuracy and efficiency. We utilize the BFCL v4 benchmark, which includes a diverse set of tasks, to assess the performance of these paradigms under different conditions.\n\n# Method\n## Benchmark and Evaluation\nBFCL v4 (Vaghasiya et al., 2026) provides 309 representative entries drawn from eight task categories: simple_python, multiple, parallel, parallel_multiple, live_simple, live_multiple, live_parallel, and live_parallel_multiple. Entries were sampled proportionally to mirror the category distribution of the full BFCL v4 benchmark, with per-category minimums applied to ensure sufficient entries in smaller categories. The complete entry ID list is released with the evaluation harness.\n\n### Chaining\nSequential multi-hop function calls, where the output of \\( f_1 \\) must be computed and passed as an argument to \\( f_2 \\). In JSON tool calling, this requires two model turns. The model calls \\( f_1 \\), receives the return value, then calls \\( f_2 \\). In programmatic tool calling, both calls appear in a single script. The model computes the intermediate value using parametric knowledge and passes it directly. The chaining ablation contains 52 entries with chain lengths of 2 to 20 calls.\n\n### Parallelism\nIndependent fan-out function calls, where \\( N \\) calls must be issued in a single step. JSON tool calling issues them as parallel tool-call objects. Programmatic tool calling writes an `asyncio.gather` block or a sequential loop. The parallelism ablation uses 32 enumeration-type entries with fan-out counts ranging from 7 to 48, plus targeted probe entries at \\( N \\in \\{60, 70, 72, 75, 100\\} \\) for Claude Sonnet 5 to locate the fan-out threshold at which JSON tool calling begins dropping calls.\n\n## Ablation Design\nThe three ablation studies target task structures where paradigm choice has been claimed to matter. Each ablation subset was constructed by selecting entries from BFCL v4 that represent the target task structure without duplicating main-evaluation entries. The complete entry ID lists are released with the evaluation harness. The chaining subset (n = 52) was optimized to cover the full range of chain lengths (\\( n_{\\text{chain}} = 2–20 \\)), with the distribution weighted toward longer chains where JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling diverge.\n\n### Context Rot\nThe flood condition injects decoy function schemas into the context alongside the entry’s relevant schemas, increasing total context size to 128 schemas. The two conditions are filtered (only the functions the query uses) and flood (128 total schemas, including the relevant functions plus corpus decoys drawn from unrelated domains).\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that programmatic tool calling outperforms JSON tool calling in terms of accuracy across various task structures. The chaining and parallelism tasks demonstrated significant differences in performance, particularly in scenarios with higher complexity and longer chain lengths. The evaluation metrics reveal that programmatic tool calling maintains a higher accuracy rate, especially in enumeration-type entries.\n\nIn summary, our findings suggest that the choice of function calling paradigm significantly impacts the performance of programming language models, with programmatic tool calling providing a more efficient and accurate approach in complex scenarios. Further research is needed to explore the implications of these findings in real-world applications and to refine the benchmarks used for evaluation.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper evaluates the performance of 14 models from two families over a span of 20 months, focusing on their accuracy in programmatic tool calling and JSON tool calling. The results indicate significant variances in performance across models, with specific attention to the impact of model generation and training data on accuracy.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in AI models have led to a variety of architectures and training methodologies. This study aims to assess the performance of these models in specific tasks, particularly in the context of tool calling. We analyze the accuracy of different models and identify patterns that may inform future developments in AI.\n\n# Method\nWe evaluated 14 models, as listed in Table 1, which spans releases from November 2024 to July 2026. All models were run at a temperature of 0 to ensure consistency in output. The evaluation focused on two types of tool calling: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. The accuracy was measured as the fraction of entries where every required function call was both present and correctly parameterized.\n\n# Results\n## 3.5 Models\nThe models evaluated are summarized in Table 1. All tables report 95% Wilson confidence intervals per row. Due to the small size of ablation entries (n = 31–52), the intervals are wide, and individual model results should be interpreted as directional rather than conclusive. Notably, programmatic tool calling underperformed JSON tool calling by an average of 14.1% in the parallel and parallel_multiple categories, while showing a 10.0% improvement in live_multiple.\n\n## 4 Experiments\nWe conducted experiments on BFCL v4 and three ablation studies (chaining, parallelism, and context rot). All experiments were run at temperature 0. The results indicate that all five Anthropic models (Claude Haiku 4.5 through Sonnet 5) matched or exceeded baseline accuracy under programmatic tool calling, with deltas ranging from 0.0 to 6.5 percentage points.\n\n### 4.1 BFCL v4 Main Evaluation\nTable 2 presents the accuracy for JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling across all 14 models on BFCL v4. The GPT-5.6 family achieved the largest gains in programmatic tool calling, with GPT-5.6-Sol and GPT-5.6-Terra each showing an absolute improvement of 10.6% over their JSON tool calling baseline. In contrast, older models such as GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, and GPT-5.4-mini fell below baseline by 19.7% to 26.9%. The failure mode for these models was consistent, producing code with literal \\n escape sequences in multiline scripts, leading to syntax errors.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe evaluation of the 14 models reveals significant differences in performance, particularly in the context of tool calling. The findings suggest that advancements in model architecture and training data can lead to substantial improvements in accuracy. Future work should focus on addressing the identified failure modes and exploring the implications of these results for the development of more robust AI systems.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of various models in the context of chaining and parallelism ablation tasks. We analyze the accuracy of different models when utilizing JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling, focusing on enumeration and aggregation accuracy.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in AI models have led to significant improvements in their ability to perform complex tasks. This study aims to evaluate the performance of several models, specifically in the context of chaining and parallelism tasks, using two different calling methods: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments with a range of models, measuring their accuracy in chaining and parallelism ablation tasks. The models were evaluated based on their ability to issue calls and produce correct aggregate answers. The sample sizes for the chaining and parallelism tasks were 52 and 32, respectively.\n\n# Results\n## 4.2 Chaining Ablation\nIn the chaining ablation (n = 52 entries, chain lengths 2–20), programmatic tool calling handles sequential multi-hop calls differently from JSON tool calling. In JSON tool calling, the model issues \\( f_1 \\), receives its return value, then issues \\( f_2 \\) in a second inference turn. In programmatic tool calling, the model writes both calls in a single script and computes the intermediate value from parametric knowledge before passing it to \\( f_2 \\).\n\nTable 3 shows per-model results. Claude Sonnet 5 achieves the largest programmatic tool calling gain (80.8% → 96.2%), followed by Claude Opus 4.8 (80.8% → 94.2%), while six models maintain near-parity (within 5% absolute). GPT-4.1 is the sole outlier, with its baseline accuracy of 98.1% collapsing to 40.4% under programmatic tool calling, driven by the \\n encoding failure described above.\n\n## 4.3 Parallelism Ablation\nProgrammatic tool calling matches or exceeds baseline accuracy for 13 of 14 models on the parallelism ablation (n = 32 entries, fan-out counts of 7 to 48). Table 4 separates the results for clarity. Token costs cross over at \\( N \\approx 26 \\). Below this threshold, programmatic tool calling is more expensive due to its fixed system-prompt overhead.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe results indicate that programmatic tool calling generally provides superior performance in both chaining and parallelism tasks compared to JSON tool calling. However, certain models, particularly GPT-4.1, exhibit significant performance degradation due to encoding issues. Future work should focus on addressing these limitations to enhance model reliability in complex task execution.\n\n# Tables\n**Table 3:** Chaining ablation accuracy (%, n = 52, chain lengths 2–20). JSON=JSON tool calling; PTC = programmatic tool calling. ± columns show 95% Wilson CI half-widths. Bold indicates the higher value per row (ties bolded on both).\n\n**Table 4:** Parallelism ablation accuracy (%, n = 32). JSON=JSON tool calling; PTC = programmatic tool calling. Enumeration and overall accuracy are identical for all models here; aggregation accuracy is discussed separately in the text. ± columns show 95% Wilson CI half-widths. Bold indicates the higher value per row (ties bolded on both).\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of various language models under different conditions of context load, specifically focusing on programmatic tool calling versus JSON tool calling. We analyze the accuracy of these models in generating code and handling complex tasks, particularly under flooding conditions where the context is overloaded with irrelevant information.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in language models have led to significant improvements in their ability to generate code and perform complex tasks. However, the impact of context load on their performance remains an area of active research. This study aims to explore how different models handle varying amounts of relevant and irrelevant context, particularly in the context of tool calling.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments using multiple language models, including Anthropic and OpenAI variants. Each model was evaluated under two conditions: filtered (only relevant function schemas) and flood (128 total schemas, including irrelevant corpus decoys). The accuracy of each model was measured in terms of its ability to generate correct code for programmatic tool calling and JSON tool calling.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that programmatic tool calling accuracy relative to JSON tool calling tracks model generation rather than model family. All five Anthropic models matched or exceeded baseline performance across all three ablation studies. Notably, the three newest GPT-5.6 variants also performed well. However, models that fell below baseline under programmatic tool calling (GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, and GPT-5.4-mini) exhibited a specific failure in generating Python code, producing incorrect newline sequences that led to syntax errors.\n\n## 5 Analysis\n### 5.1 Model Generation Predicts Programmatic Tool Calling Viability\nThe clearest pattern in the results is that programmatic tool calling accuracy relative to JSON tool calling tracks model generation, not model family. The models that fell below baseline under programmatic tool calling produced Python code with `\\n` character sequences instead of real newlines, causing syntax errors in multiline scripts.\n\n### 5.2 Programmatic Tool Calling Handles Fan-Out Structurally; JSON Tool Calling Does Not\nProgrammatic tool calling achieved near-ceiling accuracy for GPT-5 on the parallelism ablation, improving from 71.9% to 96.9%. In contrast, JSON tool calling accuracy degraded above fan-out counts of 13. This is because JSON tool calling requires the model to emit a parallel tool-call block in a single response, leading to omissions at high fan-out. In contrast, programmatic tool calling allows for loops or sequences of function calls in Python, which imposes no structural limit on count and benefits from the model’s code-generation training.\n\n### 4.4 Context Rot Ablation\nThe context rot ablation compared each paradigm under two context loads: filtered and flood. Table 5 reports accuracy under both conditions. JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling were both stable under flooding, with a mean accuracy change of −2.3% for JSON tool calling and +5.5% for programmatic tool calling. The improvement in programmatic tool calling under flood conditions was driven by models that struggled with the strict type constraints of the filtered condition.\n\nFigure 2 illustrates both trends side by side, highlighting the differences in performance between the two tool calling paradigms under varying context loads.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study demonstrates that programmatic tool calling is more resilient to context overload compared to JSON tool calling, particularly in the presence of irrelevant information. The findings suggest that model generation capabilities play a crucial role in determining the effectiveness of tool calling strategies, with implications for future research and development in language model applications.\n\n\n\n# Anthropic models: JSON tool calling vs. inline execution\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance differences between JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling (PTC) across various language models, specifically focusing on the accuracy and latency of these methods. We present a systematic evaluation of 14 language models on the BFCL v4 benchmark, revealing that PTC often matches or exceeds the performance of JSON tool calling, particularly in terms of enumeration accuracy and latency on chaining tasks.\n\n## Introduction\nAs language model agents increasingly rely on tools to extend their capabilities beyond training data, the choice of tool interface—structured JSON calls versus programmatic tool calling—has significant implications for performance. This study aims to quantify the differences in accuracy and latency between these two methods across multiple model generations.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted experiments using 14 language models, evaluating their performance on the BFCL v4 benchmark. The models were assessed based on their enumeration accuracy and latency when using JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. The enumeration accuracy was measured to determine whether the models invoked the tools correctly, while latency was measured in wall-clock time for chaining tasks.\n\n## Results\n### Accuracy\nFigure 2 illustrates the accuracy (%) on the BFCL v4 subset for JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling (PTC) by model generation. The results indicate that OpenAI models required several generations to close the accuracy gap, while Anthropic models demonstrated consistent PTC parity from the outset.\n\n- **OpenAI Models**: The performance of OpenAI models, such as GPT-4o and GPT-5-nano, shows a gradual improvement in accuracy, with PTC starting 26.9% below JSON tool calling for GPT-4o and crossing the baseline at GPT-5-nano.\n- **Anthropic Models**: In contrast, Anthropic models consistently matched or exceeded JSON tool calling across all five models, with no generation-level gap.\n\n### Latency\nProgrammatic tool calling was found to complete chaining entries in approximately half the wall-clock time of the baseline for 13 of 14 models. The per-entry latency ratios ranged from 0.32 to 0.96 of the baseline. Notably, the GPT-5.6 family achieved a 10.7% improvement over the JSON tool calling baseline.\n\n### Enumeration Accuracy\nThe enumeration vs. aggregation split revealed a programmatic tool calling behavior that diverged from the intended design. Several models produced the correct aggregation answer by leveraging parametric world knowledge rather than executing the enumeration calls. We report enumeration accuracy as the primary metric because it measures whether the model actually invoked the tools, not just whether the final answer was correct.\n\nTo quantify the fan-out count at which this structural advantage emerges for Anthropic models, we probed Claude Sonnet 5 baseline at \\( N \\in \\{60, 70, 72, 75, 100\\} \\). Enumeration accuracy was 100% at \\( N \\leq 70 \\), dropped to 75% at \\( N = 72 \\), and reached 0% at \\( N = 100 \\), indicating that the degradation onset occurs between \\( N = 70 \\) and \\( N = 72 \\). In contrast, programmatic tool calling maintained 100% enumeration accuracy at both \\( N = 72 \\) and \\( N = 100 \\).\n\n## Conclusion\nThe choice of tool interface has practical consequences for chaining, parallelism, and robustness under real-world conditions. Our findings suggest that programmatic tool calling is a viable and reliable alternative to JSON tool calling, with significant improvements in both enumeration accuracy and latency for many models. The remaining gap in performance indicates that further research is needed to explore the systematic advantages of programmatic tool calling across different model generations and real-world task conditions.\n\n\n\n# Limitations\n\nFour limitations bound the claims in this paper. First, BFCL v4 uses echo-return stubs. Each function returns its arguments verbatim rather than executing real API calls. This means we measure argument serialization accuracy, not end-to-end tool-use correctness. Results may not transfer to settings where return values affect downstream calls.\n\nSecond, the ablation entry counts are small (n = 31–52 per condition), so individual model results carry wide confidence intervals and should be read as directional. Only the aggregate cross-model patterns (11 of 14 on BFCL v4, consistent programmatic tool calling improvement under flood) are large enough to interpret reliably.\n\nThird, a recent audit found 20% evaluator-human misalignment in BFCL v4’s LLM-judge evaluation mode (Vaghasiya et al., 2026). Our deterministic scorer avoids this path, but the benchmark’s ground-truth labels may still contain noise that our scorer inherits.\n\nFourth, programmatic tool calling carries a fixed input-token overhead relative to JSON tool calling: the system prompt embeds the full instruction template as prose, whereas JSON tool calling passes function schemas through the API tools parameter, which some providers count separately from conversation input tokens. On the chaining ablation, programmatic tool calling uses 1.5× the input tokens of JSON tool calling. This overhead reverses at high fan-out, as discussed in Section 4.3. Output token counts do not differ across paradigms.\n\n# References\n\nTamer Alkhouli, Katerina Margatina, James Gung, Raphael Shu, Claudia Zaghi, Monica Sunkara, and Yi Zhang. 2025. CONFETTI: Conversational function-calling evaluation through turn-level interactions. In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, ACL 2025, pages 7993–8006.\n\nAnthropic. 2024. Building effective agents. https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents.\n\nKinjal Basu, Ibrahim Abdelaziz, Subhajit Chaudhury, Soham Dan, Maxwell Crouse, Asim Munawar, Sadhana Kumaravel, Vinod Muthusamy, Pavan Kapanipathi, and Luis A. Lastras. 2024. API-BLEND: A comprehensive corpora for training and benchmarking API LLMs. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, ACL 2024, pages 9510–9529.\n\nZehui Chen, Weihua Du, Wenwei Zhang, Kuikun Liu, Jiangning Liu, Miao Zheng, Jingming Zhuo, Songyang Zhang, Dahua Lin, Kai Chen, and Feng Zhao. 2024. T-Eval: Evaluating the tool utilization capability of large language models step by step. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, ACL 2024, pages 9510–9529.\n\nCloudflare. 2026. Code mode. https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/tools/codemode/.\n\nCloudflare Agents documentation. Last updated June 24, 2026.\n\nYaxin Du, Yifan Zhou, Yujie Ge, Jiajun Wang, Xianghe Pang, Shuo Tang, Tuney Zheng, Bryan Dai, Jian Yang, and Siheng Chen. 2026. HyperTool: Beyond step-wise tool calls for tool-augmented agents. arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.13663.\n\nDongxin Guo, Jikun Wu, and Siu Ming Yiu. 2026. The deterministic horizon: When extended reasoning fails and tool delegation becomes necessary. In Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, ICML 2026.\n\nShijue Huang, Wanjun Zhong, Jianqiao Lu, Qi Zhu, Jiahui Gao, Weiwen Liu, Yutai Hou, Xingshan Zeng, Yasheng Wang, Lifeng Shang, Xin Jiang, Ruifeng Xu, and Qun Liu. 2024. Planning, creation, usage: Benchmarking LLMs for comprehensive tool utilization in real-world complex scenarios. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2024, pages 4363–4400.\n\nHugging Face. 2024. smolagents: Building agents with code-first design. https://huggingface.co/blog/smolagents.\n\nSeth Karten, Alex L. Zhang, Kevin Thomas, Sebastian Müller, and Prime Intellect Team. 2026. Prime Agent: A self-improving RLM agent. https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/prime-agent. Accessed: 2026-08-06.\n\nMinghao Li, Yingxiu Zhao, Bowen Yu, Feifan Song, Hangyu Li, Haiyang Yu, Zhoujun Li, Fei Huang, and Yongbin Li. 2023. API-Bank: A comprehensive benchmark for tool-augmented LLMs. In Proceedings of the 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, EMNLP 2023, pages 3102–3116.\n\nTong Liu, Cheng Qian, Matej Cief, Yuan He, Daniele Dan, Nikolaos Aletras, and Gabriella Kazai. 2026. On effectiveness and efficiency of agentic tool-calling and RL training. In Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, ICML 2026.\n\nElias Lumer, Sahil Sen, Kevin Paul, and Vamse Kumar Subbiah. 2026. Recursive agent harnesses. arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.13643.\n\n\n\n# A Per-Category Accuracy on BFCL v4\n\nXuying Ning, Katherine Tieu, Dongqi Fu, Tianxin Wei, Zihao Li, Yuanchen Bei, Jiaru Zou, Mengting Ai, Zhining Liu, Ting-Wei Li, Lingjie Chen, Yanjun Zhao, Ke Yang, Bingxuan Li, Cheng Qian, Gaotang Li, Xiao Lin, Zhichen Zeng, Ruizhong Qiu, and 23 others. 2026. Code as agent harness. arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.18747.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the per-category accuracy of various models on the BFCL v4 benchmark. We evaluate the performance of 14 models across different categories, providing insights into their strengths and weaknesses.\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe BFCL v4 benchmark serves as a critical tool for assessing the capabilities of coding agents in various contexts. This study aims to quantify the accuracy of these agents across different categories, thereby identifying areas for improvement and further research.\n\n## Method\n\nWe conducted a series of evaluations on 14 models, measuring their performance in different categories. The categories included JSON tool calling, programmatic tool calling, and various live execution scenarios. The accuracy metrics were calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Accuracy} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\\text{Total Predictions}} \\times 100\n\\]\n\n## Results\n\nThe results of our evaluations are summarized in Table 6, which presents the mean accuracy per BFCL v4 category, averaged across all models. The table includes the following categories:\n\n- **JSON**: 40 samples, accuracy 86.1% ± 10.7\n- **Multiple**: 20 samples, accuracy 95.4% ± 11.1\n- **Parallel**: 40 samples, accuracy 85.5% ± 10.9\n- **Par. Multiple**: 40 samples, accuracy 85.5% ± 10.9\n- **Live Simple**: 25 samples, accuracy 76.6% ± 15.9\n- **Live Multiple**: 96 samples, accuracy 73.1% ± 8.7\n- **Live Parallel**: 24 samples, accuracy 66.1% ± 17.7\n- **Live Par. Mult.**: 24 samples, accuracy 66.1% ± 17.7\n\nOverall, the mean accuracy across all categories was 78.6% ± 4.6, indicating a robust performance by the models evaluated. The gaps observed in the parallel and par. multiple categories were largely driven by the three OpenAI models, which exhibited encoding failures.\n\n## Discussion\n\nThe findings highlight the varying performance of coding agents across different categories. The high accuracy in the JSON and multiple categories suggests that these models are well-equipped to handle structured data and multiple tool calls. However, the lower accuracy in live execution scenarios indicates a need for further refinement in real-time processing capabilities.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis study provides valuable insights into the performance of coding agents on the BFCL v4 benchmark. The results underscore the importance of category-specific evaluations in understanding the strengths and limitations of these models.\n\n## Appendix B: Programmatic Tool Calling Walkthroughs\n\nThis appendix shows concrete execution traces to illustrate how the programmatic tool calling agent loop operates in each evaluation setting. Each trace is a verbatim excerpt from a recorded trajectory, lightly condensed (absolute workspace paths are shortened to `<workspace>/`).\n\n### B.1 BFCL Single-Call (simple_python)\n\n**Task (simple_python_0)**: “Find the area of a triangle with a base of 10 units and height of 5 units.” The stub module provided to the model contains:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_triangle_area(base: int, height: int, unit: str = None) -> dict:\n"""Calculate the area of a triangle."""\nreturn _rpc.call('calculate_triangle_area', base=base, height=height, unit=unit)\n```\n\nThe agent emits a single tool call:\n\n```python\nexecute_python(code="""\nfrom stubs import calculate_triangle_area\nresult = calculate_triangle_area(base=10, height=5)\nprint(__import__('json').dumps(result))\n""")\n```\n\nThe subprocess prints:\n\n```\n{"calculate_triangle_area": {"base": 10, "height": 5}}\n[exit code 0]\n```\n\nThe stop middleware intercepts the next model call and terminates the loop. The scorer extracts function name and argument mapping from stdout, compares against ground truth, and records a single LLM turn for the entry.</workspace>\n\n\n\n# B.2 Chaining Ablation\n\n**Task (chaining_0):** “Compute the circumference of a circle with radius 7, then use that circumference as the side length of a square to find the square’s perimeter.”\n\nThe two relevant stubs are `geometry_circumference(radius)` and `geometry_square_perimeter(side)`.\n\n**Programmatic tool calling.** The agent writes a single script that computes the intermediate value in Python before passing it to the second stub:\n\n```python\nimport json, math\nfrom stubs import (\ngeometry_circumference,\ngeometry_square_perimeter)\n\nf1 = geometry_circumference(radius=7)\ncirc = 2 * math.pi * 7 # 43.982...\nf2 = geometry_square_perimeter(side=circ)\nprint(json.dumps(f1))\nprint(json.dumps(f2))\n```\n\n**Subprocess output:**\n```json\n{"geometry.circumference": {"radius": 7}}\n{"geometry.square_perimeter": {"side": 43.98}}\n```\n[exit code 0]\n\nBoth calls are resolved in a single subprocess, completing the entry in a single LLM turn.\n\n# B.3 Parallelism Ablation\n\n**Task fanout_population_7_a:** “Of 7 countries — Zorbania, Keltross, Miventa, Drulan, Quelfor, Hartox, and Bivessa — which 3 have the largest population?”\n\nThe available stub is `country_info_population(country: str) -> dict`.\n\n**Programmatic tool calling.** The agent issues one `execute_python` call that fans out all seven lookups concurrently and ranks the results in the same script:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom stubs import country_info_population\n\npop = country_info_population # alias\nasync def main():\ncc = ["Zorbania","Keltross","Miventa",\n"Drulan","Quelfor","Hartox","Bivessa"]\nres = await asyncio.gather(*[\nasyncio.to_thread(pop, country=c)\nfor c in cc\n])\npairs = sorted(\nzip(cc, [r["population"] for r in res]),\nkey=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)\ntop3 = [{"country": c, "population": p}\nfor c, p in pairs[:3]]\n```\n\nThe prompt specifies that real newlines (not \\n escape sequences) must be used in multiline scripts. The stop middleware intercepts the first model call after the execute tool returns and terminates the agent loop without issuing a second inference.\n\n**Stub module design.** Each stub function is generated from the benchmark’s function schema. It accepts the schema’s typed keyword arguments, immediately returns them as a structured dict, and prints the result to stdout. No external service is contacted. The scorer parses stdout to recover function name and argument mapping, then normalizes and compares against ground truth.	[]	[]	31684	2026-08-18 02:01:27.927209+00	2026-08-18 02:01:27.927209+00
11	11	llamaparse	# RP-OPSD: Reasoning-Pivot-Guided On-Policy Self-Distillation for Multilingual Reasoning Transfer\n\n**Xinye Wang, Junxiao Liu, Shujian Huang†**\nNational Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University\n{xinye.wang, junxiao.liu}@smail.nju.edu.cn, huangsj@nju.edu.cn\n\n## Abstract\nMultilingual reasoning transfer is crucial for extending reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) beyond high-resource languages. On-policy self-distillation (OPSD) and its variants have emerged as a promising paradigm, providing dense token-level supervision on student-generated rollouts, yet their objectives do not explicitly prioritize reasoning signals most critical to cross-lingual transfer. We characterize that target-language reasoning comprises the generation of both surface text and reasoning pivots, which are decisions that advance or redirect the reasoning process and shape subsequent inference. This motivates concentrating privileged distillation around such pivots. We therefore propose RP-OPSD, Reasoning-Pivot-guided On-Policy Self-Distillation, using the distributional shift between matched teacher views with and without an English reference solution as an operational proxy to guide privileged distillation and reference anchoring. Experiments on mathematical reasoning benchmarks covering 17 languages and multiple difficulty levels show that our method outperforms strong multilingual reasoning baselines and OPSD variants. Further analysis reveals that RP-OPSD concentrates privileged distillation on reasoning-control and problem-conditioned state-update tokens, while downweighting it for tokens that mainly support surface realization. Our code is available at https://github.com/NJUNLP/RP-OPSD.\n\n## 1 Introduction\nLarge language models (LLMs) exhibit substantial multi-step reasoning ability (Wei et al., 2023; DeepSeek-AI, 2025). In multilingual settings, however, the accessibility of this ability remains highly uneven among different languages (Shi et al., 2022; Ahuja et al., 2023). Due to the severe imbalance in multilingual training data (Nguyen et al., 2024; Nag et al., 2025), models often fail to reproduce reasoning behaviors they exhibit in English in non-dominant or low-resource languages. This disparity makes multilingual reasoning transfer a central problem: enabling high-resource reasoning capabilities to be reliably elicited, transferred, and realized in low-resource linguistic contexts.\n\nExisting adaptation strategies address this gap only partially. Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on translated rationales supplies dense training signals (Lai and Nissim, 2024; Barua et al., 2026), but translated rationales are off-policy and can contain translation artifacts that deviate from the model’s own low-resource reasoning trajectories. Reinforcement learning (RL) methods such as GRPO (Shao et al., 2024) avoid fixed rationales, yet correctness-only rewards are sparse and sequence-level, assigning credit only after a final answer is verified (Zhang et al., 2026c; Liu et al., 2026a). Such rewards provide limited pressure on the language model to learn effective reasoning strategies.\n\n## 2 Method\nWe propose RP-OPSD, which focuses on reasoning pivots to enhance multilingual reasoning transfer. The core idea is to leverage the distributional shift between matched teacher views conditioned on a solution and those without it. This shift serves as a guide for privileged distillation, allowing the model to concentrate on reasoning-sensitive tokens.\n\n### 2.1 Reasoning Pivots\nReasoning pivots are critical decisions that influence the reasoning process. They can be identified as tokens that either advance the reasoning or redirect it. For instance, in the mathematical reasoning example provided, the pivot "multiplie" indicates a critical step in the reasoning process, while surface tokens like "fait" serve to articulate the conclusion.\n\n### 2.2 Operational Proxy\nWe define the operational proxy as the distributional gap between the solution-conditioned view \\( q^+ \\) and the ablated view \\( q^- \\). This gap helps identify which tokens are essential for reasoning and should be prioritized during distillation.\n\n### 2.3 Distillation Process\nThe distillation process involves training the student model to mimic the teacher model's behavior, focusing on the identified reasoning pivots. This approach ensures that the model learns to prioritize reasoning over mere surface realization.\n\n## 3 Results\nExperiments conducted on mathematical reasoning benchmarks covering 17 languages demonstrate that RP-OPSD significantly outperforms existing multilingual reasoning baselines and OPSD variants. The results indicate that concentrating on reasoning pivots leads to improved performance in low-resource languages.\n\n## 4 Conclusion\nRP-OPSD presents a novel approach to multilingual reasoning transfer by emphasizing reasoning pivots in the distillation process. This method not only enhances the reasoning capabilities of LLMs in low-resource languages but also provides a framework for future research in multilingual reasoning.\n\n## References\n- Lai, Y., &#x26; Nissim, M. (2024). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Barua, A., et al. (2026). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Wei, J., et al. (2023). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Shao, Y., et al. (2024). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Zhang, X., et al. (2026c). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Liu, Y., et al. (2026a). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Shi, Y., et al. (2022). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Ahuja, A., et al. (2023). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Nguyen, T., et al. (2024). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- Nag, A., et al. (2025). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n- DeepSeek-AI. (2025). Title of the paper. Journal Name.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nRecent advancements in on-policy self-distillation (OPSD) have shown promise in enhancing multilingual reasoning capabilities. This paper introduces RP-OPSD (Reasoning-Pivot-Guided On-Policy Self-Distillation), which refines the OPSD framework by focusing on reasoning pivots—key decision points that influence the evolution of the latent solution state. By leveraging a privileged cross-lingual teacher context, RP-OPSD aims to improve the transfer of reasoning across languages while addressing the limitations of existing OPSD variants.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to reason across multiple languages is crucial for developing robust multilingual models. Traditional OPSD methods have provided a foundation for this task, but they often treat all generated positions uniformly, which can dilute the effectiveness of reasoning signals. This paper argues for a more nuanced approach that distinguishes between surface realizations and reasoning pivots, thereby enhancing the transfer of reasoning across languages.\n\n# Method\n## 2.1 Notations\nFor each training instance, let \\( x_\\ell \\) denote the low-resource question, \\( x_h \\) its English translation, and \\( s_h \\) an English reference reasoning trace. The trainable policy is denoted by \\( \\pi_\\theta \\). The student rollout is generated on policy from the low-resource input:\n\n\\[\ny_{1:T} \\sim \\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid x_\\ell).\n\\]\n\nAt response position \\( t \\), the student next-token distribution is given by:\n\n\\[\np = \\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid x_\\ell, y_{\n\n\n\n# 2.2 Teacher Views\n\nPrior work attributes low-resource reasoning failures to two factors: question understanding and multilingual reasoning (Liu et al., 2026a). To control for the first factor, both teacher views are given the English translation of the question, denoted by \\( x_h \\). Thus, the contrast between the two views is designed to reflect the effect of privileged reasoning information, rather than a failure to understand the low-resource question.\n\nThe solution-conditioned view receives the low-resource question, the English translation of the question, and the English reference trace:\n\n\\[\nq^+ = sg \\, \\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\, | \\, x_\\ell, x_h, s_h, y_{\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents the RP-OPSD pipeline, which enhances multilingual reasoning through a novel approach that combines on-policy target-language rollouts with a Reasoning-Pivot Transfer (RPT) gate. The method aims to improve the performance of low-resource language models by leveraging privileged reasoning sensitivity scores and applying a distillation process that focuses on reasoning-sensitive tokens.\n\n# Introduction\nMultilingual reasoning remains a challenging task, particularly in low-resource settings. This research addresses the need for effective methods to enhance reasoning capabilities across various languages. We propose the RP-OPSD pipeline, which constructs on-policy rollouts and utilizes an RPT gate to optimize the distillation process. Our approach is designed to improve the alignment of reasoning traces between target languages and English, thereby facilitating better performance in multilingual contexts.\n\n# Method\n## Overview of the RP-OPSD Pipeline\nThe RP-OPSD pipeline consists of three main components:\n1. **Construct Rollouts**: Generate target-language rollouts based on the English problem statements and their translations.\n2. **Compute RPT Gate**: Calculate the RPT gate using privileged reasoning sensitivity (PRS) scores to determine the importance of reasoning-sensitive tokens.\n3. **Apply Gate in Distillation**: Implement the RPT gate during the distillation process to focus on reasoning-sensitive tokens while anchoring surface-realization tokens to a reference distribution.\n\nThe equations governing the PRS score and the losses used in the distillation process are as follows:\n\n1. **Privileged Reasoning Sensitivity (PRS) Score**:\n\\[\n\\text{PRS} = -\\log\\left(\\frac{P(y | x, \\theta)}{P(y | x, \\phi)}\\right)\n\\]\n\n2. **Pivot Loss**:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{pivot}} = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\left( y_i - \\hat{y}_i \\right)^2\n\\]\n\n3. **Anchor Loss**:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{anchor}} = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\left( y_i - \\hat{y}_i \\right)^2\n\\]\n\n4. **Reasoning-Pivot Transfer (RPT) Gate**:\n\\[\ng = \\sigma\\left(\\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\text{PRS}_i\\right)\n\\]\n\n5. **Total Loss**:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{total}} = \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{pivot}} + \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{anchor}}\n\\]\n\nFigure 2 illustrates the RP-OPSD pipeline, detailing the construction of rollouts, the computation of the RPT gate, and the application of the gate in the distillation process.\n\n# Results\nWe evaluate the performance of RP-OPSD across 17 target languages, including 12 low-resource African languages and several medium- to high-resource languages such as Chinese, French, Japanese, Spanish, and Russian. Our results demonstrate that RP-OPSD consistently improves multilingual reasoning capabilities, as shown in Table 1. The method outperforms several baselines, including standard training methods and other multilingual reasoning alignment techniques.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe RP-OPSD pipeline effectively enhances multilingual reasoning by leveraging privileged reasoning sensitivity and a targeted distillation approach. Our findings indicate significant improvements in low-resource language settings, paving the way for future research in multilingual model training and reasoning alignment. Further exploration of the pipeline's components and their interactions will be essential for optimizing performance across diverse languages.\n\n\n\n# Method\n\n| Method       | AfriMGSM (pass@12) | PolyMath (DW-ACC) | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | |\n|--------------|---------------------|--------------------|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|              | AMH | EWE | HAU | KIN | LUG | SNA | SOT | SWA | WOL | XHO | YOR | ZUL | Avg. | ZHO | FRA | SWA | JPN | SPA | RUS | Avg. | | | | |\n|--------------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|------| | | |\n| Base         | 12.0 | 8.8 | 13.2 | 9.2 | 7.2 | 8.8 | 8.4 | 13.2 | 8.8 | 9.2 | 11.2 | 8.8 | 9.90 | 21.17 | 17.98 | 0.85 | 12.96 | 16.43 | 19.84 | 14.87 | | | | |\n| SFT          | 12.8 | 9.2 | 14.0 | 14.4 |     |     |     | 11.2 | 11.2 | 12.0 | 16.8 | 14.4 |     | 15.6 | 10.0 | 13.2 | 12.90 | 20.38 | 16.97 | 1.44 | 11.95 | 15.58 | 18.77 | 14.18 |\n| Qwen3-1.7B  | GRPO | 13.2 | 10.4 | 13.2 | 7.6 |     |     | 6.8 | 9.2 | 9.6 | 15.2 | 6.0 | 6.8 | 12.4 | 10.0 | 10.03 | 24.16 |     | 18.51 | 0.27 | 13.01 | 18.67 | 18.29 | 15.48 |\n|              | MAPO-DPO | 12.0 | 6.4 | 11.6 | 7.6 | 4.8 | 8.0 | 6.0 | 13.6 | 7.6 | 6.0 | 10.0 | 9.2 | 8.57 | 23.68 | 18.13 | 0.75 | 13.60 | 17.55 | 19.47 | 15.53 | | | |\n|              | M-Thinker | 22.8 | 13.6 | 13.6 | 10.4 | 14.0 | 10.0 | 14.4 | 22.4 | 13.2 | 14.4 | 12.0 | 15.6 | 14.87 | 24.80 | 19.14 | 1.12 | 14.88 | 19.47 | 20.32 | 16.62 | | | |\n|              | PCS | — | — | — | — | — | — | — | 16.0 | — | — | — | — | 24.69 | 19.30 | 1.39 | 15.05 | 19.63 | 20.37 | 16.74 | | | | |\n|              | COPSD | 23.6 | 14.8 | 16.4 | 14.4 | 16.0 | 11.6 | 15.2 | 26.0 | 13.6 | 15.2 | 19.2 | 14.4 | 16.70 | 23.20 | 4.00 | 19.31 | 13.38 | 17.71 | 18.35 | 15.99 | | | |\n|              | EGRSD | 24.0 | 14.8 | 17.2 | 14.0 | 13.6 | 16.4 | 16.4 | 26.8 | 12.8 | 11.2 | 14.8 | 14.8 | 16.40 | 23.31 | 19.31 | 3.25 | 14.13 | 18.72 | 19.15 | 16.31 | | | |\n|              | RP-OPSD | 24.4 | 15.2 | 20.0 | 16.0 | 18.4 | 19.2 | 17.6 | 29.6 | 15.2 | 17.6 | 16.4 | 19.2 | 19.07 | 25.49 | 20.05 | 5.23 | 15.31 | 20.85 | 20.91 | 17.97 | | | |\n|              | Base | 26.8 | 17.6 | 23.6 | 17.6 | 16.8 | 14.8 | 17.2 | 44.0 | 15.2 | 17.6 | 18.0 | 14.4 | 20.30 | 36.27 | 34.56 | 2.24 | 29.55 | 33.71 | 34.67 | 28.50 | | | |\n|              | SFT | 25.2 | 12.4 | 17.6 | 15.2 | 17.6 | 17.2 | 18.4 | 40.4 | 17.2 | 17.2 | 11.6 | 15.2 | 18.77 | 35.47 | 32.96 | 1.92 | 26.08 | 32.21 | 33.97 | 27.10 | | | |\n| Qwen3-4B    | GRPO | 28.8 |     | 15.6 | 20.8 | 17.6 | 16.0 | 14.0 | 19.2 | 44.8 | 15.2 | 16.8 | 17.2 | 14.4 | 20.03 | 38.19 | 31.47 | 29.87 | 1.92 | 34.61 | 34.45 | 28.42 | | |\n|              | MAPO-DPO | 27.6 | 13.6 |     | 23.6 | 18.0 | 18.0 | 15.2 | 17.6 | 47.2 | 19.2 | 17.6 | 16.4 | 15.6 | 20.80 | 37.55 | 34.51 | 27.52 | 33.07 | 7.52 | 33.12 | 28.88 | | |\n|              | M-Thinker | 34.4 |     |     | 15.2 | 17.2 | 22.8 | 23.2 | 20.8 | 15.6 | 50.4 | 19.2 | 18.0 | 16.4 | 17.2 | 22.53 | 38.35 | 34.51 | 28.49 | 35.52 | 8.06 | 33.49 | 29.74 | |\n|              | PCS | — | — | — | — | — | — | — | 51.6 | — | — | — | — | 38.61 | 34.72 | 8.32 | 28.70 | 35.57 | 34.34 | 30.04 | | | | |\n|              | COPSD | 34.4 |     | 15.6 | 20.8 | 16.0 | 18.8 | 17.2 | 16.4 | 20.0 | 51.2 | 14.0 | 18.0 | 21.63 | 37.34 | 17.2 | 34.77 | 7.68 | 29.49 | 35.10 | 35.26 | 29.94 | | |\n|              | EGRSD | 34.4 |     | 17.2 | 22.0 | 18.0 | 20.0 | 16.4 | 16.8 | 51.8 | 19.6 | 20.0 | 22.68 | 21.2 | 37.68 | 14.8 | 34.93 | 8.00 | 29.39 | 35.37 | 30.16 | 35.58 | | |\n|              | RP-OPSD | 35.2 |     | 22.4 | 26.4 | 22.8 | 26.0 | 25.2 | 22.8 | 54.8 | 20.8 | 22.8 | 19.2 | 23.6 | 26.83 | 39.99 | 29.44 | 12.11 | 37.01 | 35.04 | 37.65 | 31.87 | | |\n\n**Table 1:** Main results on AfriMGSM and PolyMath. Averages are arithmetic means over all reported languages; the AfriMGSM average for PCS is omitted because only Swahili is available. The metric for AfriMGSM is pass@12 (%), while the metric for PolyMath is difficulty-weighted ACC@1 (DW-ACC, %). The best result for each model scale is bolded, while the second-best result is underlined. Since the language detection tools required by PCS do not support recognizing African languages other than Swahili, we did not test PCS’s performance on these languages.\n\nAfriMGSM, RP-OPSD reaches average pass@12 scores of 19.07 and 26.83 for Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-4B, respectively, improving over COPSD by 2.37 and 5.20 points and over M-Thinker, the strongest fully reported non-OPSD baseline, by 4.20 and 4.30 points. RP-OPSD also outperforms EGRSD at both scales. Across individual languages, RP-OPSD matches or outperforms the conventional multilingual reasoning transfer baselines MAPO-DPO, M-Thinker, and PCS in every comparable AfriMGSM setting, suggesting that reasoning-pivot-aware distillation is more effective than full-trace alignment, language-reward-guided RL, or progressive code-switching.\n\nOn PolyMath, RP-OPSD achieves the highest average DW-ACC at both scales, reaching 17.97 and 31.87 and improving over COPSD by 1.98 and 1.93 points, respectively. It also exceeds M-Thinker by 1.35 and 2.13 points. Although pass@12 and DW-ACC are not directly comparable, the gains over the base models are numerically more modest on PolyMath (+3.10 and +3.37 DW-ACC) than on AfriMGSM (+9.17 and +6.53 pass@12). This pattern is consistent with DW-ACC assigning greater weight to difficult problems, thereby emphasizing the cases where further improvements are hardest to obtain. Overall, RP-OPSD yields consistent gains in both low-resource and broader multilingual mathematical reasoning. Further experiments demonstrate that these gains extend across additional benchmarks, model families, and domains; see Appendices C, D, and E.\n\n## Ablation and Analysis\n\n### 4.1 Ablation and Functional Analysis of the RPT Gate\n\nTable 2 jointly ablates RP-OPSD and functionally evaluates the RPT gate on SWA and FRA, using Base and the full RP-OPSD objective as reference points. We construct three budget-matched hard-routing variants that apply privileged distillation to 20% of rollout tokens, with no reference anchoring on the remaining 80%: TG selects the highest-gate tokens, BG the lowest-gate tokens, and RG a random subset. Thus, TG–RG tests whether gate-based localization matters beyond supervision quantity, while TG–BG tests whether the gate ranking correctly orders transfer utility. We additionally remove reference anchoring while retaining continuous RPT-weighted distillation to isolate the contribution of the anchoring branch.\n\nUnder the matched 20% budget, TG reaches 26.0 on SWA and 74.8 on FRA, compared with 20.8/74.0 for RG and 15.6/73.2 for BG. The consistent ordering TG > RG > BG supports the interpretation that RPT scores rank where privileged reasoning supervision is most useful, rather than merely identifying tokens that benefit from arbitrary supervision.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the effectiveness of the RPT gate in enhancing reasoning capabilities in language models, particularly focusing on the Qwen3-1.7B model. We analyze the performance of various configurations, including hard-gate localization and objective ablation, to understand their impact on reasoning and localization in different contexts.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in language models have highlighted the importance of reasoning capabilities in generating accurate and contextually relevant outputs. This study aims to explore the role of the RPT gate in facilitating reasoning through selective token distillation and reference anchoring. We present a detailed analysis of the configurations and their respective performances on the AfriMGSM and MGSM benchmarks.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments using the Qwen3-1.7B model, evaluating various configurations of the RPT gate. The configurations included:\n- **Base**: The standard model performance.\n- **Hard-gate localization**: Utilizing a 20% token budget to select top (TG), bottom (BG), and random (RG) tokens for privileged distillation.\n- **Objective ablation**: Removing reference anchoring to assess its impact on performance.\n\nPerformance metrics included pass@12 and ACC@1, as detailed in Table 2.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that the hard-gate localization significantly improves performance, particularly in the SWA metric, where TG outperforms RG and BG by 5.2 and 10.4 points, respectively. In contrast, the FRA metric shows smaller margins of 0.8 and 1.6 points. The overall best performance was achieved with the RP-OPSD configuration, yielding scores of 29.6% for pass@12 and 76.8% for ACC@1.\n\n## 4.3 RP-OPSD Extends Reasoning beyond Base-Model Reachability\nThe RP-OPSD method demonstrates theoretical advantages over other baseline methods by requiring only the translation of the question while keeping the reference solution in English. This approach allows for the preservation of reasoning capabilities without introducing unnecessary target-language pressure during the initial training phases.\n\n# Discussion\nThe analysis of high-gate positions reveals two functional types of reasoning pivots: reasoning-control pivots and problem-conditioned state-update pivots. The former includes transitional tokens that guide the reasoning process, while the latter encapsulates problem-specific concepts essential for advancing solutions. The findings suggest that the RPT gate effectively identifies critical reasoning points, enhancing the model's ability to generate coherent and contextually appropriate responses.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe study concludes that the RPT gate is a valuable tool for improving reasoning capabilities in language models. By selectively distilling tokens and maintaining reference anchoring, the model can achieve better performance in reasoning tasks. Future work will explore further enhancements to the RPT gate and its applications in various language processing scenarios.\n\n# References\n- Qian et al., 2025; Bogdan et al., 2025.\n- Zhang et al., 2026c; Wang et al., 2026c.\n\n# Appendix\nFurther details on the experimental setup and additional analyses can be found in Appendix A and G.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the effectiveness of cross-lingual reasoning transfer in enhancing language consistency and problem-solving accuracy across multiple target languages. We analyze the performance of various models, particularly focusing on the RP-OPSD framework, and its ability to leverage English solutions to improve outcomes in other languages.\n\n# Introduction\nThe increasing demand for multilingual models in natural language processing has led to a growing interest in cross-lingual reasoning. This study aims to explore how reasoning capabilities can be transferred from high-resource languages, such as English, to low-resource languages. We hypothesize that leveraging English solutions can significantly enhance the performance of models in target languages.\n\n# Method\nWe employed a series of experiments to evaluate the performance of different models, including the M-Thinker and RP-OPSD frameworks. The models were tested on a diverse set of problems across six target languages. We measured the language consistency (LC) and the accuracy of the models using metrics such as MGSM accuracy and MAPO.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that RP-OPSD outperforms other models in terms of both language consistency and problem-solving accuracy. Specifically, RP-OPSD achieved a reduction of 13.7 problems in the English-correct but target-incorrect set, while also increasing the English-incorrect but target-correct set by 8.3 problems. This demonstrates the model's ability to effectively transfer reasoning from English to other languages.\n\nFigure 3 illustrates the cross-lingual solution reachability on PolyMath before and after training, averaged over six target languages. The filled circles (•) represent correct answers, while the hollow circles (•) indicate incorrect answers. The upper group compares the number of English-correct but target-incorrect problems before and after training, while the lower group compares the number of English-incorrect but target-correct problems.\n\nFigure 4 shows the relationship between language consistency (LC) and MGSM accuracy on the focused AMH, SWA, ZH, KR, and TH subset. The dashed lines mark COPSD, and the arrow indicates the shift from COPSD to RP-OPSD.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings suggest that the RP-OPSD framework not only retains a significant portion of the English-reachable transfer gain but also expands the target-language solution set into areas that are typically inaccessible through traditional reasoning methods. This indicates that the model's improvements are not solely due to re-expressing reasoning already available in English but also involve a deeper integration of cross-lingual reasoning capabilities.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, our study highlights the potential of cross-lingual reasoning transfer in enhancing language consistency and problem-solving accuracy across multiple languages. The RP-OPSD framework demonstrates significant improvements, suggesting that future research should continue to explore the integration of high-resource language solutions in low-resource contexts.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the reasoning capabilities of models in the context of time-rescaling problems, particularly focusing on the impact of speed changes on travel time. We analyze the performance of various models, including RP-OPSD, GRPO, and MAPO, in accurately interpreting and solving these problems.\n\n# Introduction\nIn this study, we explore how different models handle reasoning tasks that involve time and speed calculations. Specifically, we examine a scenario where Buratino must calculate the time required to reach the Field of Miracles based on his walking speed and the effect of increasing that speed by 25%. The goal is to determine the time of arrival at the destination.\n\n# Method\nWe present a case study involving a time-rescaling problem where Buratino leaves Papa Carlo's house at 13:40 to plant coins at sunset. The problem states that if he had walked 25% faster, he would have arrived 1.5 hours earlier. We analyze the reasoning pivots used by different models to solve this problem, focusing on the correct interpretation of speed and time relationships.\n\n# Results\nThe analysis reveals that the correct reasoning pivot involves recognizing that an increase in speed by 25% results in a decrease in travel time to \\( \\frac{t}{1.25} = 0.8t \\). In contrast, an incorrect interpretation leads to the conclusion that time decreases by 25%, resulting in \\( 0.75t \\). The performance of RP-OPSD is highlighted, showing an improvement in accuracy while maintaining language coherence.\n\nFigure 5 illustrates the case study of RPT-gate localization, contrasting the correct time-rescaling pivot with an incorrect interpretation. The figure shows that the local mapping from speed \\( v \\) to \\( 1.25v \\) leads to the required time \\( t \\) being transformed to \\( \\frac{t}{1.25} = 0.8t \\). This reasoning pivot is crucial as it alters the latent solution state rather than merely restating a quantity from the problem.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings indicate that RP-OPSD effectively localizes privileged transfer around critical reasoning operations, such as the transition from speed to time scaling. The model's ability to maintain high accuracy while preserving language coherence suggests a better partition between transferable reasoning and language expression. This case study emphasizes the importance of correctly interpreting numerical values and operations in reasoning tasks.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, the study demonstrates the significance of accurate reasoning pivots in solving time-rescaling problems. The performance of RP-OPSD highlights the potential for models to improve their reasoning capabilities by focusing on operations that alter the reasoning state rather than simply translating quantities. Future work should continue to explore the implications of these findings for enhancing model performance in complex reasoning tasks.\n\n\n\n# Conclusion\n\nWe propose RP-OPSD, which contrasts teacher views to route privileged distillation toward reasoning-sensitive positions and reference anchoring elsewhere. Across 17 languages and two mathematical-reasoning benchmarks, RP-OPSD outperforms strong multilingual reasoning baselines and OPSD variants. High-gate positions exhibit recurring reasoning-control transitions and problem-conditioned state updates, whereas low-gate positions largely reflect routine target-language and symbolic realization. More broadly, RP-OPSD points toward multilingual reasoners that share computational structure across languages while retaining natural, language-specific forms of expression.\n\n# Related Work\n\n## Multilingual Reasoning Transfer\n\nRecent work on multilingual reasoning transfer has progressed from inference-time cross-lingual prompting to training-time control of reasoning language. Cross-lingual prompting aligns reasoning across languages and aggregates multilingual reasoning paths to improve zero-shot CoT (Qin et al., 2023), while Question Translation Training performs question-level alignment by translating non-English problems into English before solution generation (Zhu et al., 2024). MAPO further aligns non-dominant-language reasoning traces with English via translation-based preference optimization (She et al., 2024), and TRIT jointly trains translation and reasoning to enhance multilingual understanding and target-language generation without extra supervision (Liu et al., 2026a). Complementary work studies structured prompting, English-pivoted reasoning, RL-based alignment, and how reasoning language shapes intermediate reasoning (Ranaldi et al., 2024; Tran et al., 2025b; Tam et al., 2025; Zhang et al., 2026c,b). In contrast, our work shifts from aligning full reasoning traces to identifying where transfer should occur, using privileged-context distributional shifts to selectively transfer reasoning-critical signals while preserving target-language realization.\n\n## On-Policy Self-Distillation\n\nOn-policy distillation reduces train–test mismatch by training models on their own rollouts (Agarwal et al., 2024). OPSD uses a single model as both student and privileged-context teacher, providing dense token-level supervision without a separate teacher (Zhao et al., 2026a). Subsequent variants incorporate feedback-conditioned predictions, context internalization, cross-lingual privileged context, reflection-localized correction, and efficiency or stability enhancements (Hübotter et al., 2026; Ye et al., 2026; Liu et al., 2026b; Zhao et al., 2026b; Zhang et al., 2026a; Jang et al., 2026). RP-OPSD instead contrasts privileged and ablated teacher views to route full-distribution supervision toward reasoning-sensitive positions while anchoring elsewhere.\n\n# References\n\n- David Ifeoluwa Adelani, Jessica Ojo, Israel Abebe Azime, Jian Yun Zhuang, Jesujoba O. Alabi, Xuanli He, Millicent Ochieng, Sara Hooker, Andiswa Bukula, En-Shiun Annie Lee, Chiamaka Chukwuneke, Happy Buzaaba, Blessing Sibanda, Godson Kalipe, Jonathan Mukiibi, Salomon Kabongo, Foutse Yuehgoh, Mmasibidi Setaka, Lolwethu Ndolela, and 8 others. 2025. Irokobench: A new benchmark for African languages in the age of large language models. Preprint, arXiv:2406.03368.\n- Rishabh Agarwal, Nino Vieillard, Yongchao Zhou, Piotr Stanczyk, Sabela Ramos, Matthieu Geist, and Olivier Bachem. 2024. On-policy distillation of language models: Learning from self-generated mistakes. Preprint, arXiv:2306.13649.\n- Kabir Ahuja, Harshita Diddee, Rishav Hada, Millicent Ochieng, Krithika Ramesh, Prachi Jain, Akshay Nambi, Tanuja Ganu, Sameer Segal, Mohamed Ahmed, Kalika Bali, and Sunayana Sitaram. 2023. MEGA: Multilingual evaluation of generative AI. In Proceedings of the 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 4232–4267, Singapore. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n- Josh Barua, Seun Eisape, Kayo Yin, and Alane Suhr. 2026. Long chain-of-thought reasoning across languages. Preprint, arXiv:2508.14828.\n- Paul C. Bogdan, Uzay Macar, Neel Nanda, and Arthur Conmy. 2025. Thought anchors: Which LLM reasoning steps matter? Preprint, arXiv:2506.19143.\n- DeepSeek-AI. 2025. Deepseek-r1 incentivizes reasoning in LLMs through reinforcement learning. Nature, 645(8081):633–638.\n\n\n\n# DeepSeek-AI: Towards Highly Efficient Million-Token Context Intelligence\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we introduce DeepSeek-v4, a novel approach aimed at enhancing the efficiency of large language models (LLMs) in processing million-token contexts. Our method leverages advanced self-distillation techniques and adaptive target reformulation to optimize reasoning capabilities across diverse languages. We present empirical results demonstrating significant improvements in reasoning consistency and efficiency.\n\n## Introduction\nThe rapid advancement of large language models has opened new avenues for natural language processing. However, the challenge of efficiently handling extensive contexts remains a critical barrier. This paper addresses this issue by proposing DeepSeek-v4, which integrates skill-conditioned gated self-distillation and translation-augmented policy optimization. Our approach aims to democratize access to LLMs for low-resource languages while maintaining high reasoning performance.\n\n## Method\n### Skill-Conditioned Gated Self-Distillation\nWe employ a skill-conditioned gated self-distillation framework that allows the model to refine its reasoning capabilities based on specific tasks. The self-distillation process is guided by mutual information metrics, ensuring that the model focuses on the most informative tokens during reasoning.\n\n### Translation-Augmented Policy Optimization\nTo enhance multilingual capabilities, we implement translation-augmented policy optimization. This method allows the model to leverage its dominant English abilities to improve performance in low-resource languages. The optimization process is designed to align reasoning paths across languages, facilitating better understanding and generation of multilingual content.\n\n## Results\nOur experiments demonstrate that DeepSeek-v4 significantly outperforms existing models in both efficiency and reasoning accuracy. We conducted extensive evaluations across various datasets, showcasing improvements in zero-shot reasoning tasks and multilingual alignment. The results indicate that our approach effectively addresses the challenges of context management in LLMs.\n\n## Conclusion\nDeepSeek-v4 represents a significant step forward in the quest for efficient million-token context intelligence. By combining advanced self-distillation techniques with translation-augmented optimization, we have developed a model that not only enhances reasoning capabilities but also democratizes access to LLMs for low-resource languages. Future work will focus on further refining these techniques and exploring their applications in real-world scenarios.\n\n## References\n- DeepSeek-AI et al. (2026). Deepseek-v4: Towards highly efficient million-token context intelligence. Preprint, arXiv:2606.19348.\n- Guha, E., Marten, R., Keh, S., Raoof, N., et al. (2025). Openthoughts: Data recipes for reasoning models. Preprint, arXiv:2506.04178.\n- Huang, J., Chen, X., Luo, X., Dai, Y., et al. (2026a). Skill-conditioned gated self-distillation for LLM reasoning. Preprint, arXiv:2605.28791.\n- Huang, X., Lai, Z., Huang, Z., Chen, J., et al. (2026b). Tapo: Translation augmented policy optimization for multilingual mathematical reasoning. Preprint, arXiv:2603.25419.\n- Liu, J., Wang, Z., Li, Y., Lai, Z., et al. (2026a). Self-improving multilingual long reasoning via translation-reasoning integrated training. Preprint, arXiv:2602.05940.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the effectiveness of privileged distillation in the context of large language models (LLMs) and its relationship with RPT (Reinforcement Policy Transfer) gates. We investigate how the positioning of RPT gates influences the performance of LLMs during the distillation process, particularly focusing on the implications for multilingual reasoning.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in large language models have highlighted the importance of effective training methodologies, particularly in multilingual contexts. This study aims to dissect the role of RPT gates in the distillation process, providing insights into their positioning and effectiveness.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments to evaluate the performance of LLMs under different configurations of RPT gates. The methodology involved:\n\n1. **Model Selection**: We utilized several state-of-the-art LLMs for our experiments.\n2. **Distillation Process**: The distillation was performed using privileged information, with varying positions of RPT gates.\n3. **Evaluation Metrics**: Performance was assessed based on multilingual reasoning tasks, measuring accuracy and response time.\n\n# Results\nOur findings indicate that privileged distillation is most effective at high RPT-gate positions. This suggests a significant correlation between the positioning of RPT gates and the model's ability to leverage distilled knowledge effectively.\n\n## Figure 1: Performance of LLMs with Varying RPT Gate Positions\n*Figure 1 illustrates the performance metrics of LLMs across different RPT gate configurations, highlighting the optimal positioning for enhanced multilingual reasoning.*\n\n# Discussion\nThe results underscore the importance of RPT gate positioning in the distillation process. Future work should explore the underlying mechanisms that contribute to the observed performance improvements, as well as the potential for further optimization in multilingual contexts.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study provides valuable insights into the role of RPT gates in the distillation of large language models, particularly in enhancing multilingual reasoning capabilities. Our findings pave the way for future research aimed at optimizing training methodologies for LLMs.\n\n# References\n- Hieu Tran, Zonghai Yao, and Hong Yu. 2025a. Exploiting tree structure for credit assignment in RL training of LLMs. Preprint, arXiv:2509.18314.\n- Khanh-Tung Tran, Nguyet-Hang Vu, Barry O’Sullivan, and Hoang D. Nguyen. 2025b. Disentangling language understanding and reasoning structures in cross-lingual chain-of-thought prompting. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025, pages 12200–12206, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n- Jiaxuan Wang, Xuan Ouyang, Zhiyu Chen, Yulan Hu, Zheng Pan, Xin Li, and Lan-Zhe Guo. 2026a. Trace: Distilling where it matters via token-routed self on-policy alignment. Preprint, arXiv:2605.10194.\n- Weixuan Wang, Minghao Wu, Barry Haddow, and Alexandra Birch. 2025a. Demystifying multilingual reasoning in process reward modeling. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025, pages 9775–9788, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n- Yiming Wang, Pei Zhang, Jialong Tang, Haoran Wei, Baosong Yang, Rui Wang, Chenshu Sun, Feitong Sun, Jiran Zhang, Junxuan Wu, Qiqian Cang, Yichang Zhang, Fei Huang, Junyang Lin, Fei Huang, and Jingren Zhou. 2025b. Polymath: Evaluating mathematical reasoning in multilingual contexts. Preprint, arXiv:2504.18428.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the role of reasoning pivots in computational models, particularly focusing on the use of receiver scores as a proxy for downstream information reuse. We introduce the concept of thought anchors, which are sentences that serve as persistent information sources for subsequent reasoning. Our analysis demonstrates that the RPT (Reasoning Pivot Transfer) gate effectively identifies these thought anchors, outperforming traditional metrics such as entropy, surprisal, and KD loss.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to identify and leverage key information during reasoning processes is crucial for enhancing model performance. This study aims to explore how reasoning pivots can influence subsequent reasoning steps and how receiver scores can serve as a reliable measure of this influence. By examining the relationship between receiver scores and downstream reasoning, we provide insights into the mechanisms that underlie effective information transfer in computational models.\n\n# Method\nWe employ a framework based on the receiver score to quantify the influence of reasoning sentences on subsequent computation. The receiver score is derived from ordinary student attention, excluding privileged teacher contexts and distillation loss. We compare the performance of the RPT gate against several baselines, including entropy, surprisal, KD loss, and random permutation.\n\n# Results\nThe results, as illustrated in Figure 6, show that the RPT gate consistently outperforms all baseline metrics across various cutoffs. Specifically, the macro nDCG scores for the RPT gate are 0.699, 0.672, and 0.684 at the 10%, 20%, and 30% cutoffs, respectively. Additionally, the RPT gate achieves a macro AUPRC of 0.463, surpassing the scores of other metrics such as surprisal (0.413), entropy (0.393), and KD loss (0.387). This indicates that the RPT gate effectively identifies the most important thought anchors, which are concentrated among high gate sentences.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings suggest that the RPT gate is adept at locating positions where the privileged reference trace alters the model's reasoning state. This capability is essential for distinguishing between mere disagreement and meaningful changes in reasoning. The results highlight the importance of using matched teacher views to enhance the identification of reasoning pivots, thereby facilitating better information transfer in computational models.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, our study demonstrates the efficacy of the RPT gate in identifying thought anchors that significantly influence subsequent reasoning. By leveraging receiver scores, we provide a novel approach to understanding the dynamics of information reuse in computational models, paving the way for future research in this area.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of the Qwen3-1.7B model across various languages, focusing on the impact of different gate scores on accuracy. We present a detailed analysis of the model's performance using PolyMath and AfriMGSM metrics, highlighting the effects of teacher-student dynamics in the context of language realization and reasoning evidence.\n\n# Introduction\nThe study of language models has gained significant traction, particularly in the context of multilingual capabilities. This research aims to evaluate the Qwen3-1.7B model's performance across several languages, including ZHO, FRA, POR, THA, and KOR. We explore the implications of different scoring methods on model accuracy and the underlying mechanisms that contribute to these outcomes.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments using the Qwen3-1.7B model, evaluating it at specific checkpoints for different languages. The evaluation metrics included PolyMath difficulty-weighted accuracy and AfriMGSM pass rates. The model's performance was assessed under controlled conditions, ensuring consistent checkpoint and decoding settings across various gate scores.\n\n# Results\nTable 4 presents the results of the gate-score ablation on Qwen3-1.7B. The matched-view score represents the original RP-OPSD gate, while the teacher-student alternative modifies the gate by removing \\( q^- \\) and adjusting the RPT score. The results indicate a consistent degradation in performance when the student replaces the ablated teacher in the gate score. Specifically, the PolyMath accuracy for ZHO decreased from 25.49 to 23.46, and the AfriMGSM pass rate for SWA dropped from 29.6% to 27.2%.\n\nThe analysis reveals that the main difference between the scores lies in the quantity they isolate. The two teacher views share the low-resource question and its English translation, differing only in access to the English reference trace. The gap \\( D_{KL}(q^+ \\| pt) \\) reflects question-understanding differences and the student’s adaptation state.\n\nTable 5 provides additional results for Qwen3-1.7B across medium- and high-resource languages. The model achieved the highest accuracy on ZHO, FRA, POR, and KOR, with RP-OPSD outperforming other methods.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings suggest that the choice of gate score significantly influences model performance, particularly in low-resource settings. The results indicate that the matched contrast better localizes states with downstream reasoning influence, highlighting the importance of teacher-student calibration in language realization tasks.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis research underscores the complexities involved in evaluating multilingual language models. The Qwen3-1.7B model demonstrates varying performance across different languages, influenced by the scoring methods employed. Future work should focus on refining these methods to enhance model accuracy and understanding in multilingual contexts.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the effectiveness of the RP-OPSD method in enhancing reasoning capabilities across different languages and mathematical reasoning tasks. We present results demonstrating significant improvements in accuracy metrics for multilingual models, particularly focusing on the impact of reference-anchoring coefficients.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in multilingual reasoning have highlighted the importance of cross-linguistic transfer in enhancing model performance. This study aims to explore the effectiveness of the RP-OPSD method in improving reasoning capabilities across languages, particularly in mathematical contexts.\n\n# Method\nWe evaluate the RP-OPSD method on various language-specific models, comparing their performance against unadapted baseline models. The models are assessed using two primary metrics: MGSM and PolyMath DW-ACC. The reference-anchoring coefficient λ is systematically varied to determine its impact on model performance.\n\n# Results\n## Generalization to Phi-4-mini-reasoning\nAs shown in Table 6, RP-OPSD improves both MGSM-family answer accuracy and PolyMath DW-ACC over the corresponding base model for both ZHO and SWA. The gains hold across two target languages and two complementary mathematical-reasoning evaluations on a non-Qwen backbone. This consistent cross-family pattern provides evidence that the benefits of RP-OPSD arise from its reasoning-transfer mechanism rather than from behavior specific to the Qwen model family.\n\n## Sensitivity to the Reference-Anchoring Coefficient\nTable 8 summarizes the effects of varying the reference-anchoring coefficient λ on Qwen3-1.7B for SWA. A moderate amount of reference anchoring is beneficial: setting λ = 0.2 improves pass@12 by 2.0 points over removing the anchoring term. However, increasing λ to 0.5 or 0.8 reduces pass@12 by 4.8 and 5.2 points, respectively, suggesting that overly strong anchoring can constrain the privileged-teacher signal at reasoning pivots.\n\n## Generalization beyond Mathematical Reasoning\nTable 7 evaluates whether the multilingual reasoning transfer induced by RP-OPSD generalizes beyond the training domain. Both language-specific models, trained exclusively on the same OpenThoughts mathematics problems, improve over the Qwen3-1.7B base model in most non-mathematical domains and in overall performance for both Russian and Spanish. These out-of-domain gains indicate that RP-OPSD transfers general reasoning capabilities effectively.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings of this study underscore the potential of the RP-OPSD method in enhancing multilingual reasoning capabilities, particularly in mathematical contexts. The results suggest that careful tuning of the reference-anchoring coefficient can lead to significant improvements in model performance across languages. Future work will explore further refinements to the method and its applicability to additional reasoning tasks.\n\n\n\n# Method\n\n## Performance Evaluation\n\nTable 7 presents the out-of-domain performance on the non-mathematical domains of MMLU-ProX in Russian (RUS) and Spanish (SPA). The "Base" model denotes Qwen3-1.7B, while "RP-OPSD" refers to the corresponding language-specific model trained solely on the same OpenThoughts mathematics problems utilized in the main experiments. The columns report performance across various subjects: physics, chemistry, biology, computer science (CS), engineering, economics, business, and overall performance.\n\n| Method      | RUS                                          | SPA                                          | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|             | Phys. | Chem. | Bio. | CS   | Eng. | Econ. | Bus. | Overall | Phys. | Chem. | Bio. | CS   | Eng. | Econ. | Bus. | Overall | |\n| Base        | 32.87 | 30.74 | 48.95 | 37.80 | 23.12 | 39.34 | 36.88 | 36.88  | 34.55 | 36.03 | 35.60 | 40.45 | 42.20 | 28.17 | 40.88 | 38.40 | 36.61 |\n| RP-OPSD     | 32.87 | 31.36 | 50.48 | 38.53 | 23.84 | 40.29 | 37.89 | 37.89  | 36.47 | 36.88 | 36.04 | 40.17 | 44.40 | 29.51 | 41.47 | 38.78 | 38.18 |\n\n## Prompt Design\n\nFigure 7 illustrates the ablated-teacher prompt. The left panel provides an English reference translation, with each prompt component displayed on a separate line. The right panel shows the instantiated Swahili (SWA) version. Unlike the full teacher, the ablated teacher does not receive the English reference solution.\n\n**English reference translation**:\nQuestion: [problem_target]\nEnglish translation of the question: [problem_english]\nPlease think step by step in Swahili, and place your final answer inside \\(\\boxed{}\\).\n\n**Ablated-teacher prompt (Swahili; SWA)**:\nSwali: [problem_target]\nTafsiri ya Kiingereza ya swali: [problem_english]\nTafadhali fikiri hatua kwa hatua kwa Kiswahili, na uweke jibu lako la mwisho ndani ya \\(\\boxed{}\\).\n\n## Sensitivity Analysis\n\nTable 8 shows the sensitivity to the reference-anchoring coefficient on AfriMGSM SWA with Qwen3-1.7B. The row for \\(\\lambda = 0\\) indicates the removal of reference anchoring, while \\(\\lambda = 0.2\\) is the setting employed by RP-OPSD.\n\n| Anchoring coefficient | SWA pass@12 (%) |\n|-----------------------|------------------|\n| \\(\\lambda = 0.0\\)     | 27.6             |\n| \\(\\lambda = 0.2\\)     | 29.6             |\n| \\(\\lambda = 0.5\\)     | 24.8             |\n| \\(\\lambda = 0.8\\)     | 24.4             |\n\n## Token-Chain Analysis\n\nFor each token–CoT pair, the gate-weighted TF-IDF score is defined as:\n\n\\[\nS_{\\text{rare}}(t, c) = \\log(1 + \\sum_{i} w_{c,i}) \\cdot \\text{idf}(t), \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(x_{c,i} = t\\), which favors locally high-gate tokens while discounting token types shared by many CoTs.\n\n### Common but Case-Specific Pivots\n\nCommon but case-specific pivots identify occurrences whose gate is unusually high in the current CoT relative to the same token type in other CoTs. We define the cross-CoT baseline as:\n\n\\[\n\\mu^{-c}(t) = \\frac{\\sum_{c' \\in C, c' \\neq c} 1[x_{c',j} = t] p_{c',j}}{\\sum_{c' \\in C} 1[x_{c',j} = t]} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand rank high-gate occurrences by the positive contextual residual:\n\n\\[\nS_{\\text{case}}(c, i) = w_{c,i} [p_{c,i} - \\mu^{-c}(x_{c,i})]^{+}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\n\n# Abstract\n\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the training hyperparameters and prompt templates used in the RP-OPSD model. We detail the optimization strategies, learning rates, and other critical parameters that influence the model's performance across various languages.\n\n# Introduction\n\nThe RP-OPSD model builds upon the foundational work of COPSD (Liu et al., 2026b) by incorporating advanced techniques such as RPT gates and anchoring. This study aims to elucidate the specific configurations that enhance the model's adaptability and efficiency in processing language-specific tasks.\n\n# Method\n\n## Training Hyperparameters\n\nThe training hyperparameters for the RP-OPSD model are summarized in Table 9. These parameters were selected based on their effectiveness in previous studies and tailored to optimize the model's performance.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|c|l|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Parameter} &#x26; \\text{Value} &#x26; \\text{Parameter} &#x26; \\text{Value} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Optimization and adaptation} &#x26; \\text{On-policy rollout} &#x26; \\text{Learning Rate} &#x26; 5 \\times 10^{-6} \\\\\n\\text{Effective Batch Size} &#x26; 32 &#x26; \\text{Max. Completion Length} &#x26; 2,048 \\\\\n\\text{Generations / Prompt} &#x26; 1 &#x26; \\text{Sampling Temp.} &#x26; 1.1 \\\\\n\\text{Optimizer} &#x26; \\text{AdamW} &#x26; \\text{Top-p} &#x26; 0.95 \\\\\n\\text{Numerical Precision} &#x26; \\text{bfloat16} &#x26; \\text{Top-k} &#x26; 20 \\\\\n\\text{Max. Gradient Norm} &#x26; 0.1 &#x26; \\text{LoRA Rank (r)} &#x26; 64 \\\\\n\\text{LoRA Alpha (α)} &#x26; 128 &#x26; \\text{Training Steps} &#x26; \\text{N/A} \\\\\n\\text{EMA Decay} &#x26; 0.99 &#x26; \\text{Gate Normalization EMA z-score} &#x26; \\text{N/A} \\\\\n\\text{Score Clipping} &#x26; [-5, 5] &#x26; \\text{Gate Sharpness (β)} &#x26; 2.0 \\\\\n\\text{Gate Threshold (τ)} &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; \\text{Min. Gate (gmin)} &#x26; 0.05 \\\\\n\\text{Anchoring Coefficient (λ)} &#x26; 0.2 &#x26; \\text{Gate Warmup} &#x26; 5\\% \\text{ uniform + 5\\% interpolation} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n# Results\n\n## Prompt Templates\n\nThe prompt templates utilized in the RP-OPSD model include a student prompt, a full-teacher prompt, and a language-specific reasoning prefix, all of which adhere to the structure established by COPSD (Liu et al., 2026b). The ablated-teacher prompt retains essential components such as the target-language problem and its English translation, while omitting the English reference solution and its corresponding instruction.\n\nFigure 7 illustrates the resulting template, using Swahili (SWA) as an example. This visual representation aids in understanding the structural differences between the various prompt configurations.\n\n# Conclusion\n\nThe careful selection of training hyperparameters and prompt templates is crucial for the success of the RP-OPSD model. By building on the methodologies established in previous research, this study contributes to the ongoing development of more effective language processing models. Further research will explore the implications of these configurations on model performance across diverse linguistic contexts.	[]	[]	58547	2026-08-27 21:43:10.88865+00	2026-08-27 21:43:10.88865+00
