15	21	llamaparse	# Beyond Top-K: Replacing Black-Box Retrieval with Interpretable Agentic Operations\n\n**Sagar Tamang** ¹², **Ayush Vyas** ², **Tabarakul Hazarika** ²\n\n----\n\n**Abstract**\nRetrieval-augmented generation over long documents is dominated by one design: chunk the text, embed the chunks, and surface the top-k nearest neighbors of the query. We argue that for an important class of documents — financial statements, audit reports, regulatory returns — this design is structurally unsound, and we make the argument measurable. On a 780-page government financial report, 86.8% of content lines are table rows, thousands of near-identical figures compete in one embedding space, and a figure inherits its unit from a header a median of 13 lines above it — so a chunk boundary routinely separates a number from whether it is in lakh or crore, an error of two orders of magnitude. A table-aware chunking method built as a steelman fixes the unit problem but leaves 27–30% of numeric chunks with no fiscal year header at every chunk size we tried. We propose READ (Reliable Embedding-free Agentic Document-search), in which an agent reads the raw document through three deterministic operations — normalized lexical search, structural navigation, and bounded span reads — exposed over the Model Context Protocol, so a trajectory is a replayable audit trail, not an opaque similarity score. On 51 verified questions, READ answers 58.8% against dense retrieval’s 15.7% (pHolm = 2 × 10⁻⁵) — or 35.3% tuned, which READ still leads by 23.5 points (pHolm = 0.017). An agent given the same loop but a top-k tool reaches only 27.5%, locating the gain in the interface rather than in iteration. We also report what the evidence does not support: BM25 is statistically indistinguishable from READ, so our result separates embedding-based from embedding-free retrieval, not agentic from lexical search.\n\n1 Indian Institute of Technology Patna, Bihta, India\n2 TwoSpoon, India. Correspondence to: Sagar Tamang <sagar pa2508mch184@iitp.ac.in="">.\nPreprint. August 7, 2026.\n\n----\n\n## Introduction\n\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has become a prevalent approach for processing long documents, particularly in domains where the information is dense and structured, such as financial reporting. The conventional method involves chunking the text into manageable pieces, embedding these chunks, and retrieving the top-k nearest neighbors based on a query. However, this approach has significant limitations, especially when dealing with documents that contain structured data, such as tables and figures.\n\nIn this paper, we focus on the challenges posed by financial statements and similar documents, where the context of numerical data is crucial for accurate interpretation. We argue that the conventional RAG design is inadequate for these types of documents due to the inherent structural issues that arise from chunking. Specifically, we highlight the problem of chunk boundaries separating critical contextual information, leading to potential misinterpretations of numerical values.\n\n## Method\n\nTo address these challenges, we propose a new framework called READ (Reliable Embedding-free Agentic Document-search). This framework allows an agent to interact with the raw document through three key operations:\n\n1. **Normalized Lexical Search**: This operation enables the agent to perform searches based on the normalized text, ensuring that variations in terminology do not hinder retrieval.\n\n2. **Structural Navigation**: This operation allows the agent to navigate the document's structure, such as headings and sections, to locate relevant information more effectively.\n\n3. **Bounded Span Reads**: This operation permits the agent to read specific spans of text, ensuring that it captures the necessary context around numerical data.\n\nThese operations are exposed over the Model Context Protocol, which provides a transparent and replayable audit trail of the agent's decision-making process.\n\n## Results\n\nWe evaluated the performance of READ against conventional dense retrieval methods on a 780-page government financial report. Our findings indicate that READ significantly outperforms dense retrieval methods. Specifically, READ achieved an accuracy of 58.8% on 51 verified questions, compared to only 15.7% for dense retrieval (pHolm = 2 × 10⁻⁵). Even when tuning the dense retrieval method, which reached a maximum accuracy of 35.3%, READ still led by 23.5 points (pHolm = 0.017).\n\nFurthermore, we explored the performance of an agent using a top-k tool under the same conditions, which yielded an accuracy of only 27.5%. This result underscores the importance of the interface design in facilitating effective retrieval, rather than merely relying on iterative improvements.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nOur research demonstrates that conventional retrieval methods are insufficient for processing structured documents like financial statements. The READ framework, with its agentic operations, provides a more reliable and interpretable approach to document retrieval. By separating embedding-based from embedding-free retrieval, we highlight the potential for improved accuracy and understanding in the context of complex documents.\n\n----\n\n**Figure 1**. Two retrieval interfaces over the same document. The conventional pipeline commits to a partition before the query is known, so a figure can arrive without the header that states its unit and fiscal year — on this document that separation survives every chunk size we tried (Section 4). READ leaves the document intact and lets the agent choose what to read next, so each step is a citable line range rather than a similarity score. Figures at right are accuracy, in percent correct, over the same 51 verified questions: 15.7% for dense top-k against 58.8% for READ (Section 8). Tuning the dense baseline’s chunk size and retrieval depth raises it to 35.3% at best, which READ still leads by 23.5 points (Section D).</sagar>\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has become the standard approach for grounding language models in external knowledge. However, this paper identifies a class of documents—such as financial statements and regulatory filings—where traditional retrieval methods fail due to structural issues. We present a comprehensive analysis of these documents, highlighting their tabular nature, the prevalence of near-duplicate numbers, and the importance of layout for meaning. Our contributions include a measured characterization of these documents, the development of a robust chunking method, and the introduction of READ, an embedding-free retrieval interface that directly engages with the document content.\n\n## Introduction\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is the default recipe for grounding language models in external knowledge (Lewis et al., 2020; Gao et al., 2024). A corpus is split into chunks, each mapped to a dense vector (Karpukhin et al., 2020), and at query time, the top-k nearest neighbors are concatenated into the prompt. A decade of work has refined every stage—chunking policies, embedding models, sparse–dense fusion, reranking (Wang et al., 2024; Cormack et al., 2009; Khattab &#x26; Zaharia, 2020)—but the contract has not changed: the model sees a small, similarity-ranked slice of the corpus, selected by a mechanism it cannot inspect, steer, or verify.\n\nThis paper is about a class of documents for which that contract fails, and about why the failure is structural rather than a matter of tuning. The class is large: financial statements, audit reports, statutory filings, and regulatory returns, which share three properties—they are overwhelmingly tabular, they contain thousands of near-duplicate numbers, and they carry meaning in layout, where a figure’s units and fiscal year are declared in a header some distance above it. Consider the question that motivated this work, asked of the Gujarat Finance Accounts 2024–25, Volume I: what is the difference between the Fiscal Deficit and the Revenue Surplus for 2024–25? The operands sit two lines apart in the same statement, and their difference appears nowhere in the document—it must be computed. In a pilot run (Section 7), a tuned dense retriever failed to place both operands in the same top-k context on any trial, and the generator correctly reported that it could not answer. This is not a hallucination; it is retrieval declining to deliver, and it is the best case. The worse case is a retriever that surfaces one plausible figure and a fluent generator that uses it.\n\nWe make three moves. First, we measure the setting rather than assert it: Section 4 quantifies tabularity, numeric near-duplication, and the distance between a figure and its governing unit header, and then measures how much of that context chunking destroys. Second, we build the strongest chunker we can—one that carries unit and fiscal-year headers across every boundary—so that what remains is a property of the paradigm rather than of a lazy implementation. Third, we describe READ—Reliable Embedding-free Agentic Document-search—an interface in which a tool-calling agent reads the document directly, and we compare it against dense, sparse, and hybrid baselines under a paired protocol.\n\nOur contributions:\n- A measured characterization of exactness-critical documents. We quantify the properties that defeat chunk-and-embed retrieval on a real 780-page financial report.\n- A steelmanned baseline, and the residue it cannot fix. Table-aware chunking cuts unitless chunks from 18.0% to 0.3%, but 28.9% of numeric chunks still carry no fiscal-year header (Table 2). We report the strong configuration throughout.\n- READ, an embedding-free retrieval interface, released as an MCP server, together with the finding that normalized lexical matching is what makes it viable on converted PDFs—and a measurement of exactly which conversion artifacts make it so (Section 5.2).\n- A conversion-fidelity ceiling. We quantify the damage the PDF converter inflicts (Table 4) and show it is a bound shared by every retrieval method, requiring its own question category rather than being attributed to a retriever (Section 5.3).\n- An evaluation protocol with three independent grading signals, including a mechanical groundedness check that a fluent-but-unsupported answer cannot pass (Section 6.3).\n\n## Related Work\n\n### 2.1. RAG and Its Moving Parts\nRAG couples a retriever with a generator so outputs are conditioned on retrieved evidence (Lewis et al., 2020). Dense passage retrieval established learned dual encoders as the dominant retriever (Karpukhin et al., 2020), refined by late-interaction models (Khattab &#x26; Zaharia, 2020), learned sparse expansion (Formal et al., 2021), and unsupervised contrastive encoders (Izacard et al., 2022). The BEIR benchmark showed that BM25 (Robertson et al., 1995; Thakur et al., 2021) remains a strong zero-shot baseline against far heavier neural retrievers—an early signal that lexical matching is harder to beat than assumed.\n\nIn deployment, the retriever is one of many coupled components. Wang et al. (2024) catalogue the design space and find end-to-end quality highly sensitive to each choice. Two fragilities matter here. Position effects: models under-use evidence in the middle of long contexts (Liu et al., 2024), so a retriever that surfaces the right chunk may still not deliver it usefully. Behavioural drift: the services powering embedding and generation change opaquely over time (Chen et al., 2024), so an index built against one model version silently degrades against another. Adaptive schemes such as Self-RAG teach the model when to retrieve and to critique what it retrieved (Asai et al., 2023), but keep the index and its maintenance burden.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content not provided in the current text.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content not provided in the current text.]\n\n## Method\n\n### 2.2. Long and Structured Documents\nLong-document QA exposes the weakest link of chunk-based RAG: the chunker. Financial statements encode meaning in layout — row/column adjacency, multi-page tables, header inheritance — which fixed-size chunking destroys. Work on spreadsheets illustrates the escalating machinery required to compensate: SpreadsheetLLM compresses sheets to fit context windows (Dong et al., 2024), while FRTR builds multi-granular row, column, and block embeddings fused by reciprocal rank fusion to reach usable accuracy on enterprise workbooks (Gulati et al., 2026). These systems show that structure-aware retrieval can be built, at the price of ever more elaborate corpus-specific indexing. We take the opposite route: leave the document intact and give the agent operations expressive enough to exploit its structure directly.\n\nAn alternative response is to dispense with retrieval and rely on long context. Our document is 1.68 MB and fits a 1M-token window, so this is feasible here; but it remains costly, exhibits positional degradation (Liu et al., 2024), and does not scale to corpora that keep growing. We include it as a baseline rather than dismissing it.\n\n### 2.3. Agentic Retrieval and Tool Use\nA separate line makes retrieval iterative. Multi-hop systems learn to retrieve reasoning paths rather than isolated passages (Asai et al., 2020; Trivedi et al., 2023), and ReAct-style agents interleave reasoning with search (Yao et al., 2023; Schick et al., 2023). The competence of models at operating software (Chen et al., 2021) underwrites a stronger version: SWE-agent showed a bash-equipped agent navigates million-line repositories (Yang et al., 2024), and Agentless showed a fixed grep-and-read pipeline rivals full agent frameworks (Xia et al., 2024). Memory-augmented conversational agents such as Chronos likewise replace opaque recall with structured, searchable stores (Sen et al., 2026b). The Model Context Protocol (Anthropic, 2024) standardizes how such tools are exposed. READ is an MCP server whose tools target a single long document rather than a repository.\n\n#### Interface A: embedding-mediated top-k\nAn offline pipeline partitions \\( D \\) into chunks \\( \\{c_j\\}^M_{j=1} \\), embeds each as \\( v_j = E(c_j) \\in \\mathbb{R}^d \\), and returns\n\n\\[\nR(q) = \\text{arg top } k \\frac{E(q) \\cdot v_j}{\\|E(q)\\| \\|v_j\\|} \\quad (1)\n\\]\n\nThree properties follow. Lossy projection: all evidence available to selection is compressed into \\( d \\) floats, so distinctions below the resolution of \\( E \\) are unrecoverable at selection time. Fixed budget: exactly \\( k \\) chunks are surfaced whether the question needs one cell or forty pages. Opacity: the only account of why \\( c_j \\) was chosen is a scalar.\n\nA fourth property is specific to structured documents and is the one we quantify. Chunking is a partition of \\( D \\), but \\( u \\) is defined by inheritance from preceding lines. A chunk \\( c_j \\) spanning lines \\([a, b]\\) is unit-preserving only if some line in \\([a, b]\\) declares \\( u(b) \\). Nothing in Equation (1) enforces this, and Section 4 shows how often it fails.\n\n#### Interface B: interpretable agentic operations\nREAD exposes \\( D \\) through deterministic operations:\n- **SEARCH(p)** → \\(\\{(i, \\ell_i, \\text{loc}(i))\\}\\): all lines matching pattern \\( p \\).\n\n## Results\n[Results content not provided in the current text.]\n\n## Discussion\n[Discussion content not provided in the current text.]\n\n## Conclusion\n[Conclusion content not provided in the current text.]\n\n## References\n- Dong et al., 2024\n- Gulati et al., 2026\n- Liu et al., 2024\n- Asai et al., 2020\n- Trivedi et al., 2023\n- Yao et al., 2023\n- Schick et al., 2023\n- Chen et al., 2021\n- Yang et al., 2024\n- Xia et al., 2024\n- Sen et al., 2026b\n- Anthropic, 2024\n\n[Note: The sections not provided in the current text have been marked accordingly. Please provide additional content if available for a more complete representation.]\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper explores the limitations of traditional document retrieval methods, particularly in the context of tabular data. We propose a novel approach that emphasizes interpretability and agentic operations, allowing for more effective extraction of information from complex documents. Our method leverages a closed-loop system that hypothesizes, searches, inspects, and reads spans of text to ensure accurate retrieval of data, particularly numeric values that are contextually dependent on their headers.\n\n## Introduction\nDocument retrieval systems have primarily focused on top-K retrieval methods, which often overlook the nuances of structured data, such as tables. This paper addresses the challenges posed by tabular documents, where the context of numeric values is critical for accurate interpretation. We introduce a new framework that enhances the retrieval process by incorporating agentic operations that allow for dynamic interaction with the document structure.\n\n## Method\nOur method consists of several key operations:\n- **OUTLINE() → S(D)**: This operation retrieves statements and sections along with their line and page extents.\n- **READ(a, b) → (ℓa, ..., ℓb)**: This function allows the agent to read a bounded contiguous span of text, enabling it to capture relevant headers and context.\n\nThe agent operates in a closed loop, hypothesizing a lexical anchor, searching for relevant information, inspecting hits against the outline, and reading spans to either extract answers or refine its search. A trajectory is defined as a sequence \\( \\tau = (o_1, r_1, ..., o_T, r_T) \\) that terminates in an answer with cited locations.\n\n### Determinism and Auditability\nEach operation \\( o_T \\) is a pure function of the document \\( D \\) and its arguments, ensuring that re-executing \\( \\tau \\) reproduces the same results. This allows for third-party verification of the evidence chain. An answer is considered grounded if each figure it contains appears verbatim in some span surfaced in \\( \\tau \\).\n\n## Results\n### The Setting, Measured\nOur testbed consists of the Gujarat Finance Accounts 2024–25, a comprehensive financial statement of an Indian state government. The document spans 780 printed pages and is predominantly tabular, with a significant number of numeric tokens.\n\n#### Table 1. Corpus Profile\nThe document is overwhelmingly tabular, with 86.8% of non-empty lines being table rows. The numeric tokens exhibit a high repetition ratio, indicating that many values are near-duplicates.\n\n### What Chunking Destroys\nWe analyze the impact of chunking on the retrieval of numeric data. We define a chunk \\( c \\) as unitless if it contains no unit declaration and yearless if it lacks a fiscal-year column header. Our findings indicate that naive chunking significantly degrades retrieval accuracy, particularly at smaller chunk sizes.\n\n#### Table 2. Chunking Analysis\nThe results demonstrate that smaller chunks often fail to retain the necessary context for accurate interpretation of numeric values. This highlights the importance of maintaining structural integrity in document retrieval processes.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur research underscores the necessity of developing retrieval methods that are sensitive to the structural and contextual elements of documents, particularly in tabular formats. By employing agentic operations and ensuring the interpretability of retrieved data, we can enhance the accuracy and reliability of document retrieval systems. Future work will focus on refining these methods and exploring their applicability across various document types.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we explore the limitations of traditional top-K retrieval methods in document retrieval tasks and propose a novel approach that emphasizes interpretability and agentic operations. Our method leverages a table-aware chunking strategy to enhance the retrieval of relevant information while maintaining contextual integrity. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach through empirical evaluations and provide insights into the implications for future research in document retrieval.\n\n## Introduction\nDocument retrieval systems have traditionally relied on top-K ranking methods to return the most relevant documents based on user queries. However, these methods often lack interpretability and can lead to the loss of contextual information, particularly in structured documents such as tables. In this work, we introduce a new framework that goes beyond top-K retrieval by incorporating agentic operations that allow for more nuanced interactions with document content.\n\n## Method\n### 5.1 Implementation\nOur system, named READ (Reliable Embedding-free Agentic Document-search), operates as a read-only MCP server (Anthropic, 2024) over a directory of Markdown files. It exposes three operation classes as four tools: grep (normalized line search), list (files with sizes), outline (headings with line numbers), and read (numbered lines, capped at 400 per call). Each tool is restricted to a root directory and is designed to prevent excessive context flooding. Notably, our approach does not utilize embedding models, vector indices, or learned components; the server's entire state is derived from the document text.\n\n### 5.2 Why Normalized Matching\nA direct grep over converted PDF text is significantly less effective than over clean text, a fact that has not been adequately addressed in previous agentic-search research, which typically evaluates on already-clean corpora. Our conversion process introduces various artifacts, including spurious leading empty columns, emphasis markers, and inconsistent spellings of statement numbers. To mitigate these issues, READ employs a normalized matching strategy that creates two versions of each line: one with emphasis and cell boundaries replaced by spaces, and another with all separators and thousands-commas removed. This normalization allows for more accurate matching while preserving original line numbers for result chaining.\n\n## Results\nWe evaluated the performance of our table-aware chunking strategy across a range of chunk sizes, as shown in Table 2. The results indicate that fixed-size chunking leads to a significant degradation in context preservation as chunk sizes decrease. In contrast, our table-aware chunker effectively maintains the governing unit and fiscal-year rows across boundaries, drastically reducing the number of unitless chunks.\n\n### Table 2. Context destroyed by chunking, over a 5× range of chunk size.\n| Chunk Size | Fixed-size Unitless | Fixed-size Yearless | Table-aware Unitless | Table-aware Yearless |\n|------------|---------------------|---------------------|-----------------------|-----------------------|\n| 800        | 67.4%               | 73.8%               | 14.7%                 | 30.1%                 |\n| 1,500      | 36.3%               | 55.8%               | 1.0%                  | 28.7%                 |\n| 2,000      | 18.0%               | 45.9%               | 0.3%                  | 28.9%                 |\n| 2,500      | 11.6%               | 43.5%               | 0.2%                  | 28.3%                 |\n| 4,000      | 5.4%                | 40.5%               | 0.4%                  | 27.2%                 |\n\nThe table demonstrates that while fixed-size chunking suffers from context loss as chunk sizes shrink, our table-aware chunking method nearly eliminates unitless chunks and maintains a consistent level of yearless residue across varying chunk sizes.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur findings highlight the importance of interpretability and context preservation in document retrieval systems. By moving beyond traditional top-K methods and employing agentic operations, we can enhance the retrieval process and provide users with more meaningful interactions with document content. Future work will focus on further refining our approach and exploring its applicability across different document types and retrieval tasks.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper explores the limitations of traditional top-K retrieval methods in document retrieval systems, particularly in the context of numeric data. We introduce a novel approach that emphasizes interpretability and agentic operations, allowing for more effective handling of complex queries involving figures and statements.\n\n## Introduction\nDocument retrieval systems often rely on top-K algorithms to return the most relevant results based on user queries. However, these systems can struggle with numeric data, especially when formatting issues arise. This paper investigates the impact of normalization and formatting on retrieval accuracy, particularly in financial documents where figures are critical.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments to evaluate the sensitivity of document retrieval systems to various types of formatting issues. Our focus was on numeric data, specifically examining how different representations of figures affect retrieval outcomes. We categorized the types of formatting errors encountered and analyzed their impact on retrieval performance.\n\n### Table 3. Matching sensitivity (7 of 12 probes shown)\n| Query                 | Kind                  | Norm. | Literal |\n|-----------------------|-----------------------|-------|---------|\n| 48964.89              | figure, ungrouped     | ✓     | ✗       |\n| Statement Subject      | split across cells    | ✓     | ✗       |\n| 48,964.89             | figure, as printed    | ✓     | ✓       |\n| Fiscal Deficit        | inside **. . . **     | ✓     | ✓       |\n| fiscal deficit        | lowercase             | ✓     | ✓       |\n| Statement No. 17      | statement number      | ✓     | ✓       |\n| Co-operation          | row label             | ✓     | ✓       |\n\nThe first row is the important one. On this document, the agent’s queries are figures — reverse lookups, cross-checks, verification of a computed value — and an agent that has derived or recalled a number writes it without separators. Digit grouping is precisely what breaks that search, and 14,269 of the document’s numbers carry it. We report this narrower mechanism rather than the broader one we first assumed, and we retain read naive grep as an ablation that isolates it.\n\n## Results\n### Table 4. Conversion damage in the corpus\n| Class                                      | Count |\n|--------------------------------------------|-------|\n| Suspect bare integer (missing decimal)     | 174   |\n| Orphaned separator cell                    | 139   |\n| Word split across cells                    | 133   |\n| Detached separator (owner identified)      | 22    |\n| Decimal detached from its digits           | 15    |\n| Total instances                            | 483   |\n| Table rows affected                        | 440 (2.6%) |\n| Numeric rows affected                      | 381 (3.0%) |\n| Numeric rows with a magnitude error        | 11 (0.09%) |\n\nEvery retrieval comparison on a converted PDF silently assumes the text is a faithful rendering of the document. It is not, and the gap is neither uniform noise nor negligible: it is structured, it is measurable, and where it bites it changes a figure by two orders of magnitude.\n\nThe clearest case is Statement No. 18, which lists loans sanctioned in perpetuity. The printed interest rates are 11.00 and 10.00 per cent. The conversion places the digits in one table cell and the decimal point in the cell below:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n16458: &#x26; \\ldots |500.00| **1100** | \\\\\n16459: &#x26; \\ldots |471.50| **.** | \\\\\n16460: &#x26; \\ldots |500.00| **1000** | \\\\\n16461: &#x26; \\ldots |500.00| **.** | \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nAn agent that retrieves these lines perfectly still reads 1100 where the document prints 11.00. The same pattern appears in Statement No. 2, where the Consolidated Fund deficit Rs. 26,837.65 crore is split into the digit run 2683765 on one line and the separators , . on the next, so the well-formed figure occurs nowhere in the corpus.\n\nIt reframes what a retrieval failure means. In an informal evaluation of this document, a system was judged to have failed on the Statement No. 18 question. Inspection showed it had retrieved exactly the right lines and read them faithfully; the source text was wrong. Distinguishing “did not find the evidence” from “found evidence that the conversion had already destroyed” requires the trajectory, which is available for READ and for top-k systems alike but not for a system that reports only an answer.\n\nIt is rare but concentrated. Only 3.0% of numeric rows are affected and 0.09% carry an outright magnitude error, so this is not a pervasive degradation. But the affected rows cluster: a question that happens to target Statement No. 18 is far more likely to be unanswerable-as-converted than the aggregate rate suggests.\n\nA second observation from the same evaluation is worth recording because it is not a failure. Statement No. 18 is denominated in lakh while most aggregate statements are in crore, so the same amount is correctly reported as either Rs. 10,597.61 lakh or Rs. 105.98 crore. A grader that does not normalize units scores one of these wrong.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n### 6. Evaluation Protocol\n#### 6.1. Systems\nAll systems share one backbone, one answer format, and one accounting of tokens, cost, and latency, so a difference between them is a difference of interface. Two entries carry the argument. READlit isolates normalized matching (Section 5.2). AgenticVec is the critical control: an agent with the same loop and budget but a vector-search tool instead of READ’s operations. If READ’s advantage came merely from being able to iterate, AgenticVec would close the gap; if it comes from the interface, it will not. Without this control, any result is attributable to iteration alone.\n\nRetrieval baselines use the table-aware chunker (Table 2) and a local bge-base-en-v1.5 encoder. Embeddings run locally so the baseline does not drift when a hosted embedding endpoint updates (Chen et al., 2024); Section D reports the chunk-size and k sweep, and we report the best dense configuration found rather than the first.\n\n#### 6.2. Benchmark\nQuestions span six categories: single-figure lookup, multi-row aggregation, cross-statement arithmetic, structural navigation, unanswerable probes, and conversion-limited items whose answer the converter destroyed (Section 5.3). Gold answers are transcribed from the document by the authors and then checked mechanically: every expected figure must appear within one line of its declared gold line, and the column mapping of each source table is verified against an arithmetic identity the table itself satisfies rather than against our reading of its header (Section C). Arithmetic questions carry both the derived answer and its operands, and a validator asserts that the derived value does not appear anywhere in the corpus — otherwise the question is a lookup in disguise. No model generates or grades a gold answer.\n\n### 6.3. Metrics\nOur numeric matcher normalizes lakh, crore, and thousand before comparison for exactly this reason (Section 6.3). Three independent signals are used, because no one of them is trustworthy alone.\n\n1. **Exact-figure accuracy**: Deterministic numeric comparison after normalizing Indian digit grouping, parenthesized and (-) negatives, and lakh/crore units, at 0.5% relative tolerance — enough to equate \\(48,964.89\\) and \\(48,964.9\\) while separating \\(48,964\\) from \\(18,942\\).\n\n2. **LLM judge**: A grader with the reference answer in hand, held fixed across systems, emitting a structured verdict. We report agreement with the deterministic signal; disagreements are read by hand.\n\n3. **Groundedness**: Does every figure in the answer appear in text the system actually retrieved or read? This is purely mechanical, and it is the metric a fluent-but-unsupported answer cannot pass. It is computable for READ and for top-k systems alike, since both expose exactly what they surfaced.\n\nWe additionally report hallucination rate on unanswerable probes, page-level evidence coverage, tool-call counts, tokens, cost, and latency. Comparisons are paired: exact McNemar on discordant pairs, bootstrap confidence intervals on the delta, Holm-corrected across the family of baselines. We report the benchmark’s minimum detectable difference so that a null result is reported as underpowered rather than as no difference.\n\n## Results\n### 7. Pilot Observations\nBefore the full evaluation, we ran a two-question pilot to exercise the harness. We report it because it makes the mechanism concrete, and we state plainly that \\(n=2\\) supports no quantitative claim. The dense baseline abstained on every cell, reporting that the Fiscal Deficit was not present in its retrieved excerpts. The two operands lie on lines 385 and 432 — 47 lines apart, in the same statement — and no top-8 slice of 2,000-character.\n\n[Results content continues here.]\n\n## Conclusion\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n[References content goes here.]\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates the effectiveness of agentic operations in document retrieval systems, particularly focusing on the limitations of traditional top-k retrieval methods. We present a comparative analysis of various retrieval systems, highlighting the performance of our proposed method against established benchmarks.\n\n## Introduction\nThe advent of advanced retrieval systems has led to a growing interest in understanding the interpretability and efficiency of these systems. Traditional top-k retrieval methods, while effective, often fall short in providing comprehensive answers to complex queries. This study aims to explore the potential of agentic operations to enhance document retrieval performance and interpretability.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments using two backbone systems to evaluate the performance of our proposed retrieval method, READ, against a dense baseline and BM25. The evaluation was based on a set of 51 questions, with a focus on accuracy and interpretability.\n\n## Results\n### 8.1 Main Comparison\nREAD achieved an accuracy of 58.8% on the benchmark, significantly outperforming the dense retrieval method, which scored only 15.7%. The paired difference of 43.1 points was statistically significant (pHolm = 2 × 10⁻⁵, see Table 8). Furthermore, READ also outperformed a hybrid pipeline by 29.4 points (p = 0.016).\n\n### 8.2 What Does Not Support a Stronger Claim\nDespite the promising results, BM25 remains a strong competitor, achieving an accuracy of 51.0%, which is only 7.8 points below READ. The confidence interval for this comparison spans zero (p = 1.00), indicating that the difference is not statistically significant. The analysis revealed that of 22 discordant questions, 13 favored READ while 9 favored BM25, suggesting that the evidence does not definitively separate embedding-based retrieval from embedding-free retrieval.\n\n## Discussion\nThe findings indicate that while agentic operations can enhance retrieval performance, the cost and complexity of these methods must be carefully considered. The results also highlight the importance of methodological rigor in evaluating retrieval systems, as parsing errors can lead to misleading conclusions.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur study demonstrates that agentic operations can improve document retrieval performance, but further research is needed to fully understand their implications and to refine the methodologies used in such evaluations. The results suggest that while READ shows promise, it is not without its limitations, particularly when compared to established methods like BM25.\n\n## References\n- [1] Table 6: Pilot on the motivating question, two backbones.\n- [2] Table 8: Comparison of retrieval systems.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n[Method content goes here.]\n\n## Results\n\n### Table 7: Accuracy and Behaviour Over 51 Questions\nGrounded is the fraction of answers in which every figure stated appears in text the system actually retrieved or read. Halluc. is the fraction of the five unanswerable probes answered rather than declined. Intervals are Wilson 95%.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{System} &#x26; \\text{Acc. (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{95\\% CI} &#x26; \\text{Grounded} &#x26; \\text{Halluc.} &#x26; \\text{\\$/q} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{READ and its ablations} &#x26; &#x26; &#x26; &#x26; &#x26; \\\\\n\\text{no-outline} &#x26; 66.7 &#x26; [53.0, 78.0] &#x26; 66.7 &#x26; 40 &#x26; 0.040 \\\\\n\\text{naive-grep} &#x26; 66.7 &#x26; [53.0, 78.0] &#x26; 60.4 &#x26; 0 &#x26; 0.043 \\\\\n\\text{READ} &#x26; 58.8 &#x26; [45.2, 71.2] &#x26; 58.0 &#x26; 40 &#x26; 0.058 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Retrieval baselines} &#x26; &#x26; &#x26; &#x26; &#x26; \\\\\n\\text{BM25} &#x26; 51.0 &#x26; [37.7, 64.1] &#x26; 67.3 &#x26; 40 &#x26; 0.020 \\\\\n\\text{hybrid} &#x26; 29.4 &#x26; [18.7, 43.0] &#x26; 72.0 &#x26; 60 &#x26; 0.022 \\\\\n\\text{agentic RAG} &#x26; 27.5 &#x26; [17.1, 40.9] &#x26; 42.6 &#x26; 0 &#x26; 0.050 \\\\\n\\text{dense} &#x26; 15.7 &#x26; [8.2, 28.0] &#x26; 47.9 &#x26; 80 &#x26; 0.023 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n### Table 8: Paired Comparisons Against READ\nPositive ∆ favours READ. p is exact McNemar, Holm-corrected over the six comparisons. Dense here is the configuration of Section 6 (2,000-character table-aware chunks, k=8); Section D sweeps that configuration and reports the comparison against dense’s best setting.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{vs.} &#x26; \\Delta (\\text{pp}) &#x26; \\text{95\\% CI} &#x26; p_{\\text{Holm}} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{dense} &#x26; +43.1 &#x26; [27.5, 56.9] &#x26; 0.00002^{*} \\\\\n\\text{agentic RAG} &#x26; +31.4 &#x26; [13.7, 49.0] &#x26; 0.012^{*} \\\\\n\\text{hybrid} &#x26; +29.4 &#x26; [11.8, 47.1] &#x26; 0.016^{*} \\\\\n\\text{BM25} &#x26; +7.8 &#x26; [-9.8, 25.5] &#x26; 1.00 \\\\\n\\text{naive-grep} &#x26; -7.8 &#x26; [-23.5, 7.8] &#x26; 1.00 \\\\\n\\text{no-outline} &#x26; -7.8 &#x26; [-21.6, 5.9] &#x26; 1.00 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n### Observations\nThe gap is concentrated in lookup, the largest category: 70.8% for READ against 12.5% for dense (Table 9, Figure 4). These are the questions that ask for one printed figure from a named row and column, which is precisely where thousands of near-identical numbers compete in one embedding space (Section 4). On navigation — questions about which statement, which page, which unit — every system does comparatively well, because the answer is carried by prose and headings rather than by digits.\n\n### Cost\nREAD is the most expensive system per question ($0.058 against dense’s $0.023) and the slowest (31.4 s against 12.7 s), because it makes 4.8 tool calls per question and dense retrieval scores 15.7% on this document — we claim it scores 35.3% when tuned, at 41% higher cost per question, and that READ still beats that configuration by 23.5 points (p_{\\text{Holm}} = 0.017).\n\n## Conclusion\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n[References content goes here.]\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n[Method content goes here.]\n\n## Results\n### Accuracy by Category\nTable 9 presents the accuracy percentages by category for various systems. Conv.-lim. questions have evidence damaged in PDF conversion (Section 5.3); no system can be expected to reach 100%.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{System} &#x26; \\text{Look.} &#x26; \\text{Arith.} &#x26; \\text{Agg.} &#x26; \\text{Nav.} &#x26; \\text{Conv.-lim.} &#x26; \\text{Unans.} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{No-outline} &#x26; 70.8 &#x26; 33.3 &#x26; 100.0 &#x26; 83.3 &#x26; 50.0 &#x26; 60.0 \\\\\n\\text{Naive-grep} &#x26; 75.0 &#x26; 33.3 &#x26; 60.0 &#x26; 66.7 &#x26; 50.0 &#x26; 100.0 \\\\\n\\text{READ} &#x26; 70.8 &#x26; 44.4 &#x26; 40.0 &#x26; 50.0 &#x26; 50.0 &#x26; 60.0 \\\\\n\\text{BM25} &#x26; 62.5 &#x26; 33.3 &#x26; 20.0 &#x26; 66.7 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 60.0 \\\\\n\\text{Hybrid} &#x26; 20.8 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 60.0 &#x26; 66.7 &#x26; 50.0 &#x26; 40.0 \\\\\n\\text{Agentic RAG} &#x26; 16.7 &#x26; 11.1 &#x26; 20.0 &#x26; 50.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 100.0 \\\\\n\\text{Dense} &#x26; 12.5 &#x26; 11.1 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 50.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 20.0 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n### Failure Taxonomy\nTable 10 shows the failure taxonomy, which counts the failing cells by cause over 51 questions. "Never surfaced" means the gold evidence never entered the model’s context.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{System} &#x26; \\text{Never Surfaced} &#x26; \\text{Abstained (Answerable)} &#x26; \\text{Ungrounded} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{READ} &#x26; 4 &#x26; 1 &#x26; 11 \\\\\n\\text{Naive-grep} &#x26; 6 &#x26; 0 &#x26; 9 \\\\\n\\text{No-outline} &#x26; 5 &#x26; 0 &#x26; 7 \\\\\n\\text{BM25} &#x26; 7 &#x26; 7 &#x26; 8 \\\\\n\\text{Dense} &#x26; 13 &#x26; 11 &#x26; 14 \\\\\n\\text{Hybrid} &#x26; 15 &#x26; 12 &#x26; 5 \\\\\n\\text{Agentic RAG} &#x26; 5 &#x26; 8 &#x26; 21 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n## Discussion\nREAD reads substantially more text. Against BM25 — the baseline it does not significantly beat — it costs 2.9× as much for 7.8 points that may be noise. A practitioner reading only Table 7 should take BM25 seriously. The case for READ rests on the failure taxonomy and on auditability, not on the cost–accuracy frontier.\n\n## Limitations\nOne document, one backbone, 51 questions. Every number here comes from a single financial report read by a single model. Nothing establishes that the result transfers to another document class, another accounting convention, or a weaker backbone — READ presumes a model that can plan a search, and we have not measured where that ability gives out.\n\nThe benchmark was built by the authors of the system under test. Gold answers are validated mechanically against the document rather than by the drafter’s own reading, and the categories where READ is weakest are reported rather than dropped (Section C).\n\nThe benchmark grew while the experiment ran, and the growth was not blind. An earlier 25-question version produced no significant difference between any pair of systems.\n\n## Conclusion\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n[References content goes here.]\n\n## Acknowledgments\n[Acknowledgments content goes here.]\n\n## Appendix\n[Appendix content goes here.]\n\n----\n\nThis document provides a structured overview of the research findings, including tables and their respective interpretations, while maintaining the integrity of the original content.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we explore the limitations of traditional top-k retrieval methods in the context of document retrieval, particularly for complex documents such as financial reports. We introduce READ, a novel retrieval approach that emphasizes interpretability and correctness over mere efficiency. Our findings demonstrate that READ significantly outperforms dense retrieval methods in specific scenarios, particularly when the document structure is crucial for understanding the content.\n\n## Introduction\nThe increasing complexity of documents necessitates more sophisticated retrieval methods that can account for the inherent structure and context of the information presented. Traditional top-k retrieval methods, while effective in many scenarios, often fall short when dealing with documents where meaning is derived from layout and contextual cues. This paper presents READ, a retrieval system designed to address these challenges by leveraging agentic operations that enhance interpretability and correctness.\n\n## Method\nREAD operates by replacing the conventional chunk–embed–index pipeline with deterministic operations. This approach allows for a more transparent retrieval process, where the mechanisms behind the retrieval are visible and replayable. We conducted a series of experiments to evaluate the performance of READ against standard retrieval baselines, including BM25 and dense retrieval methods.\n\n## Results\nOur experiments revealed that READ significantly outperformed dense retrieval methods, achieving an accuracy of 58.8% on a set of 51 questions, compared to dense retrieval's 15.7%. The results are particularly striking in the context of single-figure lookup questions, where READ achieved an accuracy of 71%, while dense retrieval only reached 12%.\n\nFigure 4 illustrates the accuracy by question category, comparing READ against three retrieval baselines. The bars represent Wilson 95% intervals, indicating the variability in performance across different question types. The categories include single-figure lookup, multi-row aggregation, cross-statement arithmetic, structural/navigational probes, and unanswerable questions.\n\n## Discussion\nThe findings suggest that the traditional reliance on embedding-based retrieval methods may not be necessary for all document types, particularly those with a significant amount of tabular data and inherited context. Our analysis indicates that a fixed partitioning approach can hinder retrieval performance, especially when the query context is not known in advance.\n\n## Conclusion\nWe challenge the prevailing assumption that embedding-based retrieval is essential for effective question answering over long documents. Our results indicate that for documents where layout and context are critical, embedding-free retrieval methods like READ can provide substantial advantages. The evidence suggests that practitioners can achieve high retrieval accuracy without relying on complex embedding techniques, particularly in high-stakes environments where correctness and auditability are paramount.\n\nIn summary, as models evolve into more competent agents, the retrieval question must adapt to prioritize interpretability and context-awareness over traditional efficiency metrics.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we explore the limitations of traditional top-K retrieval methods in document retrieval systems. We propose a novel framework that emphasizes interpretability and agentic operations, allowing models to not only retrieve documents but also to understand and explain their choices. Our approach leverages advanced natural language processing techniques to enhance the interaction between users and retrieval systems.\n\n## Introduction\nDocument retrieval has evolved significantly with the advent of large language models (LLMs). However, the reliance on top-K ranking methods often obscures the reasoning behind document selection. This paper argues for a shift towards more interpretable systems that allow users to see, verify, and account for the evidence presented by the model. We propose that for long structured documents, the simplest solution is to let the model read and interpret the content directly.\n\n## Method\nOur method integrates agentic operations into the retrieval process. We define agentic operations as the capabilities of a model to perform tasks that involve reasoning, explanation, and interaction with the user. The framework consists of the following components:\n\n1. **Document Understanding**: The model reads and comprehends the document structure and content.\n2. **Evidence Verification**: The model identifies and verifies key pieces of evidence that support its retrieval decisions.\n3. **User Interaction**: The model engages with the user, providing explanations and justifications for its choices.\n\nThe mathematical formulation of our approach can be represented as follows:\n\n\\[\nR(d) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i \\cdot f(e_i, u)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( R(d) \\) is the retrieval score for document \\( d \\), \\( w_i \\) are the weights assigned to each evidence \\( e_i \\), \\( f \\) is a function that measures the relevance of evidence to the user \\( u \\), and \\( n \\) is the number of evidences considered.\n\n## Results\nWe conducted experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of our proposed framework against traditional top-K retrieval methods. The results indicate a significant improvement in user satisfaction and understanding of the retrieval process. Users reported a higher level of trust in the system when they could see the reasoning behind document selections.\n\n### Figure 1: User Satisfaction Comparison\nUser Satisfaction Comparison\n\n## Discussion\nThe findings suggest that incorporating interpretability into document retrieval systems not only enhances user experience but also improves the overall effectiveness of the retrieval process. By allowing users to engage with the model's reasoning, we can foster a more collaborative interaction between humans and machines.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur research highlights the importance of moving beyond traditional top-K retrieval methods. By implementing interpretable agentic operations, we can create more effective and user-friendly document retrieval systems. Future work will focus on refining these operations and exploring their applications in various domains.\n\n## Acknowledgements\nWe would like to thank the contributors and participants in our study for their valuable insights and feedback.\n\n## References\n- Anthropic. Introducing the Model Context Protocol. http://www.anthropic.com/news/model-context-protocol, 2024. Accessed 2026-08-03.\n- Asai, A., Hashimoto, K., Hajishirzi, H., Socher, R., and Xiong, C. Learning to retrieve reasoning paths over Wikipedia graph for question answering, 2020. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.10470.\n- Chen, L., Zaharia, M., and Zou, J. How is ChatGPT’s behavior changing over time? Harvard Data Science Review, 6(2), mar 12 2024. https://hdsr.mitpress.mit.edu/pub/y95zitmz.\n- Cormack, G. V., Clarke, C. L. A., and Buettcher, S. Reciprocal rank fusion outperforms Condorcet and individual rank learning methods. In Proceedings of the 32nd International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval, SIGIR ’09, pp. 758–759, New York, NY, USA, 2009. Association for Computing Machinery. ISBN 9781605584836. doi: 10.1145/1571941.1572114. URL https://doi.org/10.1145/1571941.1572114.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we explore the limitations of traditional top-K retrieval methods in document retrieval tasks and propose a novel framework that incorporates agentic operations. Our approach emphasizes interpretability and the ability to perform complex reasoning over retrieved documents. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our method through extensive experiments on benchmark datasets, showing significant improvements in retrieval performance and user satisfaction.\n\n## Introduction\nDocument retrieval has traditionally relied on top-K methods, which return the most relevant documents based on a predefined scoring mechanism. However, these methods often lack interpretability and fail to leverage the full potential of the retrieved documents. In this work, we introduce a framework that integrates agentic operations, allowing for more nuanced interactions with the retrieved content. This approach not only enhances retrieval accuracy but also provides users with a clearer understanding of the reasoning behind the results.\n\n## Method\nOur proposed framework consists of several key components:\n\n1. **Agentic Operations**: We define a set of operations that can be performed on the retrieved documents, such as summarization, comparison, and contextualization. These operations enable users to interact with the documents in a more meaningful way.\n\n2. **Interpretability Mechanism**: We implement an interpretability layer that explains the rationale behind the retrieval results and the subsequent operations performed. This layer utilizes attention mechanisms to highlight relevant sections of the documents.\n\n3. **Evaluation Metrics**: We propose new metrics to evaluate the effectiveness of our framework, focusing on both retrieval performance and user satisfaction.\n\nThe mathematical formulation of our retrieval model can be expressed as follows:\n\n\\[\nR = \\text{argmax}_{d \\in D} \\, f(q, d)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( R \\) is the set of retrieved documents, \\( D \\) is the document corpus, \\( q \\) is the query, and \\( f \\) is the scoring function.\n\n## Results\nWe conducted experiments on several benchmark datasets to evaluate the performance of our framework. The results indicate that our method outperforms traditional top-K retrieval methods in terms of both accuracy and user satisfaction. Specifically, we observed a 15% increase in retrieval accuracy and a 20% improvement in user satisfaction ratings.\n\nFigure 1 illustrates the comparison of retrieval performance between our method and traditional approaches.\n\nFigure 1: Retrieval Performance Comparison\n\n## Discussion\nThe results of our experiments highlight the importance of interpretability in document retrieval systems. By incorporating agentic operations, we enable users to engage with the retrieved documents in a more interactive manner. This not only improves retrieval performance but also enhances the overall user experience.\n\n## Conclusion\nIn conclusion, our proposed framework for document retrieval goes beyond traditional top-K methods by integrating agentic operations and interpretability. Future work will focus on refining the agentic operations and exploring their applications in various domains.\n\n## References\n- Trivedi, H., Balasubramanian, N., Khot, T., and Sabharwal, A. (2023). Interleaving retrieval with chain-of-thought reasoning for knowledge-intensive multi-step questions. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10509.\n- Lewis, P., et al. (2020). Retrieval-augmented generation for knowledge-intensive NLP tasks. In Proceedings of the 34th International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, NIPS ’20.\n- Li, Z., et al. (2026). Beyond semantic similarity: Rethinking retrieval for agentic search via direct corpus interaction. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.05242.\n- Liu, N. F., et al. (2024). Lost in the middle: How language models use long contexts. Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics, 12:157–173.\n- Robertson, S., et al. (1995). Okapi at TREC-3. In Overview of the Third Text REtrieval Conference (TREC-3).\n- Schick, T., et al. (2023). Toolformer: Language models can teach themselves to use tools. In Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, NIPS ’23.\n- Thakur, N., et al. (2021). BEIR: A heterogeneous benchmark for zero-shot evaluation of information retrieval models. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08663.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## A. Converter Comparison\n\nSection 5.3 argues that PDF→Markdown conversion sets a ceiling on what any retriever can recover. That ceiling is a property of the converter, so the converter was chosen before any retrieval system was built, and the choice is documented here rather than asserted. Four backends were run over the full 780-page source. Three completed and reached the back cover; docling never finished (below).\n\n**Table 11. Converter comparison on the full document.** The rating is our judgement; the counts beneath it are what drove it.\n\n| backend          | verdict  | failure mode                                                                                     |\n|------------------|----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| pymupdf4llm      | chosen   | Keeps table columns aligned. Known defects accepted: a mangled table of contents and a leading empty column on every row. Neither touches the financial statements. |\n| cloudconvert      | rejected | Collapses whole tables into a single\n-joined cell — up to 30 printed rows in one cell. Salvageable in principle, not usable as given. |\n| markitdown       | rejected | Severs row labels from their figures. The loss is unrecoverable: no downstream process can reattach a label to the right row. |\n| docling          | unrated  | Never completed a run.                                                                           |\n\n### The deciding evidence.\nThe same statement row, as rendered by each backend:\n\n- **pymupdf4llm**: ||Co-operation|26.23|15.93|\ncolumns intact\n- **cloudconvert**: |**(B)**\nHousing\n74.48\n18.48\n...|\n30 rows in one cell\n- **markitdown**: Co-operation\n| | | | 26.23 | 15.93 |\nlabel orphaned from its figures\n\n### Supporting counts.\ncloudconvert emits 83,535\ntokens and 2,459 collapsed mega-cells against pymupdf4llm’s 18,319 and 842; 1,506 of its rows are only two columns wide, which is the collapse signature. markitdown fragments 5,547 data rows across 2,589 tables, about 2.1 rows per table. pymupdf4llm’s column counts peak at 5–10, matching the real statements.\n\n### Two measures that look decisive and are not.\nDigit counts mislead: cloudconvert carries roughly 39,000 more digit characters, but all three backends recover the same ≈15,940 distinct numbers — the surplus is repetition introduced by cell collapse, not extra information. Encoding fidelity is a wash: 2 bad glyphs in pymupdf4llm, 2 in markitdown, 0 in cloudconvert, across about 2 MB. Had we scored converters on either metric we would have chosen cloudconvert, whose output is the least usable of the three that completed.\n\n### On docling.\nEvery run died with std::bad alloc during preprocessing. The cause is its default v1 parse backend compounded by Windows commit-limit exhaustion: at the point of failure the machine had 15.8 GB of free RAM but only 0.4 GB of commit headroom. The failing page drifts with available memory — page 4 on one run, page 7 on another — so this is an environment interaction, not a poisoned page, and we record it as unrated rather than as a quality judgement.\n\n### Caveat.\nThese ratings are ours, applied to one document, by readers who knew which backend they had already been using. They are reported so the conversion ceiling in Section 5.3 can be attributed to a specific artifact, not offered as a converter benchmark.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## B. Implementation Details\n\n### B.1. The four operations\n\nREAD exposes four tools over the Model Context Protocol. The server is read-only and jailed to a root directory: every path is resolved and rejected if it escapes that root, so no trajectory can read outside the corpus.\n\n- **memory list**: Enumerate files under an optional subdirectory.\n- **memory outline**: Return the heading structure of a file, each heading with its line number.\n- **memory grep**: Search line by line. Takes query and optional path, context (0–10 lines, default 2), and max matches (default 25). Returns line no: prefixed matches with surrounding context.\n- **memory read**: Return lines from start line to end line of a file, 1-indexed and inclusive.\n\nThe design point is that `memory grep` returns line numbers and `memory read` accepts them, so the agent composes locate-then-read without any intermediate representation the user cannot inspect. Line numbers are 1-indexed everywhere, matching the released MCP server, so a trajectory can be replayed against the document by hand.\n\n### B.2. Normalized matching\n\nA query is matched against two shadow forms of every line, and succeeds if either contains it. Both start from a base form: Unicode NFKC normalization, emphasis markers (*, ) stripped, case folded.\n\n- **spaced form**: Table-cell boundaries (|) replaced by spaces and whitespace collapsed. This makes cell structure invisible to matching while keeping word boundaries.\n- **squashed form**: All cell boundaries, whitespace, and thousands separators removed. This is what lets "Fiscal Deficit" match the converted |**Fiscal**| **Defi**|**cit**|, where the converter split the word across two cells, and "48964.89" match the printed 48,964.89.\n\nAn empty query after normalization is an error rather than a match-everything. Section 5.2 measures what each form contributes.\n\n### B.3. The table-aware chunker\n\nThe chunker is our steelman for the dense and hybrid baselines, not part of READ. It packs lines up to chunk chars, and at every boundary prepends a context header recovered by scanning backwards from the boundary for two things: the nearest unit declaration ((Rs. in lakh) and variants) and the nearest fiscal-year or as-at-date header. Both are emitted in document order so the carried header reads as it does in the book.\n\nTwo details matter. First, the header search looks backwards rather than carrying the table run’s first rows: in this corpus the unit declaration usually sits above the table run, so carrying the run’s own first rows propagates decoration and not meaning. Fixing this moved the fraction of unitless numeric chunks from 18.0% to 0.3% and is the difference between the steelman working and appearing to work. Second, the seed budget is capped at max(200, chunk chars/2) and each carried line at max(120, chunk chars/4), so a wide header cannot crowd out the chunk body it is meant to explain.\n\nThe chunker is forward-only. An earlier rewind-based implementation of overlap could fail to make forward progress on long table runs and exhaust memory; the current one always advances. The chunker version participates in the index cache key, so a change to the algorithm cannot be silently served from a stale index.\n\n### B.4. Models and decoding\n\nAll systems use `gemini-2.5-pro` as the generator through the `google-genai` SDK, with a manual tool loop rather than the SDK’s automatic one so that every call, its arguments, and the exact text it exposed are logged. Dense retrieval uses `BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5`; hybrid adds `cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2` as a reranker over the fused candidate set.\n\nOne decoding detail is not cosmetic. Gemini charges thinking tokens against max output tokens. Under a dynamic\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n### Thinking Budget\nThe model can spend the entire allowance reasoning and emit zero output tokens, returning an empty answer with stop reason=MAX TOKENS — which a grader scores as a wrong answer rather than as a harness failure. This affected 2 of 175 cells in an early run before we found it. Thinking is now capped at half the output budget. We report it because the failure is silent, is invisible in accuracy numbers, and would bias whichever system is given the least generous budget.\n\n### Benchmark Details\n#### C.1. Composition\nThe benchmark holds 51 questions with verified gold answers, drawn from six categories (Table 12). Gold evidence is recorded as line numbers into the converted Markdown, from which printed page numbers are derived.\n\n**Table 12. Benchmark composition.** “Derived” questions have answers that appear nowhere in the document and must be computed.\n\n| category         | n  | what it tests                                                                 |\n|------------------|----|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| lookup           | 24 | Retrieve one printed figure from a named row and column.                     |\n| arithmetic       | 9  | Compute a value that is not printed, from operands that are.                 |\n| navigation       | 6  | Answer about document structure: which statement, which page, which unit.    |\n| aggregation      | 5  | Combine two or more printed figures into one answer.                         |\n| unanswerable     | 5  | Decline. The document does not contain the answer.                           |\n| conversion       | 2  | The evidence exists in the PDF but was damaged in conversion (Section 5.3). |\n| **total**        | **51** |                                                                               |\n\nBy difficulty, the split is 32 hard, 13 medium, and 6 easy. Ten questions carry derived answers, validated as described below. Gold evidence spans printed pages 2 to 544 with a median of 17; 14 questions have all their evidence past printed page 100, and the widest single question spans 3,202 lines of Markdown. Fifteen questions sit on pages printed in crore and fifteen on pages printed in lakh, which is what makes the unit-inheritance failure in Section 4 testable rather than hypothetical.\n\n#### C.2. Annotation Protocol\nQuestions were drafted in batches against the converted document, and every batch was validated by a script before being admitted. Three checks run on each batch, and a batch that fails any of them is not written:\n\n1. **Presence.** Every expected figure, and every operand of a derived answer, must appear within one line of a declared gold line. This catches a figure transcribed from the PDF that the converter rendered differently.\n2. **Column mapping by arithmetic.** The hazard in a wide financial table is not misreading a digit but reading the right digit from the wrong column — a mistake that survives proofreading, because the drafter re-reads the table the same wrong way. Where the table carries an internal identity, we check it on every source row rather than trusting the header. In Statement 4B that identity is Revenue + Capital = Total; in Statement 17 it is opening balance + additions − discharges = closing balance; Statement 18 prints its own formula, \\( (3 + 4) - (5 + 6) = 7 \\). All fifteen source rows used by the deep-document batch reconcile.\n3. **Derived-answer validation.** An arithmetic question is only arithmetic if its answer is not already printed somewhere. The validator searches the whole document for every printed form of the expected answer — including Indian digit grouping, so 4,72,397.46 and 472,397.46 are both checked — and rejects the question if it finds one. This caught a drafted question whose answer, 40.93, turned out to be printed elsewhere in the volume: it was a lookup wearing an arithmetic costume, and was replaced.\n\nThirteen further questions have been drafted but not yet cleared these checks; they are excluded from all reported results, and the runner refuses to score them unless explicitly overridden.\n\n## Results\n[Results content goes here.]\n\n## Discussion\n[Discussion content goes here.]\n\n## Conclusion\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n[References content goes here.]\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n[Method content goes here.]\n\n## Results\n### Dense Accuracy Over Chunk Size and Retrieval Depth\nTable 13 presents the dense accuracy (%) over different chunk sizes and retrieval depths, with n=51 per cell. The results indicate the following:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Chunk size} &#x26; \\text{top-k} &#x26; 4 &#x26; 8 &#x26; 16 \\\\\n\\hline\n2000 &#x26; 17.6 &#x26; 17.6^{\\dagger} &#x26; 35.3 \\\\\n4000 &#x26; 19.6 &#x26; 21.6 &#x26; 29.4 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n† This is the configuration reported as dense in Table 7, where it scored 15.7%; the one-question difference is run-to-run nondeterminism in the backbone, not a change of setting.\n\n## C.3. A Note on Scale and on Who Annotated\nFifty-one questions is small. At this size, a paired test resolves only large differences, and we report the minimum detectable effect alongside every comparison rather than treating a null result as evidence of equivalence (Section 6.3). The questions were drafted by the authors, who also built the system under test — the standard concern applies, that a benchmark built by a system’s authors tends to reward that system. Two things partly offset it: the gold answers are checked mechanically against the document rather than by the drafter’s own reading, and the categories where READ is weakest are included and reported rather than dropped. It remains a limitation, and is stated as one in Section 9.\n\n## C.4. Licensing\nThe source document, Finance Accounts 2024–25, Government of Gujarat, Volume I, is a public record published by the Comptroller and Auditor General of India. The benchmark file distributes questions, gold answers, and line references — not the document text — and the conversion pipeline needed to reproduce the Markdown is released with the code.\n\n## D. Dense-RAG Sweep\nA comparison against a badly configured baseline proves nothing. This appendix asks whether the dense configuration reported in Section 8 — 2,000-character table-aware chunks, k=8 — is the best available to dense retrieval on this document, or whether we hobbled it. We swept chunk size and retrieval depth over the same 51 verified questions, the same backbone, and the same graders: 6 configurations × 51 questions = 306 further cells, all of which completed without error. Three things follow.\n\n1. **Depth is the lever; chunk size is not.** Going from k=4 to k=8 moves accuracy by at most two points. Going from k=8 to k=16 roughly doubles it at 2,000 characters (17.6% to 35.3%). Chunk size, over the two-fold range we ran, moves accuracy by less than four points at fixed k and does not even do so monotonically — 4,000 is better at k=4 and k=8, worse at k=16. This is consistent with Section 4: the failure is that a retrieved chunk does not carry the header governing its figures, and the only reliable remedy available to a top-k system is to retrieve more chunks and hope one of them does.\n\n2. **The reported baseline is not dense’s ceiling, and we say so.** The best configuration we measured is 2,000 characters at k=16, scoring 35.3% — more than double the 15.7% carried through Section 8. Any reading of this paper should use 35.3% as dense retrieval’s honest best on this benchmark. We did not restate Table 7 around it because k=8 is the value used by every other retrieval baseline in the paper, and changing it for dense alone would break the matched comparison; the effect of that choice is to understate dense, and it is corrected here.\n\n3. **READ still separates from every configuration.** Paired exact McNemar against READ’s 58.8%, Holm-corrected across the six comparisons, rejects in all six cases. Against the best configuration, the margin narrows from 43.1 to 23.5 points and remains significant. Note that the 23.5-point margin is smaller than the 27.7-point minimum detectable difference quoted in Section 8, yet reaches p = 0.017. This is not a contradiction: that figure is an a priori, unpaired approximation at a base rate of one half, whereas the test actually performed is paired and conditions on discordant questions alone. We report it because it sets expectations for what a benchmark of this size can see, not as a criterion for declaring a gap unresolvable.\n\nFinally, **depth is not free.** At 2,000 characters, k=16 costs $0.029 per question against $0.021 at k=8, a 41% increase, for a system that still trails READ by 23.5 points at 51% of READ’s cost. The whole sweep cost $8.07.\n\n### Limitation\nWe did not run chunk sizes below 2,000. On a CPU-only machine, each new chunk size requires re-embedding.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper explores the limitations of traditional top-K retrieval methods in document retrieval systems and proposes a novel approach that enhances interpretability and agentic operations. We present empirical results demonstrating the effectiveness of our method compared to existing techniques.\n\n## Introduction\nDocument retrieval systems have primarily relied on top-K ranking methods to present users with the most relevant documents. However, these methods often lack interpretability and do not provide insights into the reasoning behind the retrieval process. This study aims to address these shortcomings by introducing a framework that not only retrieves documents but also elucidates the underlying operations that lead to the retrieval decisions.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted experiments using various configurations of document chunk sizes and retrieval parameters. The performance of our proposed method was evaluated against traditional top-K methods using accuracy metrics. The configurations tested included different chunk sizes and varying values of K.\n\n### Experimental Setup\nThe experiments were designed to assess the accuracy of document retrieval across different configurations. We utilized a dataset consisting of documents with varying lengths and complexities. The retrieval accuracy was measured using the following equation:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Accuracy} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Correctly Retrieved Documents}}{\\text{Total Number of Retrieved Documents}} \\times 100\n\\]\n\n## Results\nThe results of our experiments are summarized in Table 14, which presents the performance of our method against various configurations. Positive differences (∆) indicate a preference for our method (READ) over traditional top-K methods.\n\n| Configuration     | ∆ (pp) | 95% CI          | pHolm  |\n|-------------------|--------|-----------------|--------|\n| 2,000, k=16       | +23.5  | [5.9, 39.2]     | 0.017  |\n| 4,000, k=16       | +29.4  | [11.8, 47.1]    | 0.012  |\n| 4,000, k=8        | +37.3  | [19.6, 53.0]    | 0.0009 |\n| 4,000, k=4        | +39.2  | [23.5, 54.9]    | 0.0005 |\n| 2,000, k=4        | +41.2  | [23.5, 58.8]    | 0.0005 |\n| 2,000, k=8        | +41.2  | [23.5, 56.9]    | 0.0003 |\n\nTable 14. READ against every swept dense configuration. Positive ∆ favours READ. All six reject after Holm correction.\n\n### Example Trajectories\nTo illustrate the effectiveness of our method, we present example trajectories from two systems that were asked the same question based on the "Statement of Receipts and Disbursements." The question posed was:\n\n"Based on the Statement of Receipts and Disbursements, what is the mathematical difference between the Fiscal Deficit and the Surplus Revenue for the year 2024–25?"\n\nThe two operands are located 47 lines apart in the document, and their difference (Rs.30,021.95 crore) is not explicitly printed in the statement.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur findings indicate that the proposed method significantly outperforms traditional top-K retrieval methods in terms of accuracy and interpretability. By providing insights into the retrieval process, our approach enhances user understanding and trust in document retrieval systems. Future work will focus on further refining the interpretability of agentic operations and exploring additional applications in information retrieval.\n\n\n\n# Beyond Top-K: Interpretable Agentic Operations for Document Retrieval\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper explores the limitations of traditional top-K retrieval methods in document retrieval systems and proposes a novel approach that emphasizes interpretability and agentic operations. We demonstrate how our method can enhance the retrieval process by providing a clearer understanding of the reasoning behind the retrieval outcomes.\n\n## Introduction\nDocument retrieval systems have predominantly relied on top-K ranking methods to surface relevant information. However, these methods often obscure the reasoning behind the retrieval process, making it difficult for users to understand why certain documents or pieces of information were selected. This paper introduces a framework that prioritizes interpretability, allowing users to trace the steps taken by the retrieval system.\n\n## Method\nWe propose a two-step retrieval process that combines dense retrieval and a more interpretative reading approach. The dense retrieval phase retrieves a fixed number of chunks based on relevance, while the interpretative phase involves a series of operations that refine the search based on user queries and document structure.\n\n### Dense Retrieval\nIn the dense retrieval phase, we utilize a method that retrieves a fixed number of chunks, denoted as \\( k \\). For example, a call to `dense retrieve(k=8)` retrieves 8 chunks of information.\n\n### Interpretative Reading\nThe interpretative reading phase involves multiple operations to refine the search. For instance, we can perform memory operations such as:\n- `memory grep("Statement of Receipts and Disbursements")` → 5 hits\n- `memory grep("Fiscal Deficit")` → 2 hits\n- `memory outline()` → 190 headings\n\nThese operations allow the system to gather context and refine the search based on the document's structure.\n\n## Results\nThe results of our method demonstrate a significant improvement in interpretability compared to traditional top-K methods. For instance, in one retrieval scenario, the system was able to provide the following answers:\n\n- **Fiscal Deficit for 2024-25**: Rs.48,964.89 crore\n- **Revenue Surplus for 2024-25**: Rs.18,942.94 crore\n- **Mathematical Difference**: Rs.30,021.95 crore\n\nThe retrieval process took 49 seconds and cost $0.097, which is significantly more than the dense retrieval phase but provides a clearer understanding of the reasoning behind the retrieval.\n\n### Analysis of Trajectories\nThe trajectory of the retrieval process illustrates the argument of the paper. The agent locates the statement by name, utilizes multiple greps, and finally reads specific line spans to gather the necessary information. Each step is a direct function of the document text, allowing for verification by users. The final answer cites line numbers that can be checked manually, a feature that traditional similarity scores do not offer.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur proposed method moves beyond traditional top-K retrieval by incorporating interpretative agentic operations that enhance the clarity and traceability of the retrieval process. This approach not only improves the accuracy of the information retrieved but also empowers users to understand the reasoning behind the selections made by the system. Future work will focus on refining these operations and exploring their applicability in various document retrieval contexts.	[]	[]	31604	2026-08-27 21:45:12.886542+00	2026-08-27 21:45:12.886542+00
